     4	
     5	Brom had loaded her by the well, in the grey, with the steading's woman holding the lantern. Two boxes hung from the pack-saddle on their own ropes, one to a side, and the third rode between them on a folded blanket with the empty pannier strapped over it. He had gone round the animal three times, pulling at each knot. Then he had stood back and looked at the load as he would have looked at a wheel that he had built himself.
     6	
     7	"She'll want to go on the low side of you," said Lira.
     8	
     9	"On the low side of nothing. I go on the low side of her." He clicked his tongue as he had been shown, and the small brown ears came round. "I was told it once. I'd be ashamed to be told it twice."
    10	
    11	It was the hardest cold that they had yet walked in. The ruts of the valley road had frozen as the last grain cart left them, with every hoof-mark standing up like a thing cut in iron, and the pale grass on either hand was white to the knee. Their breath went ahead of them. On the gate-walk a lean man stood with his long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every morning that they had spent behind that fence; and when Lira looked up, he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had nothing in her right hand, and she lifted two of her own.
    12	
    13	They went down the stretch at the one time of day when nothing had ever come up it. Cael walked last. He had the white stone on his left hand at a hundred and ten yards, and he gave it the look that he gave it every time. Fifty-seven evenings had gone by since anything stood there.
    14	
    15	Lira was in front of him, and he watched how she went. The ankle was taped, and under a healer's order still, so that she set the great boot down whole at every stride, heel and toe together, and rolled nowhere. She had done it for fifteen days on turf. On frozen ruts it was a different trade. Twice in the first mile the boot skated, and twice her right arm went out and her left stayed where a knot held it; she had been told that she was to fall on her face sooner than put that hand out, and he saw that she had taught the hand to believe it.
    16	
    17	"I can hear you counting," she said, without turning her head.
    18	
    19	"I'm not counting."
    20	
    21	"You're doing the other thing, then. The one where you'd catch me." She put the boot down. "Don't. If I go over, I go over. She'd sooner mend my nose."
    22	
    23	They came to the ford a little before noon.
    24	
    25	The row of white stones stood whole along the lip, as the hold had set it, and the water went over the sill no more than shin-deep, with a rim of ice along both beaches. Seln crossed first, and then Karis, who lifted her coat. Brom led the mule down to the edge on her off side, where he had walked all morning, and she set her forefeet on the wet rock and would not move them.
    26	
    27	He clicked. He said a few reasonable things to her in a low voice. She regarded the far bank with her kind eye and stood like a milestone.
    28	
    29	"Near hand," said Lira. "You're on her off side, and the water's moving. She'll not cross with anybody on her near hand."
    30	
    31	"I'm not on her near hand."
    32	
    33	"Seln is. He's standing on the far beach, straight in front of her left eye."
    34	
    35	Seln, who had heard, walked ten paces up the far beach without a word. The mule went into the river as if nothing had ever been the matter. Brom, who had been leaning on the rope, went in with her a good deal faster than he had meant to, and came out on the other side wet to the thigh.
    36	
    37	"I'd like it entered," he said, "that I did everything I was told."
    38	
    39	"You did," said Karis. "She was told something else. You will have to learn which of you ranks."
    40	
    41	They made their camp that night on a carters' halting-ground a little above the wash, with the mule hobbled at the windward end and the three boxes under her blanket. Seln lit a small fire with his flint. They had kept that custom since the fifty-ninth day, on the strength of a finding, and it was a comfort still.
    42	
    43	Cael had the first watch. He sat with his back against a chock-stone and Hesk's book shut on his knee, and for some time he left it shut.
    44	
    45	He owed a healer two readings of himself with the tenth fragment, on two different days, with a night between; and after those he owed her the whole of it at once. She had set it at a well that morning, with a pannier on the coping. Nine days had gone by since the first reading, in a loft, and he had let every one of them pass. She had seen why from a stride and a half. He had found a place in himself that did not move, and he had been afraid of it, and he had liked it better as a line on a leaf.
    46	
    47	He could do the second tonight. Four people were asleep or near it, and the fire was low. He had his fingers on his own wrist before he saw what he was about.
    48	
    49	It was the same thing that she had stopped at the well. He wished to have three readings done in three nights, so that he could set them down at her first bench and stand square. She had told him to read it frightened, and to read it when he was not. She had said nothing about reading it in a hurry; that was a third temper, and it was the one that he had been in when he lay down on her table.
    50	
    51	He took his fingers off his wrist. The listening went on at the edge of his thought, quick and slow, and he let it go on, as a miller lets a mill; he would read himself on a night that was only a night, and until then the debt could stand where she had put it. It was the first that he had ever left standing without picking at it. He wrote one line, and gave the watch to Brom at the turn.
    52	
    53	*Hundred and eleventh day. The halting-ground above the wash. One reading done, two owed, and nothing tried tonight; I was in a hurry, and she never asked for that. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
