   203	He turned to the stair, and Lira's fingers had closed on his sleeve. He had a good deal that he wished to tell her, about a road that morning and an arm against a wall; and he had got as far as drawing breath for it when she shook her head, once, against the grey.
   204	
   205	"Later."
   206	
   207	He gave it back to her exactly as she had given it.
   208	
   209	"Later."
   210	
   211	---
   212	
   213	He went up thirty steps on his own feet, like anybody.
   214	
   215	At the fourth from the top he stopped. The square was over his head, and it was the colour of slate; he could see one star in it. Below him the stair went down into black, and he knew that a girl with one arm was looking up it at his back. He knew that Seln had turned round in the doorway and was facing the room, which was his way of saying that he had seen the thing before and did not need to see it again. Three more people were beyond the corner of that door, and could have seen nothing if they had tried.
   216	
   217	Above him there was the lip of the floor, and a great deal of open sky. He was a yard below the level of the stone, and no person on that rim could have seen the top of his head.
   218	
   219	It was dusk. Shadow had been lifting in him for an hour, as it lifted at every dusk, like a tide coming up a beach. He had told it no on the fifth evening past the Line, and at a ford with the light going, and on a gate-walk; he had refused it on something over a hundred evenings, and let it have him once, for ten strides.
   220	
   221	He did not tell it no.
   222	
   223	The sound of his own breath stopped. That was how it came: the world went on exactly as before, and he went out of it. He lifted his boot to the third step and set it down, and no stone answered, and he could not hear his coat. Far below him he heard a small sharp breath that was Lira's, since she had been looking at a boy on a stair, and now she was looking at a stair.
   224	
   225	He went up over the lip and stood on the floor.
   226	
   227	He had made up his mind about the next thing in the dark. There were ten bolts lying on that grey somewhere, where they had skated and stopped, and one kick would send any of them ringing; so he gave the read the ground, which was the oldest and quietest thing that he owned, and it went out under his soles for its pace and a half, as it always did. It told him what was there, and did not tell him what anything meant. But a spent bolt has a weight, and he felt the first of them lying a yard ahead and a little to the left, like a pencil on a table, before his boot could find it.
