     1	# Chapter 58 — Thirty-Four
     2	
     3	They came to the fork two hours after sunrise on the hundred and twenty-second day, and the healer stopped the grey mule in the middle of it.
     4	
     5	It was a plain enough place. The road divided at a heap of chock-stones that carters had left, and the right hand went on south and east under the foot of a long bare ridge. The left climbed away toward a notch between two hills, with the frost lying blue in it, and beyond the notch there was nothing to be seen but sky.
     6	
     7	"That's mine," said Oryn.
     8	
     9	She did not make a matter of it. She went to Brom first, as she went to the worst on any bench, and looked at four yards of webbing from her stride and a half, and then came the rest of the way and put two fingers under the lowest turn.
    10	
    11	"It's sitting where she laid it. You'll not undo that knot, and you'll not have anybody undo it who hasn't forty cold terms of knots behind her. When you're down, you go to that woman before you go to your supper."
    12	
    13	"Before my supper," said Brom. "I've got it."
    14	
    15	"See that you have."
    16	
    17	She turned to Lira, and held out her two hands, and Lira gave her the sling without a word. The wool came down. The healer's fingers went round the forearm, overlapping, and she took the short reading, to ten.
    18	
    19	"All eight. They've taken no harm from a stair or a wall, which is more than you deserved of them." She drew the wool up and tied her own knot, high, with the hand at the collar-bone. "It holds nothing. I said first snow, near enough, and I'll say when; you'll not say it for me because a flake's come down."
    20	
    21	"I'll not say it for you."
    22	
    23	Lira had the bow off her back. She held it out by the stock, with the one bolt lying in its groove.
    24	
    25	"This is yours."
    26	
    27	"It rode on my mule for thirty rounds in a cover, and I looked at it at noon one day, at a stone, and left it where it was. It's done more across your knee than it ever did for me." Oryn did not put out a hand. "There's one in it. See that it stays in till it's wanted."
    28	
    29	She looked along the five of them then, once. Her sum ran to the oxen ford on the hundred and twenty-fifth, three weeks after it had looked for her; then she said the thing that she had stopped the mule to say.
    30	
    31	"I'm three weeks late there, and three weeks is three weeks, and there's an old woman at Thornwater whose knee I said I'd see, and I said it after the thaw."
    32	
    33	"I've been a stop on my own route for nine days. I'm going back to being the route."
    34	
    35	Nobody answered her. She had told them at a mill what she would do on the day that the round could not spare her, and what they were to say to it. Cael had written down, where he wrote such things, that he would not ask her to stay.
    36	
    37	She clicked her tongue at the jenny, and took the left-hand road.
    38	
    39	He went with her. He had not meant to, and he did not know that he was going to until his boots were on the frozen ruts beside hers, at her stride and a half, with the grey mule's breath on the back of his neck. She gave him one glance and let it be.
    40	
    41	He counted, since he could not walk beside anybody without counting. He said nothing through the whole of it. There were a good many things that he might have asked on that piece of road: whether a child's chest had changed, and how a round was walked in snow, and when he would see her. There was the other, which was whether she would ever tell him what she had refused to tell him on her table. He asked none of them. Each was hers to give, and she had a road to walk.
    42	
    43	At fifty paces she stopped.
    44	
    45	"That's far enough. You've four people standing in a road."
    46	
    47	"I know."
    48	
    49	She shifted the rope on her shoulder. She was looking up at the notch, and her face was as tired and as level as he had ever seen it; and whatever had given under her at a marsh's edge, three days before, had been put back where she kept it.
    50	
    51	"Find me at Thornwater," she said.
    52	
    53	She went on four strides, and spoke again without turning her head.
    54	
    55	"Bring the map," said Oryn, to the road in front of her.
    56	
    57	He stood until the notch had her. She did not look back, and he had not thought that she would. Then he turned round.
    58	
    59	There were four of them behind him, and one mule. They stood at the heap of stones as she had left them: a small man with a case on his chest, and a small woman with a hide under her arm, and a very big one bound up like a parcel, and a girl with a bow on her back and one sleeve empty. It was the crew that had come down off a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second day past the Line, and it looked a good deal the worse for wear.
    60	
    61	He took out Hesk's book and wrote it standing, with the pencil cold in his fingers.
    62	
    63	*Hundred and twenty-second day, the fork. Oryn north. On her terms. I didn't ask. Five on the bearing.*
    64	
    65	---
    66	
    67	The road bent away south before noon, and the brass did not.
    68	
    69	Karis had it on the hide at the bend, with her back to the wind. The bright edge lay through her three dots and ran off the skin; and when she lifted her eyes from it, they went along the back of the ridge, which was long, and bare, and going exactly as the brass went.
    70	
    71	"Up," she said. "It is a strip a mile wide, as I told you. That ridge is in the middle of the strip, and I can see both sides of it from the top."
    72	
    73	They went up, with Brom on the low side of the mule and three boxes swaying. It was a great whale's back of pale grass, a mile broad on its crown, with the country falling off on either hand. In every fold of its flanks a line of white lay, where the frost had not lifted for a week and would not now lift before the thaw. From above they looked like chalk strokes ruled on a slate.
    74	
    75	She did not pretend that the line was better than it was. At each halt she had Seln stand a furlong ahead of her, and looked back along the turf to the last place where they had rested; then she moved him a few paces to one hand or the other, with her arm, until the two marks and her own boots lay in a row. It was how a person carries a ruler across country when she has none long enough, and she said that it was good to the width of the strip and no better.
    76	
    77	Seln went ahead between halts, as he had gone ahead on every road since a shelf. He came back to them once in each hour, by whatever clock he carried, and gave his report in a word.
    78	
    79	"Nothing."
    80	
    81	It was the word that he gave at the end of every watch, and Cael had heard it some hundreds of times. He heard it seven times more before dark. Each time Karis nodded, and wrote the hour, and went on.
    82	
    83	There were five of them, and he had been right about the sound. It was thinner. Nobody walked last now with her eyes on Brom's right shoulder; he found himself doing it, and was told by Brom, without heat, that one healer had been enough and that this one had no hands.
    84	
    85	Lira walked beside him for a mile in the afternoon, on the frozen turf, with the bow knocking against her back.
    86	
    87	"You went fifty paces with her," she said. "I counted them. What did you ask?"
    88	
    89	"Nothing."
    90	
    91	"Good." She put the great boot down whole, and the next. "She'd have answered you, and then she'd have had to carry having answered for four days through a gap. I thought that you'd know that. I wasn't sure of it."
    92	
    93	They slept on the ridge in a hollow, without wood, in every blanket that they had. Brom laid six bowls on the turf. He looked at the row, and took one back; and it passed without remark, since he had done exactly that on a hundred nights.
    94	
    95	It was that night that the ribs began to talk.
    96	
    97	She had told him that they would. The cold got into the three old breaks some time after the stars came out, and stayed there, like a thumb pressed on a bruise. He could breathe, and he could walk, and he could not find a way to lie. He counted it: this was the fourth night of her ten.
    98	
    99	The second day on the ridge was the clearest that he had seen past the Line.
   100	
   101	By noon he could see for thirty miles. There was no road in any of it, and no smoke, and not a fence or a roof; there were hills going away blue behind hills, with the white lying in their folds, and a sky over them so large that he could not look at the whole of it at once. Four people were strung out along the turf ahead of him, small and dark. A man who knew every road out of a town was walking toward a place where there was none. A girl who had been ranked every week of her life was a mile from the nearest thing that could have measured her, with a bow on her back that she could shoot and could not bend.
   102	
   103	Nothing in all that country had a seal on it, and nothing in it had ever heard his name.
   104	
   105	He had Hesk's book out, to enter the hour, as Karis did. He wrote something else.
   106	
   107	*I love this country.*
   108	
   109	He looked at it. He had never written that of anywhere. He had been in other people's country since he was fourteen, and had learned every yard of it as a prisoner learns a wall.
   110	
   111	It was true, and he let it stand; and the other thing was in the same coat with it, and had been all morning. They were walking along a ruler. Three floors lay in a row, as stones lie along a path; and when he looked ahead down the ridge, to where the strip went on into the blue, he was more afraid of what was under the brass than he had been of a thing in a marsh.
   112	
   113	He put the book away, with both of them in it, and went on.
   114	
   115	---
   116	
   117	Seln was standing on the crest when they came up to him at dusk, and he did not say the word that he had said seven times the day before and nine times since morning.
   118	
   119	The ridge ended there. It broke off in a long brow, and under the brow a valley began: a great shallow head of pale ground, scooped out of the hills as a bowl is scooped, and open toward the south. The light was going. What there was of it lay level across the grass and showed him the size of the place, which he did not at once believe. Seln gave it one word, as he gave everything.
   120	
   121	"Larger."
   122	
   123	The wind had dropped with the sun. There was no bird over that ground, and no beast on it, and nothing that moved in all the width of it but the last of the light going up the far slope; Cael had stood above two such places, and neither had been a third the size of this.
   124	
   125	There was a thing standing in the middle of it.
   126	
   127	Cael could not have said what. It was grey, and upright, and it was taller than a man; how much taller he had no means of telling, with nothing beside it to measure by and the better part of half a mile of dusk between. It was not a ring of stones. It was not a floor with a hole in it, or four walls. It had a top that was not level. That was all that the light would give him, and when he tried to hold more of it, the dusk kept the rest.
   128	
   129	"I can't make it," said Lira. She had her right hand over her eyes. "It's not one thing, I think. Or it is, and there's sky in it. I'd be guessing."
   130	
   131	"Then do not," said Karis.
   132	
   133	She was not looking at the middle. She was looking at the ground between, where two faint lines went round the valley's head, high up, a long way above anything else.
   134	
   135	"Those are ruts," she said. "Old ones, with turf in them. Somebody drove round this, once, as they drive round the others. But they kept a great deal farther off."
   136	
   137	"And that?" said Brom.
   138	
   139	Below the ruts, a furlong down the slope, there was something else going round. It was a line of small dark uprights, set close, running away on both hands with the curve of the ground until the dusk had it. From the crest it looked like a row of stitches.
   140	
   141	They left the mule on the brow, hobbled, with the boxes. Karis took the long cord over her shoulder, and her lamp, unlit.
   142	
   143	At the old ruts Lira stopped, and looked down the slope, and then at Cael.
   144	
   145	"I'll go to it. I'll not go over it. I want that heard, since she's not here to say it."
   146	
   147	"It's heard."
   148	
   149	She went down from the ruts by herself, on the whole of her foot, counting under her breath, and he counted with her. The line had lain forty paces inside the ruts at the Stair, and the ruts at the Fallow had kept forty yards. She passed forty with the iron still a long way below her. She passed a hundred.
   150	
   151	At a hundred and eighty-nine, or within a pace or two of it, she stopped one pace above the dark uprights.
   152	
   153	"The iron marks it," she said. "I'm not going over."
   154	
   155	"A hundred and ninety," said Karis, at the ruts. "Not forty."
   156	
   157	"Whoever drove a cart round this one kept a furlong off." Lira had not moved from where she stood. "The iron tells us where to stop. Without it you'd be well down the hill before you thought to look."
   158	
   159	Nobody crossed. Cael came down to her, and the others behind him; and they stood in a row along the outside of that fence, in the last of the light, as people stand at the rail of a bridge.
   160	
   161	He could have gone over. He knew only that he had crossed three such lines before and that the fragments had answered there; nobody had tested this line, and nobody would. He kept his boots beside theirs. It was a crew's line or it was nobody's, and he had learned on a white floor what it cost to hold a different ruling from the people whom he was ruling for.
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	It was iron, of the registry's pattern.
   166	
   167	He had seen four such stakes. There had been one at a grey stone on the Fallow, and two at the Stair with a road between, and one at the track's end beside a marsh. Each had stood alone at an edge where a cord had been laid.
   168	
   169	These did not stand alone. Anchor had known it before his eyes did; it had asked at two bars with an old road between them, ten days ago, and here it asked at every pace, in its thin held note, as far as he could hear. There was a bar at his right knee, a hand's length clear of the turf, and another a pace beyond it, and another a pace beyond that. They went on so in both directions, one to a pace, round the head of that valley, for as far as he could see iron against the grass.
   170	
   171	Seln had knelt at the nearest, and he did not touch it.
   172	
   173	"Thirty-four," he said. "And beneath the number, the stroke and the bar."
   174	
   175	He moved to the next on his knees, and the next.
   176	
   177	"Thirty-four. Thirty-four. Every one."
   178	
   179	Lira had gone along the fence to the left, counting under her breath, and Brom to the right. They came back together.
   180	
   181	"Forty," said Lira, "and it doesn't stop. I could see a dozen more."
   182	
   183	"Forty on mine," said Brom. "The same."
   184	
   185	Karis had her lamp lit by then. She had set a point on its wick with her own hand, outside that line, and she had the long cord laid along the feet of the bars with Cael at its far end. There was no call for anybody's foot. The line was marked at every pace in iron; no foot was needed to find it.
   186	
   187	She laid it twenty times. At each cord's end she set her short cord from the last bar to the line that the long one would next take, as she had on three other rounds. Cael, holding the lamp for her, saw that the short measure did not come to the knot that it had always come to. It came to half.
   188	
   189	She did it twenty times because she did not believe it, and he watched her cease to disbelieve it at about the twelfth.
   190	
   191	They had gone twenty cords along that fence when she stood up, with the short cord in her fist. It was full dark.
   192	
   193	"Half the bend is twice the round. Four hundred. Near enough. Twice the radius. The same shape, at twice the size, round a thing I can't see."
   194	
   195	Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
   196	
   197	Seln had not got up from his knees. He was looking along the bars, away into the dark, as he looked through a paper.
   198	
   199	"That's not a survey. That's a cordon. They've been here more than once."
   200	
   201	Nobody asked him who. He had said the name of it once, at a stake, and it had not grown any smaller since.
   202	
   203	"There was a sheet with two," he said, after a breath. "I told you that I could not place it. A marsh three days behind us, and this: I can place it now."
   204	
   205	Lira was standing a pace above the iron, where she had stopped, with her right hand shut on the strap of the bow.
   206	
   207	"Twice the size. Is it twice the *anything else*?"
   208	
   209	It was the question, and all five of them knew it. A line half this size had put out five sigils at a stride and given them back at a stride, the same, with nothing lost. Would one of twice the size give them back? There was no cord that could tell her that. Somebody would have to walk over that iron with a Path in her and come out again; and the one person who could have stood at the fence with her sleeves up was two days gone through a gap.
   210	
   211	"I'm not putting a foot over that," said Brom. "I've one arm, and I've been told what it's good for, and there's nobody on this side of it to tell me again."
   212	
   213	"I have not a single sheet of this place," said Karis. "I should like that entered beside whatever is ruled. I want it as I wanted the Stair. I am not going to say that I would go alone, since I have learned what that sentence costs other people."
   214	
   215	"There are forty bars to the left of me that I have counted, and a great many more that I have not," said Seln. "I should want to walk every one of them by daylight before I advised anybody to stand inside them. I do not think that we have the bread."
   216	
   217	Cael had waited for the four. There was a silence after the fourth, of about the length that a hoarse voice would have taken to say its piece; and he let it run to its end.
   218	
   219	"Not without her. Not without knowing what a circle twice the size does to the four of you. And not without the map finished."
   220	
   221	"It's finished," Karis said. "It's a line."
   222	
   223	"Then it's a line that points somewhere. We go home and find out where."
   224	
   225	She had the hide out under the lamp before he had done speaking. She made a fourth ring on it, at the very edge of the skin, where there was no ink of the carter's at all, and wrote no word beside it; and she laid the brass through her three dots. It ran through the middle of the new ring, and off the hide, and on across the lid of the box.
   226	
   227	They climbed back to the crest and slept there, in the wind, with the valley black below them and nothing lit in it. He wrote by her lamp, when the others were down.
   228	
   229	*Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're oriented.*

