    73	"He says that it is true to a hair," said Karis, at his elbow. She was looking at the brass as she had once looked at a founding stone. "I should like to see it against mine."
    74	
    75	"Tonight. I've two letters to write, and that boat's not going to wait for me."
    76	
    77	He wrote them at the end of Pike's plank, on two leaves torn out of Hesk's own book, with the horn ink-pot and a borrowed pen. He had owed both for a long while, and he had been waiting for a thing to say; and he had not expected, when it came, that it would go into so few words.
    78	
    79	*Nineteen, past the Line, and there's a roof. It's an inn. The shape you said to mind is a line, and I've got the tool for it now. — C.*
    80	
    81	The second took him longer, since it was to a woman who had kept a ledger with his name in it, in a yard, and had never once shown it to a seat.
    82	
    83	*Vell. There's a second ledger on the continent with my name in it, kept by a one-armed man in a town on no map, and this week he stopped numbering me. He said his book was wrong about us by being too small. You said once that somebody should have a record that doesn't answer to the Compact. There are two now. The session still stands. — C.*
    84	
    85	There was a third that he owed, to a friend on a bluff who lost to him every time he ran it. He had no line for that one yet. It would go by the first boat of the thaw, and he wrote in his own book that it was to.
    86	
    87	He folded the two, and put no place on either, and gave them to the ferryman's boy with a copper apiece. The clerk's packet went into the same bag. Seln watched it go down the bank, with his hands behind him.
    88	
    89	"It will say that a crew kept what it was sent for," he said. "It cannot say why, since he does not know. I find that I can bear that."
    90	
    91	The scow went over once more, and came back empty. Then the ferryman and his boy took her lines in. They drew the hauling rope off the drum, fathom by fathom, stiff with ice, and coiled it in the shed; and the gelding was led away up the bank to the smith's stable with his blanket on.
    92	
    93	"That's her shut," said Brom.
    94	
    95	The snow was coming down on the landing, and on the bare cable, and on the far bank, where there was no longer anybody at all.
    96	
    97	---
    98	
    99	It had been the hundred and twenty-second morning, eight days before, when Oryn left five people standing at a heap of chock-stones and took the left-hand road.
   100	
   101	She did not turn her head at the notch. She had left a good many gates in thirty rounds, and had found out in the first of them that a healer who looks back has only doubled what she is carrying.
   102	
   103	The jenny went as she had always gone, which was badly. She led as a gate swings. For the whole of the first morning Oryn's right hand kept going out to a small brown cheek that was two days behind her on another road; it met a grey ear like an oar, and came back. She let it. She had told a girl in a yard that a beast should not be named, and had learned at a wicket how little that helped.
   104	
   105	She did her sum as she climbed, since she did it every morning, and it came out as she had given it to five people at a heap of stones. There was a bench at the mill that she had left a day short, and two gates beyond it that had not seen her at all. There was the ford with the oxen. Beyond the ford was the gate that had given her the coat, and beyond that the child.
   106	
   107	She had sent word for the child. No word had come back to the mill before she left it, which meant that the breathing had not changed, or that the carter had been slow. She could not tell which. She put it with the things that could only be carried, where it did not get into her hands.
   108	
   109	Then she let herself go over the other, since she had four days and nobody to see her do it.
   110	
   111	She began with what her hands had, as she had been taught. They were full again. She had mended nobody for three days, and she could feel the purse of her reserve sitting heavy and whole, as it had not sat since a mill. They had left a shoulder in the keeping of a woman who knew more about holding than she did, and she had a drover's turn to show for it, which she could lay now herself.
   112	
   113	It was the other way about that she had no practice in. He had been in *her*.
   114	
   115	She had felt him go along. She would have known that step anywhere now, quick and then dragging, like a person walking a road with a stone in his boot; and while it went, his voice had told her what the same step had found in himself.
   116	
   117	She had said the only true thing that she had. In eleven years she had put her hands into more bodies than she could number, the hurt and the whole and the dying, and every one of them had been all current, fast or slow, standing behind a break or running free. She had never met a still thing in a living person. The dying went still all at once, and everywhere. This was neither of those.
   118	
   119	She did not ask herself what it was. That belonged to a small pale woman with three boxes, and Oryn had no box. She had given him a healer's sentence about it, and she would have said the same to a carter with a scab.
   120	
   121	There was a thing that she had said at the marsh's edge, and she looked at that last.
   122	
   123	*So are you.* It had come out of her crooked, and she had heard it come. She went over it now, strictly, with her knuckles ready. He was on the route. That was the whole of what the words said, and they were true of a miller's man and an old woman on a hurdle. She had gone into a dish of water after a shoulder, since she was nearest. He had gone into a hole after her. Neither of those had anything to say to a morning on a plank at Thornwater, when a boy lay still and let her spend.
   124	
   125	They were two leaves. She would not have the one laid over the other, by him or by herself. She had not forgiven him. The question stood where it had stood, and it had got company.
   126	
   127	---
   128	
   129	The gap took the four days that a carter's skin had given it.
   130	
   131	It was a high bare way between two hills, with nothing on it but frost and a few sheep that belonged to nobody, and she slept each night with her back against the jenny's belly and her feet in her pannier. On the fourth afternoon the ground began to fall. There was a valley under her with a pale road in it, going east and west, and she knew that road; she had gone round a ring of stones by it, thirty times, with a mule.
   132	
   133	She came down to Thornwater's eastern gate at dusk on the hundred and twenty-fifth day. The bar was drawn for her before she had called.
   134	
   135	"It's yours when you're here," said the steading's woman. She had a lantern, and she looked the healer over with it as she would have looked over a cart that was late home. "You're thin. You've been looked for."
   136	
   137	"By the bench?"
   138	
   139	"By the bench. And by one that walked."
   140	
   141	She led the jenny in under the gate-walk. A lean man stood up there with a long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every evening that she could remember, and he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had never known him do that for her. She understood, crossing the yard, that it was not for her: it was for a girl with one arm, who had sat on those planks beside him and shot at a sack, and he was asking after her in the only way that he had.
   142	
   143	"Her arm's whole," said Oryn, upward. "It held a stair, which it shouldn't have. It's held nothing since."
   144	
   145	The two fingers lay down again on the wood.
   146	
   147	There was no brown mule at the trough. She had known that there would not be, and she looked for it all the same, and was annoyed with herself. The back room had been swept. Her strip of canvas lay along the sill as she had left it, and somebody had put a cold fowl on the plank under a cloth.
   148	
   149	There were seven of them under the back room's window in the morning, in the frost, and the first was sitting bolt upright on the bench's end with a stick across her knees.
   150	
   151	She was a very small old woman. Oryn had mended that knee at a mill, thirteen days before, and had seen its owner go away up a valley on a hurdle between two grandsons. She came over the low place every year before the snow, to her daughter's hearth in this long house; and Oryn had told her, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee here, after the thaw.
   152	
   153	"I said after the thaw," said Oryn.
   154	
   155	"You said it to a woman lying on a hurdle. I never agreed to it." The old woman rapped the knee with her knuckles. "I rested it ten days, as I was bid. Then I came over to my daughter's, as I do, and I came on my own two legs, to find out whether the thing was yours or mine. It's a day and a half. I did it in two. I'd one of the boys with me to carry the bread."
   156	
   157	"On a stick."
   158	
   159	"I never leaned on it. I brought it to hit him with."
   160	
   161	Oryn got down on her heels in the frost, and put both hands round the joint. Her lips went to three. It was all there: the old banks, worn as smooth as a doorstep, and the current going through the place where a ladder had been as though no ladder had ever stood in it. She had promised that knee a look after the thaw, and it had walked over a hill to be looked at before the snow.
   162	
   163	"It's held," she said.
   164	
   165	"I know that it's held. I've just walked a hill on it. I came to have you say so."
   166	
   167	That was one of seven. She did the rest as she always did them, and two of them took a mending apiece, and at the end of it she sat on her keg with her head down for a count of fifty.
   168	
   169	The word came on the second day, with the last of the grain carts.
   170	
   171	It was a long stooped carter whom she knew, fetching for the gates up the water, and he had been as far as the ford with the oxen and a gate beyond. He gave it to her in the yard, as he had been given it, in the order of the road. The mill had had nothing worse than a crushed thumb. The two gates above it had buried nobody. At the ford a man had lost a finger to a chain and kept the hand; they had bound it themselves, badly, and it had not gone bad. And at the gate where the little one was, the breathing was no different. She coughed at night, and slept after.
   172	
   173	Oryn heard the whole of it standing, with her hands under her arms.
   174	
   175	Three weeks, then, and seven gates, and nobody dead of it. She did not take that for a reason. A person who has crossed thin ice once has not learned that ice will bear; she has been lucky, and knows it by the cold in her stomach afterward. But the round was whole. Every gate on it was where she had left it, with everybody in it that she had left there, and waiting.
   176	
   177	She owed that bench two days more. Then she would go north by the wicket.
   178	
   179	---
   180	
   181	It was the steading's woman who told her about the carter, on the evening of the hundred and twenty-eighth, with the sky gone the colour of a slate.
   182	
   183	"He goes down to the board at first light, with my bark. He'll not come up again this side of the thaw, if that does what it looks like." The woman jerked her chin at the north. "If you've anything to send to that crew of yours, it's tonight."
   184	
   185	"They're not mine."
   186	
   187	"No. You've said." She set a strip of clean bark down on the scrubbed plank, and a pot of the hold's ink, and a pen cut from a goose quill. "There it is, all the same."
   188	
   189	Oryn looked at it for some time after the door had shut.
   190	
   191	She did not keep a book. She had said so to the pale one across a fire: a healer who needs a page to know who is owed has stopped looking at them. She had never in thirty rounds set down a word about any person whom she had had her hands in. But those five were two days south, with a saddle between that would be shut by morning, and she could not look at them. There was a girl down there with an arm in a knot of hers, who had been told *first snow*; and by the feel of that sky she would be looking at it out of a window tomorrow.
   192	
   193	She could not read an arm from here. She had said as much in a yard, of a nearer gate than this.
   194	
   195	So the arm would wait until she could have it between her two hands, and that would not be before the thaw. The girl knew who was to say when. There was nothing in that to write.
   196	
   197	What was there, then? She went at it as she went along a bench, a person at a time, and told each the truth in a sentence.
   198	
   199	The boy would be carrying her round. He had been told at a bar of iron that he was to have it and do nothing with it, and he had never learned to do nothing with a thing. He should hear how it stood.
   200	
   201	The small one had been in a loft when Oryn said that an old woman's knee did not care what was under a floor. She was a person who liked to know how a thing came out.
   202	
   203	And there was what a round of three weeks had cost, which was the sum that all five of them would have been doing since a fork. It was not a kindness to tell them. It was a finding, and she gave people their findings.
   204	
   205	That left what she wanted, and she looked squarely at it, as she had on a road out of Oxhollow and under a cold stone. There was a calfskin with a strip of brass on it that pointed somewhere. Wherever it pointed, there would be an edge; and at the edge, somebody ought to be standing on the right side with her sleeves pushed up. She had said it in a loft, and she had been shown twice since what it was worth. And he was on the route, which meant one thing and no more.
   206	
   207	She went over what she was leaving out, as she would have gone over a bench at the end of a day, to be sure that nobody had been missed.
   208	
   209	There would be nothing in it about a plank. That stood where she had put it, and a strip of bark was no place to move it from. There would be nothing about a still thing under a boy's breastbone; she had told him to leave it alone, and she would not be the one who put a finger on it from two days off. She would not ask after a mule. And she would not tell five people that she had been glad of their bowl, since she had eaten out of it standing, on purpose, for nine days, and they had every one of them seen her do it.
   210	
   211	She dipped the pen. She wrote slowly, in a hand that had not been used since a healing house's morning list, and she did not write one word that she would not have said aloud at a gate.
   212	
   213	It came to four lines, and a letter for her name.
   214	
   215	*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
   216	
   217	---
   218	
   219	The carter came over the saddle on the hundred and thirty-first morning, with a hand's depth of snow on the road and his eyebrows white, and he had a strip of bark for Pike and another that was not for Pike at all.
   220	
   221	"From the healer," he said, at the inn's door. "She was writing. I never saw her write. I'm to say that it's for the five of you and that there's no answer wanted."
   222	
   223	Cael took it to the hearth. There were not a dozen people in that long room so early. Brom was laying out the bowls at their end of it, as he had at every halt, and Lira sat on the hearthstone with her right hand full of kindling. Karis had come out of the room behind the chimney with the hide under her arm, and Seln stood at the far end of the fire, by the small window that looked down on the landing.
   224	
   225	It was a hand that Cael had never seen. It was square, and stiff, and it pressed, like a person lifting something heavy. He read the four lines out, as they stood, a stop at a time: the route, and a knee, and what three weeks had cost; and then the thaw, and a map, and what he was to do when he was told.
   226	
   227	Nobody spoke for a breath. Brom had six bowls in a row on the board. He looked along them. He had put the last one down a little apart, as he always did; and he did not take it back.
   228	
   229	"Six, by letter."
   230	
   231	It was Lira who said it. She had turned on the hearthstone, and her eyes had gone past him to that window, where the snow was lying a hand deep on the sill. Then they came down to the grey wool across her own breast, and to a knot that was not hers.
   232	
   233	"She'll say when," she said.
   234	
   235	"After the thaw," said Brom.
   236	
   237	Karis had the hide unrolled on the board by then, between the bowls, with a pebble on each corner. There were four rings on it. The fourth lay at the very edge of the skin, where the carter had never had a wheel; and through the middles of the other three a line was ruled in pencil, very fine, which ran on through the fourth and off the hide.
   238	
   239	She laid the Ternhall brass along that line, on the three centres that she had paced, and took her hands away.
