   241	"Yours," she said. "From the other end."
   242	
   243	He had the strip from Denvash in his coat. It was cold from the back room, and it took the firelight all along one edge. He set it down on the skin beyond hers, with its end on the ring by the marsh, and slid it up until the two lay in one length; and he bent, as she had bent beside a fire in the dark, and looked for daylight.
   244	
   245	There was none. The edge lay on the dot at the hall, and on the dot at the Stair, where hers met it; he moved it along, and it lay on the dot at the Fallow. It touched the three for him exactly as hers had touched them for her.
   246	
   247	*The sites were on an alignment. Not random.*
   248	
   249	He did not say it. It was Karis who spoke, with her finger on the place where the pencil left the skin.
