   265	Nothing out here had read him. At three floors older than any registry, every Path in the party had stopped at a line and his had not; a fourth had been paced from outside and not crossed. Nobody alive — least of all him — knew why. Someone had made the pattern. That was as far as anyone could say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:3:They were out of Lowmarch two hours before the light on the hundred and thirteenth day, with the stars still hard over the river and every blade in the meadow standing white.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:7:They passed the well at the fork in the dark. It had a new rope on its windlass, and somebody had mended the curb where six stones had been set; and Cael, who had hauled foul air out of that shaft for three days on a bitten leg, put his hand on the stonework as he went by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:41:The lean-to had no front. It was three walls of board and a roof of turf, with the mill's warm stone for its back; she had her plank on two kegs in the middle of it, and her things laid out along a shelf as they had lain on a sill at Thornwater. He could see the whole of her work from where he sat, and he watched it as he had once watched a sixth-year's bout from a rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:45:It was a bad hand. He was one of the miller's men, and a rope had taken it nine days before, against the drum of the hoist that lifted the sacks; it had been bound up in a meal-bag since, and it lay in her palms like a thing that had been trodden on. She had her eyes shut. Her lips were moving, and Cael found that he knew where she was by the count: at three she had the banks, and by seven she had gone along the bed to where it was stopped. He had lain under those hands for twenty minutes. He had a fragment in him of what she was doing, the tenth, and it sat quiet under his breastbone and did not stir toward her, since it stopped at the length of his own arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:49:"That's one," said Oryn. "You'll not close it on a rope before the thaw. Send in the child."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:85:She did eleven of the rest with one hand each, and that took her less time than the knee had. There was a carter with a wrenched back and a woman whose tooth she looked at and would not touch. She laid two fingers on a wrist, or a palm on a forehead, for a count of ten, and told each of them the truth in a sentence. She put tape on a thumb. She told a big sullen man that there was nothing wrong with him that a week away from his brother would not cure, and the whole bench laughed, the man included.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:153:"I was to be at this gate on the ninety-sixth. I came in on the hundred and twelfth. That's sixteen." The thumb went down. "There's a day's bench here still, that I'd have done tomorrow, and the road beyond it: two gates, and then the ford with the oxen on the hundred and twenty-first, where they looked for me on the hundred and fourth. That's seventeen." A finger. "Now put your ridge into it. It's a half-day up to wherever this is, and I'd not see the place before dark. A day while you're in it. A half-day down. That's two, if every one of you walks out on his own legs, and you've not once done that since I knew you." Another finger. "So the ford's nineteen. And beyond the ford there's a child that I heard on the eighty-ninth and can't hear now before the hundred and twenty-eighth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:227:They were half an hour getting away, since a healer does not leave a gate as a crew leaves a camp. She went into the mill and came out with a loaf and a cheese that somebody had pressed on her. She showed the miller's wife how the hand was to be bound at night. And then she went round to the back of the cart-shed with thirty feet of three-strand rope over her shoulder, and what she brought back on the end of it made Brom put his hand over his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:233:The brown mule had turned her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:239:"She's a mule. She knows where the hay used to come from." Oryn took up the rope. "You'll keep on the low side of her, as I told you. I'll go behind with this one, where she can't see the load and want it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:7:They were alone under the ash. The nearest of the other fires was forty yards off, with two carters asleep at it, and the mule stood between with the boxes under her blanket. Brom had built their own fire up. Lira sat on the middle box with her foot set down whole and her right hand over the knot of her sling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:9:"The margin is pricked by the same wheel," said Seln. "There is a tooth missing from it. Every eleventh hole stands a little wide of its neighbours, on both sheets, in the same place. And the hand is one hand. He makes the foot of that letter with a second stroke, which is a fault, and he has made it twice." He lowered the papers. "They were written at one desk, by one clerk, I should say at one sitting. He ruled two sheets and filled them one after the other, and they were folded into the same wrapper before the ink of the second was a day old."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:"The first was a question, and I said so at a stake. It sent a crew to fetch what was not there, in order to learn what became of the crew. A hundred marks is a fair price for a question. It is high, since nobody had ever taken such a paper; but a carter could believe in it." His finger moved to the bracket. "Nobody who has kept a book believes in this. It is two days out and two home, for a case that one person can carry on a strap. There is not a hold on that sheet that would pay thirty for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:19:"So he's overpaid," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:21:"No; he has measured. He knows what the first was priced at and who took it. He knows that five people took it who have a hundred or so in hand and the cold term coming, and he has asked himself what sum such a crew cannot leave hanging on a nail." Seln's voice did not change. "That's not a wage. That's a lure, cut to fit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:Nobody at that fire needed the list read out. There was a Wind on it, and an Iron Skin, and an Ember, and a Shadow, each with a tier and a rank, in files that had been kept for years on the far side of the Line. And there was a boat gone west ten days ago to say that every one of those four had walked onto a certain kind of ground and been able to do nothing there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:47:He said it to the fire, in the mild voice in which he had once read out a floor schedule, and then he sat back out of the light. Cael understood him well enough: a broker's clerk had stood at a plank for a hundred days and watched which crew took which paper, and it had wanted only one fact about a boy to write the second sheet; the boy had supplied that fact himself, aloud, at an inn's boards, when he showed that he had never yet walked past a paper that paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:59:"He's just told you what it is. It's got a ruled edge and a word for itself. I don't need it read to me a third time." Her right hand had closed on the knot. "There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts. There's a fence at forty, the day the hold comes off it. That's ninety marks of work that was posted by people who want a fence. We'd be down from both before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:73:"Three hundred's a number a man doesn't reach for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:77:"It isn't. You said what the paper is. I'm saying what the number is." He frowned at the coals. "My father bought a horse at a fair, the year I was nine. A grey, sixteen hands, with a neck on him like a bridge. Every man on that field could see that he was worth three hundred of anybody's money, and the dealer wanted a hundred for him, and wouldn't say why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:79:Cael kept still, since it was the third time in twelve days that Brom had spoken of that house, and he had never before told a whole thing out of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:81:"My father knew horses. I'll give him that; it was the one thing that he did know. He went all over that animal with his hands for an hour, and there was nothing wrong with a leg or an eye or his wind. So he stood there. I can see him standing. He knew that a sound horse doesn't go for a third of his price, and he knew that he'd found nothing, and the two wouldn't lie down together." Brom's big shoulders moved. "And then he reached, since it was three hundred for one, and he could not bear to see another man lead it off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:103:"Yes. I have heard what it is, and I do not dispute a word of it. I say only that I should go if there were nobody to go with me, and that I should very likely not come back, and that I should go regardless." She drew a breath. "That is not a reason for any of you. I want it known for what it is. It is the thing that I want, and I have learned to say those aloud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:155:Lira's eyebrows went up, and Cael felt his own go. In two years on a bluff, and a hundred and twelve days of roads, he had never heard Brom repeat what another person had said to him. Brom carried other people's words inside his shirt, on dead slips, and did not say them over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:189:*Hundred and twelfth day. Lowmarch, the meadow. Pike said the eighth line in our hearing, twice. Eleventh; Teague ten up. He says his book has no column for what the line means. A hundred and two in hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:191:*A second paper, off the nail. The same clerk and the same wheel. Three hundred. Lira said walk away, and she said it first, and she was right; four hands went up after it, and mine was one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:217:*HOLD.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:223:He got up, which he seldom did once he was down, and turned his shoulder to what was left of the room. He gave it out as he gave out a line: the recovery, and the Stair road, and the ridge, and the three hundred. He said that it was taken, and by whom. And then he said the other word, and he said it slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:227:Cael stood with nothing in his hands, and he knew what that word was for: a captain asked for it so that a job should be kept for him. Pike had turned it round, as a healer had once said that her table was turned: he had put it on a paper so that if five people did not come back, nobody else should be sent up that ridge after three hundred marks to learn why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:239:"Yes," Cael said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:243:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:259:On that same night, the hundred and twelfth, Seln had the second watch, and he spent it reading two papers that were no longer in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:263:The frost was coming down, and he could hear it, very faintly, in the grass. Four people slept behind him, and a mule stood over three boxes; and at eleven other fires in that meadow there was nobody awake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:277:He let himself go back to the corridor, which he allowed seldom. It was distempered green to the height of a man's shoulder, in a district seat three postings and twelve years behind him, and he had stopped dead in the middle of its floor with an armful of somebody's travel endorsements. A clerk had put his head out of a side door to ask whether he was unwell. He had not been unwell. He had understood, in the space between one step and the next, what a certain run of ledgers was that he had copied the year before as dull upkeep; and whose question it had been all along.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:305:For twelve years the thing in the case had been like weather to him. It had fallen on forty children, and on a good many more before them, as rain falls on a field; and a man does not go looking for the face of rain. He files what it did; and Seln had filed it, and had been very good at that, and it had let him sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:321:The paper followed the case. He had said so himself. If the case were to go south tonight, by itself, on one man's chest, then by first light there would be nothing at this fire that anybody in the west had priced. Five people would wake to a walk at fifty and a fence at forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:3:The bar came out of Thornwater's western gate on the hundred and eleventh morning for five people, three boxes and a mule that belonged to none of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:Brom had loaded her by the well, in the grey, with the steading's woman holding the lantern. Two boxes hung from the pack-saddle on their own ropes, one to a side, and the third rode between them on a folded blanket with the empty pannier strapped over it. He had gone round the animal three times, pulling at each knot. Then he had stood back and looked at the load as he would have looked at a wheel that he had built himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:13:They went down the stretch at the one time of day when nothing had ever come up it. Cael walked last. He had the white stone on his left hand at a hundred and ten yards, and he gave it the look that he gave it every time. Fifty-seven evenings had gone by since anything stood there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:35:Seln, who had heard, walked ten paces up the far beach without a word. The mule went into the river as if nothing had ever been the matter. Brom, who had been leaning on the rope, went in with her a good deal faster than he had meant to, and came out on the other side wet to the thigh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:53:*Hundred and eleventh day. The halting-ground above the wash. One reading done, two owed, and nothing tried tonight; I was in a hurry, and she never asked for that. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:57:They came over the saddle above Lowmarch in the afternoon of the hundred and twelfth day, and he stopped on the crown of it and counted what was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:59:It was the fourth time that he had looked down on that town from high ground. He had learned on the first that it was a river, and a ferry, and two inns, and a smith, and a meadow with no wall. On the others he had learned to read the meadow as he had once read a board, since it changed with every week, and whatever was true of that country showed there first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:61:The cold had been at it. Where forty fires had burned when the roads were dry, he made out twelve, drawn in close about the great ash, and half the trodden grass had gone back to white. The ferry's cable stood across the water like a chalk line, with frost on it. The scow lay on the near bank. A brown gelding with one white foot was walking his circle in the capstan-shed's doorway with a blanket on his back, and Cael was glad to see that somebody had thought of the blanket. Both inns were smoking. There was a skin of ice along the eastern bank as wide as a cart, and beyond it the river ran dark and fast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:65:"It's gone home. He said that it would, in the cold term." Brom had the mule's rope, and he was looking at the smith's chimney. "Half of them have fences of their own to sit behind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:75:"Ours. She came in at Millrace last night, down the mill water off the hill track, with a rope on her shoulder and no beast. It's the next gate on her round after that grain hold of yours, and she's sixteen days behind at it." He jerked his chin at the canvas. "I'm the mill's carter. I left a bench full when I pulled out this morning, and my own sister's boy on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:79:"A carter's day. Less, for legs." He looked at the brown mule with some attention, and then at the boxes, and Cael watched him decide that it was no business of his. "You'll want to be quick, if it's her you want. She's never more than two days at our fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:83:They stood with it on the hill for a breath. She had told them that if they wanted her sooner than Thornwater they were to ask a carter, and they had asked the first carter that they met, and she was a day from where they stood. Cael had expected to count gates for a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:93:They went down into the meadow with the light going. The old place under the ash was empty, since a good many places were, and Brom unloaded there and rubbed the mule's back with a twist of hay while Seln walked once round the twelve fires and came back and said that there was nothing. A boy came for the standing-money. Karis gave him two coppers, which were too small a matter for her book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:97:"He's not said ours," said Brom. "The Fallow. He wrote it eleven days ago, and sent it up to us on a piece of bark, and it's not been said where we could hear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:109:It was the eighth line under their heading. Cael had read it on a scrap of bark in a grain hold, across the bones of a goat, and he knew every word; but he had never heard it said. Pike gave the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the broker's posting. He said that it was taken and witnessed. He said what had not been found there, and that the crew had come back. He gave the count of those who went in and of those who came out, which was the same count, and at the end of it he said that no price was paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:115:It was a thin house. There were perhaps twenty people on benches that had held forty, and every one of them had heard that line on the hundred and first day, when it was written. They had not then had the five to look at. Cael felt the room turn its attention on them as he had felt a yard do it once, at an academy on a hill. But this was no registry's look. Nobody's eyes went to where a tier ought to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:119:Three old men sat beyond her, at boards of their own. Cael had seen them under the ash for a hundred days without ever learning what they did, three grey heads over a pair of dice. The dice lay on the wood between them now. The eldest had his hand half closed over them, and he neither threw nor gathered them up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:Pike had dipped his pen. He did not write with it. The line had been in his book for eleven days. He raised his eyes to the sailcloth over his head, and found the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:145:She had gone straight to it, on her whole sole, and stood with her nose a hand from the square black writing. Cael came up beside her. The five names were where she had said, on the eleventh line; and over them, where there had been two crews under theirs, there was one that had been written back above.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:153:"You've read my lines. Every one says what came back, and what was paid on it. That one says the things weren't there and the box stayed shut. A hold comes down to my plank with a job to give, and asks me who fetches what he's sent for. I've a crew that was sent for a thing and came home without it." He held up his one hand. "It's nothing against you. The cloth can't say whose fault a thing was. It can only say what was on the cart."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:157:"On the cloth. I never said the cloth was all of it." He looked at the fire for a breath, and then at his book, and he went on to the book, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "I've had that sheet thirty years. Forty-odd crews have been on it, one time and another, and gone off it. Some went home. Some the road had. There's not one of the forty that ever went over a line and came back to tell me so; there's none that I ever heard of who tried. I've a line in here now that says it was done." His finger lay on the leaf. "Six in and six out, and the sixth a healer. I'd a good look for the column to put that in. There isn't one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:163:He said it mildly, and he took up his cup. Cael stood before a sheet of sized sailcloth with eleven lines on it, and found that he was not thinking of the line that they had lost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:183:"In the broker's strong-box, with his seal on it, and him not knowing what was in it. He says so. I believe him; he's not a man who'd keep three hundred marks shut up for a month if he knew that he had them." Pike took his finger away. "I said it to the meadow, twice. Nobody scratched. Then Teague came down off his fence for flour, and read it, and hung it where you found it. That's eight days it's been on my nail."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:187:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Stair road. South of Lowmarch; the first day; where the road drops off the ridge and the ground opens. Three hundred marks (three hundred).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:191:For the space of a breath there was only the fire. Three hundred marks was more than twice what the five of them had earned in a hundred and twelve days. He did the sum without wishing to, and saw Brom doing it, with his lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:Outside, the frost had come down on the meadow, and their own fire was the smallest of the twelve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:11:"May I read you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:13:"Yes. That's the answer, and I'm the one giving it, and I want you to notice that it's the same day as the lean-to and a different question, and that the rule held both times. Put your hand on it. Surface first. Count of ten. Don't go in past the count until I say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:29:He went to her. She held out her wrist, inside upward, as he had seen a hundred people hold theirs out to her; it was broad and brown, with a white scar across the heel of the hand that he had never been near enough to notice. He put his right hand on her wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:53:"Tide," he said, at ten, and took his hand away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:57:"Well, I'd hope so," said Oryn. "I've been one for eleven years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:115:"Yes," she said. "That's what I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:117:She said it without any pride, and without the least surprise, as she might have agreed that her coat was brown. Then she got up off the rock and went to take the grey mule's rope out of Brom's hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:131:The ridge did not run down into a valley. It broke off, as a table breaks off at its edge, and its western face went down in one long slope of pale grass to a low place that lay below them like the print of an enormous thumb. It was a bowl, though so shallow that a cart might have been driven anywhere in it. He put it at five hundred yards from side to side, which was far wider than the swell that the Fallow Ring had stood on, and it had not half that swell's height.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:133:The carters' ruts did what he had seen them do once before. Halfway down the slope the ground eased into a kind of brow, and at the brow the live ruts swung away to the north and went round the whole of that low place, keeping their distance from it, and came back to their line on the far side. He could see them all the way, two pale threads on the grass, and inside them an older road went straight on down, grown over, with a hundred years of turf in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:147:"It's what he calls the road," said Karis. Her voice was very small. "It is on no map that I own, and every carter in that meadow has known for a hundred years what is at the end of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:159:They brought the mules down to the brow. Karis gave Brom her box-rope to hold, and went on by herself down the old road toward the floor, with her arms a little out from her sides, counting under her breath. Cael walked a stride behind her and to one side, and he had no need to count, since he was watching the back of her neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:161:At forty paces she stopped as if she had come to a wall. Her sigil had gone out at that stride, as it had gone out on the Fallow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:171:"Two hundred. I'll pace it in the morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:199:"That first has stood five cold terms. I said so of its fellow on the Fallow, and I see no cause to alter it." He bent closer. "This has had one. Two, at the most. And it is the same number. Thirty-two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:221:There were two bars of iron, eight feet apart, with a road going between them. Anchor had been asking at every sill and gate and rail for the better part of a year, and it asked now, in its thin held note: here was a doorway, and here were two posts to it, and would he not like something set across? He told it no, as he had told it at a stake on the Fallow. He left unexamined whatever might have been set there already, since he had no cause to examine it; and that refusal he was to go over more than once in the day that followed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:223:They made their camp on the outside of the stone, between the two irons, with the brow forty paces above them and the mules hobbled a little up the slope. Brom laid out six bowls, and he did it without looking at anybody, and the healer ate out of the last of them standing up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:225:She came by Cael afterward, at her stride and a half, with the grey mule's rope over her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:241:"I have walked the ruts," said Seln. "All the way round, at dusk. It is sixteen hundred paces and a little more. I found no person and no sign of one, and I shall walk it again before light. That is a fact about the ruts." He folded his hands. "If I had laid this, I should not be on the ruts tonight either. I should be a quarter of a mile back from them, on my face, in grass of this colour, and I should come up to them when you were over that stone and not before. So I shall tell you what I can see, which is nothing. And I shall tell you what I should assume, which is that they are here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:255:"I'll stand at that stone with my sleeves up. I said it, and I meant it." Oryn was sitting on her heels. "I've learned what my hands are worth on the far side of one. If a thing goes wrong in there, you bring it to me. It's two hundred yards. I've seen an arm carried as far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:275:*Hundred and thirteenth day, night. At the edge of the second one. A stone, and two bars, both numbered thirty-two; one five cold terms old, one a cold term or two, with a mark under its number that Seln won't read. A floor with no stones on it, and a hole, and a stair. Nobody on the ruts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:277:*Tide: once, on the healer, on the ridge road. I asked twice today and she granted the second. The short count and then a minute of the long, at one sitting; I am counting that as one, since it was one asking and one yes. At three there were banks. I knew the Path by ten. In the minute, one channel and one thing in it, going somewhere. It cost her nothing that she would notice. It cost me the use of that hand for an hour, and most of a loaf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:3:They went in at first light on the hundred and nineteenth day, with a mist lying on the reeds as thick as wool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:7:Five sigils had gone out at the stone, one after another, and nobody had remarked on it. Nobody spoke on the path. The reeds stood over them on both hands, white with rime, and forty feet out on the left the stems leaned apart and closed again, keeping their pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:11:She stood on the kerb, where it was two strides broad, and set her pannier down between her feet. She was a long way inside the line, with no Path to count by, and she knew it. She folded her arms, and looked across thirty yards of water at a gap in a grey wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:25:It was a room. He could see that much while he walked, and he took it in as he had once taken in a floor from a rail, without stopping. It was twelve strides each way. The walls were a yard through, and they stood three times Brom's height at the far corner and were broken down to the height of a man's chest in two places on the left, with every break worn as round as a river stone. There was no roof and there never could have been a beam; the mist lay across the top like a lid. And the inner face of every wall was cut. The sunk lines ran in their courses from the water's skin to the broken tops, course over course, straight and hooked and crossing, nine deep where the wall was whole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:47:Cael was two strides behind him. He heard the knocking stop. It had been coming round the ledge after them, plate by plate, under the northern wall; and it left off between one plate and the next, as a footstep leaves off outside a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:65:Seln had not raised his voice. He had pitched it, as he had once pitched a single word down two hundred yards of old road, and it carried to every corner of that room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:95:The knocking came round to it at once. It did not wait for eleven. The plate went up at four, and the two halves shut, and the wash of it ran along the foot of three walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:127:Brom had got himself up to sitting. He sat astride the low wall as a man sits a horse, with his bound arm against his chest, and he was studying the gap beside him. Cael could read what was in his face. The gap was two strides wide. On its far side the wall began again, low, and ran on to the corner, and round, to where the others were; and at the foot of the gap, between the two ends of wall, lay the sill. It was the old work. A man who stood on it stood on nothing that the thing below could read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:175:Cael did the sum, since it was the only thing left to him that was quick. Brom lay two strides from the sill. She had fifteen yards still to cross, hopping. The thing had been at a stone in eleven counts from a third of the dish away, and it had only the width of a room to go. And when she reached him she would stop, since a healer who has come to a fallen man gets down on her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:179:He had ten fragments. He went through nine of them in the time of one breath, as he had on a frozen road, and it was different, because here there was nobody to see, and he need hold none of them back. Wind was in his feet already. It would put him over the wall in a count. Then it would lock him where he landed, dumb, for a count more, the stillest thing on that floor, at a time and a place that he had not chosen. Storm would lay the water flat, and what was that to a thing with no eyes? Ember's fire was for things that could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:181:There was Anchor, which he had set for the first time yesterday, on his knees, on a kerb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:223:The water took him to the waist, and it was so cold that it had no feeling in it. He waded two strides, with the ground gone soft and sliding under him, and fell forward across the great slab that lay aslant in the middle. It was a hand under the surface. He got his right palm down upon it, and told it to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:225:Anchor went down his arm, and the point stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:229:He was already moving, since he had not yet begun to pay. He threw himself back across the two strides of deep water, and got his chest onto the ledge, and his knees; and behind him the whole of the hole stood up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:247:It was Seln who sent it across the room, in the voice for distances; and beyond the gap a square brown woman with a very big man's arm over her shoulders did not turn her head to ask why. There were two strides of plate between the pair of them and the sill, and nothing under those for as long as a boy could hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:27:She sat down where she had knelt, on the frozen turf, with her knees drawn up and her forehead on them, and it was as long as it takes to count a hundred before she lifted her head. Nobody went to her. Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:51:He sat with his back against a bar that the registry had driven five cold terms ago, and his legs straight out before him, as wet to the waist as the healer was. The fire was three yards off, and he knew that he ought to go to it; he did not seem to have the means, and so he went over his bill instead, since that could be done sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:55:There was the other thing, for which he had no good word. He had held a point against a pair of jaws while four bolts went in, and it had taken everything else that he owned to do it; not one of the ten fragments had yet come back to where it lived, and he felt like a house from which the furniture has been carried out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:75:It was only three words. But it had not come out as her other sentences came, each one finished and set down like a bowl on a board; the middle of it had given a little under her, as a plate gives underfoot. He made no answer, since he did not think that she could have borne to be answered. She had told a mill that she would be nobody's sixth, and a board at Thornwater that she was a stop; she had eaten out of their bowl for a week, standing; and this was the only way that she had of saying what a person became to her when she had gone into a marsh after his friend and watched him go into worse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:113:The first was the one that she had lit, at the track's end, with wet people round it and a line of breeches steaming on a rope between two bundles of reeds. The second she built for herself, twenty yards off and upwind, no bigger than a hat, since she would not have sparks near paper. She had the lid of the middle box for a table there, and her morning's work laid out on the turf under pebbles, one sheet beside another, to lose the damp that they had taken from a wall. She had gone into that place for the inner faces of four walls, and had come out with as much of one of them as a person can rub upside down in an hour with a girl holding her by the belt; and she set about it now as if the hour had been a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:139:Cael had been on roads with that woman for nineteen days in all, on a mountain and a moor and a ridge, and he had never once seen it. She lay down last at every camp, a stride and a half beyond the farthest of them, with a mule between, and at Thornwater she had shut a door. She had told him on a rock that there was nobody there who had not seen her asleep, and he had taken it for a manner of speaking; she slept as a person pays a debt, privately, to have it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:161:*Anchor: one point, on the slab in the hole, held while four went in. I couldn't have told you my own name.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:237:"Yes. Hand on the wrist. The short reading first, to ten, and then the neck, and the long reading for one minute, which I'll count. You'll talk while you're in. If I say *out*, you come out with the sentence in your mouth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:263:"I gave it one a long while back, on an old leaf, when it was only something that another person's hands had caught the edge of. The still place. I never had anything to lay the words on till last night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:275:"A still place that everything goes around. I've never felt that in anyone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:283:"You weren't frightened, saying it. I'd have felt that; your step didn't alter." She rubbed her hands down her breeches. "That's the three, and the telling, as I set them at a well. It's paid. The other thing was never a debt, and it stands where I put it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:299:*Tide: once, on the healer, by her leave.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:301:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:They came over the crown of the rise as he finished. Cael knew the man in front by the way he stopped there, with his thumbs in his belt, and looked at everything before he came on. He looked at the hall first, and for some time. Then his eyes came down the old ruts to a rope of stiff breeches between two bundles of reeds. They went to a big man lying on his back with an arm strapped across him, and to a girl on a grey stone with a crossbow on her knee. Last of all they went, as they always went, to the number of people who were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:41:"A bolt, six days gone. I drew it and shut it on a road. Then he came off a wall yesterday and hung by it, and I shut it again, in that ditch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:51:Cael waited to see how a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, would take that from somebody who had never mended a thing in her life by laying a palm on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:61:"It's a drover's. It keeps the weight on the ribs and off the joint." The woman did it again. "I've no Path that knits, girl. I can't shut a hole. I can keep one shut for a cold term, if he's the sense to let me, and I've done it for forty of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:71:It had been the hundred and seventeenth morning, three days before, when Teague took his crew up the notch from the mill with one more in it than had come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:He had not set it. The woman with the net had not put her mark on the shingle, and the four marks were lost, as he had said they would be. He had sent his word north by the mill's carter, in so many words, for the man with the book: five strokes to look at, and all of them walking; and the three hundred was to stay where it lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:85:He did not think that anybody had. He thought it in the same place where he had thought that a certain paper was wrong, which was the back of his neck, and he had learned in nine cold terms what that was worth: it was worth going to look. He had said *we'll come look* to that boy across an inn's floor. He had gone up a ridge and looked, and had found the thing half over. It seemed to him now that the looking was not finished until he knew whether six faceless people had turned round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:97:The boy had stopped eating. The spear had said nothing, and the old woman had looked at Teague over her bowl as she had looked at him for nine cold terms whenever he was about to do something that she would have to bind afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:109:He had not asked her to come. He had only told her what he had seen, and she had named the beast that she would load. It was how she had joined the crew in the first place, nine cold terms ago, at a ford with a carter's leg in it: she had looked at how the thing was being carried, and had put down her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:131:It was the middle of the hundred and twentieth day when the ground fell away in front of him, and he saw reeds, and grey walls standing in water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:137:He looked at the rope between the reeds. There were breeches on it, frozen as stiff as boards, six pairs, and they had not got so wet by walking on grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:139:Teague stood on that rise with his thumbs in his belt, and added it up. They had gone into the Fallow, six of them, and come out; a one-armed man had said so twice under his eave. They had gone into the Stair and one had walked out of it declaring. And here was the drowned hall, which nobody went near, and a rope of wet breeches, and six at a fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:141:A book had columns. It had one for what was sent for, and one for what came back, and one for what was paid. He had been at the head of Pike's cloth for a good many cold terms by filling those three better than other men. He could think of nothing that this crew had done since the Fallow that would go into any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:143:He had told himself at a grey stone that a captain who asks a question he has no column for has only given himself something more to carry. It had been sound, and he had had three to carry then. He had four now. And he was proposing to stand on some floor beside that boy after the thaw, if the boy went where he seemed to be going. A man does not walk his people up to a thing that he has been too careful to ask the name of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:37:The healer had listened to the whole of it with the grey mule's rope over her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:45:"I shut that shoulder last night. A mend so deep wants a week of somebody's eye on it, as her arm wanted its fortnight, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends an hour of herself on a thing and walks off in the week that it's deciding. That's the hundred and twenty-first." A finger followed the thumb. "There's an edge at the end of that brass. I said in a loft where somebody ought to stand when you cross one, and I learned yesterday what it's worth to stand there." Another finger. "And I've sent word north that I'm nineteen, or more. It's the *or more* that I'm spending."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:59:They left the stone at first light on the hundred and sixteenth day, and for three days the country was kinder to them than it had any need to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:65:Brom had the brown mule and two of the boxes, and the kit on his own back by its strap. The healer came last with the grey, which carried the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:95:The track went under the turf on the morning of the hundred and eighteenth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:153:"That has stood five cold terms. It is of an age with the Fallow's, and with the old one at our last stone, and I should say that the same mallet drove all three." He brushed his knee. "There is no second. Nobody has come back to this place with a newer bar and a mark under his number; or if he came, he drove nothing. I shall not choose between those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:187:It was a hundred and eighty-five yards from the stone to that kerb, as Karis paced it afterward, and nobody else among them could have told iron from a reed at such a distance. Cael did not doubt her. Somebody had come here five cold terms ago with a cord and a mallet, and had driven one stake on the line, where he was sent to drive it. Then he had walked in along that grey path to set another on the lip of the thing itself; and whatever lay under the lip had not cared for the weight of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:219:They walked. Though not one of them could have said so yet, that was the rule of the place, and it kept them company the whole of the way in: a hundred and eighty-five yards, which Karis counted under her breath, with something in the reeds holding its station on them for exactly as long as they did not stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:223:It was two strides broad, of the same grey, and it went away to the right and the left in a great curve; Cael put the whole round of it at seventy yards across. Within it there was water, a foot or two deep and as clear as a well's, moving slowly outward and spilling over the kerb's low places into the marsh. Under the water lay the floor. It had been one floor once. It was broken now into great plates, like the ice on a pond that has gone to pieces and frozen again, and every broken edge was round. Thirty yards off, in the middle of it, the walls stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:247:It was eleven for the second stone also, and Karis wrote the two figures side by side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:251:She had a dozen pebbles in her belt, for the corners of the hide. She chose among them with her thumb. She had stood on the gravel bar at Lowmarch through forty evenings with a river in front of her and nothing that she was allowed to do, and Cael had seen what she did with stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:267:"It was eleven for a thing that lay where it fell," said Karis slowly. "It is fourteen for that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:275:She gave it to him before she had thought, and then she stared, and so did the rest of them. He had read out a great many things to that notebook in a hundred days: the width of a road, and the age of a bar, and what a paper meant. He had asked each time that it should be entered as said. He had never once put a mark in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:293:"I carry one fragment that was made for nothing else. It's Anchor, the thinnest of the ten. I've had it for the better part of a year, and it has asked at every sill and gate and rail from Ostrand to this kerb, and I've told it no each time." He made himself say the rest. "I've let it look, and I've taken two of another person's down. I have never set one, and I can't tell you that I'm able to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:303:The water was as cold as the trough at Thornwater. He reached down through a foot of it and laid his palm on the nearest plate, which was smooth, and furred a little with silt. He knew what he was about to make. He had been inside two of them, and had felt one against the front of his coat in a chalk cut: a thing set against a surface, and told to stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:305:Anchor did not leap, as Wind would have. It went down his arm as a plumb line goes down a well, and there was a point under his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:319:It was like having a tooth drawn. Every other fragment that he owned went thin and far away, Wind and Storm and the tenth together, and the cold of the water, and the kerb under his knees; there was only the one point, and something very strong that wished to take it up, and himself saying that it should stay. The jaws came down a second time, and a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:363:"I'll be on the kerb, where the path stops. It's two hundred yards from there to my hands, near enough, and I've learned how far a person can be carried."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:367:"If it comes for one of you, I've a fragment that it will go to first. I held Anchor for sixty. I don't know that I can hold it for longer." He looked at Seln. "There's nobody on any rim. If the floor opens, I'll use what I have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:375:"Yes," Karis said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:387:*Anchor: one point, set on a plate at the kerb's foot, and held for sixty counts. The first that I ever set. It came at eleven and stayed with it till I let go, and while it did I had nothing else at all. Not counted in the number. I'll not call a trial a use.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:3:A hawk over that place at first light on the hundred and fourteenth day would have seen three rings, one inside another, and a line drawn across them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:5:The outermost was the carters' road, two pale ruts going the whole way round the low ground. Forty paces inside it lay the edge that could not be seen, where a grey stone sat on the turf between two iron bars. And in the middle, two hundred and twenty yards from the stone, was the floor, as round as a coin, with a square hole at its centre no bigger than a cart. The line across all three was the old road. It came down off the ridge from the east, over the brow where the ruts turned aside, between the two bars, and straight on down the frosted grass to the floor's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:31:Cael looked up the old road, into the sun, and had to put his hand over his eyes. On the brow, where the ruts turned north, there was a shape standing that had not been there when Seln walked the ruts in the dark. It stood forty paces above the stone. There was a second shape beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:33:Forty paces to the stone, and five yards beyond: that made forty-five. He knew that distance, since he had walked a mile once in front of a cart with a grey thing keeping it behind him in the grass, patiently, waiting upon a signal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:41:Something was wrong with that shot, and though he had only fifteen yards to cover, it took him the whole of them to say what. A bow on that brow could have let all five of them walk down to the floor, and had them two hundred yards inside, with nothing over them and nowhere to go. It had chosen to shoot the last man instead, when he was five strides from the way out, with a healer standing in plain sight at the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:45:He was twelve feet from the stone when he made up his mind. He had refused Anchor, the thinnest of his ten, at these two irons the evening before, and again in the frost an hour ago. He let it look now, on purpose, with his eyes open; and since he had stopped dead to do it, he stood on a frozen road for the length of two counts while a friend with a bolt in him was being turned about by the arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:47:At twelve feet there was a greyness in the air between the bars, like breath. He took one stride more, and it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:57:The woman in the cut had laid nineteen points round him while he fought her, each as fine as a fishing line, from fifteen feet, with the stillness of a person threading a needle. Whoever had laid these was forty paces off. And they were thick: every one was as thick as his wrist, and there were three where she would have used thirty. It was the same trade in a different hand, as a ship's cable and a stitch are the same trade: this hand threw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:61:The healer did not so much as turn her head. She had Brom's left arm across her shoulders and she was bringing him up the road toward the stone at a stumbling run, with her eyes on the grey wool of his coat where a dark stain was beginning. She had three strides to go, and Cael saw her thinking of nothing in the world but those three strides, at the end of which she would be a Tide again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:81:Seln was beside them, having come back up forty yards of road without a sound, and Lira and Karis were behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:97:That made six, and four of them had bows. Every one was the grey of the road in a dry month. They wore hoods, and under each hood there was a cloth drawn up across the face, of the kind that carters wore against dust. Not one of them had called out, or so much as lifted a hand to another. They stood at four stations round five hundred yards of grass, outside an edge that none of them had needed a stone to find, and they looked down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:101:"Can we rush the two?" said Brom. He was grey round the mouth, and his voice was quite steady. "It's forty yards."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:109:He had not called them. They had risen as they had risen in a lane on the Fallow, each offering what it had. Wind was in his feet, and though it could not take him through three courses of that fence, it could take him a long way along the inside of it, faster than a hooded man could run round the ruts. Storm, the heaviest of them, lay ready; there was a rut at his feet, running straight up the hill to the brow, with seed on the grass at either lip. Ember had fire for his hand, and there was a great deal that a boy could do from where he stood, in a place where four of his friends could do nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:143:It was a hundred and ninety yards of grass. He had paced none of it, and he measured it now with his lungs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:155:They were not aimed, or not as the first had been. Cael saw them only when they were falling, two dark strokes dropping steeply from the north and from the south. They had been lofted from better than two hundred and fifty yards by people who knew to a hair how far their bows would throw. One struck the plate ten feet from Brom and leapt up again, end over end, and the other came down beyond the hole and slid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:159:He was on the floor himself then. It was as cold through his boots as the other had been, and as silent, and thirty yards of it had never seemed so far. The hole was in front of him. It was two strides square, with an edge as sharp as a box's; and on its near side a step went down, and another, and a third, each of them white with frost and sunlight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:181:At the fortieth there was no forty-first. His boot came down on the level, and he walked into Seln's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:197:Karis made a sound in her throat. From the floor to the roof, on all four sides, the grey was covered with the fine sunk lines that had been round the feet of eleven stones on the Fallow: straight, and hooked, and crossing. They ran in bands, one above another, each as tall as a man's forearm. There was more of it on one wall of that room than on the whole of the other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:213:It was a plain square opening in the wall, the width of the stair. Seln stood in it with his back to the room and looked up forty steps at a small square of blue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:223:"They are two hundred and sixty yards from that hole, on every side, and very little above it. A bolt that comes in at the top must come in nearly level. It strikes the wall or the upper steps." Seln turned round. "From the rim, nobody can see into this. Nobody can see so much as the fourth step. And anybody who comes to the edge of the hole to look down is a black shape on a bright square, to a person sitting where you are sitting, with that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:301:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:307:The dark came down on them like a cloth. Far up, at the head of forty steps, there was a small square of sky. Two of the six under that lid were bleeding, and it was not yet the middle of the forenoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:5:It stood at the head of forty steps, a man's height on each side; for the first hours after the lamp went out it was grey, and everything else was black. Cael learned the room by its sounds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:85:"I learned at the other one how far a person can be carried with a thing bound up," she said, after one of those counts, to nobody. "It came to about two hundred yards." He heard her rub her palms together. "It's a little over two hundred from the head of that stair to where I'm a healer. It's a short walk. I never saw a longer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:107:"I have had since this morning to read it, and you should have my reading. The bolt she read for herself, on the road, and she read it rightly. A crew with a hurt man and a healer at the stone might have fought its way back to her. A crew with a hurt man and a healer who has no hands will sit still." His mild voice did not alter. "There are six of them, for a rim of sixteen hundred paces. That is a quarter of a mile to each station, and nobody to spare, which is why they have not come down; if one leaves his place, there is a gap in the dish that five people might run for. They will not come in, and they do not need to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:111:"Because there will be a frost tonight like the last. We have no fire. We have a man with iron in him, who will be a good deal worse by morning in this cold, and a healer who can do nothing for him until he is carried past a certain stone. They have only to stand where they are. Some time tomorrow we shall come up that stair with our hands open, to buy two hundred yards of grass."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:133:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:149:"There's a paper, priced so that we'd come. There's a floor at the end of it where a Wind can't run, and an Iron Skin is a big man, and an Ember can't light her own lamp, and a Shadow has a knife and fifteen years. There's a bolt, placed to bring a healer over a line. There's a fence behind her. There are four bows on a rim, far enough back that we can't reach them and near enough to shoot anybody who climbs. And there's a night." He drew a breath. "Every piece of it does one thing. It takes away what one of us can do. It's a list with six names on it and a line through each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:169:"I don't know. I tried it a second time, on a doorway, with Lira on the far side, and I couldn't get hold. I could see it and I couldn't reach it; seeing isn't reach. I'd have to find where this one's made fast, and get my hand on that place, from the inside. I can't see any of it from farther than ten feet." He made himself finish. "And it's better than two hundred yards from here, with six of them looking down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:177:"I shall say the condition, since it is to be used, and since I am the one here who was trained to count who is looking. That one was given, as I understand the matter, to be kept from any person who could carry the seeing of it west. Count who that is, today. It is six people, and every one of them is two hundred and sixty yards from that hole and can see nothing below its lip. If you take it on the stair, under the floor, there is no line from any of them to you; and from that step onward they have nothing to see, which is the whole of what the thing does." He folded his hands. "You let it go again where the ground hides you. Then what they have is a fence that failed, and a boy getting up out of a ditch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:219:It was dusk. Shadow had been lifting in him for an hour, as it lifted at every dusk, like a tide coming up a beach. He had told it no on the fifth evening past the Line, and at a ford with the light going, and on a gate-walk; he had refused it on something over a hundred evenings, and let it have him once, for ten strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:231:It took the whole of his attention, and he saw that at once, and saw the price of it. He was a boy walking across thirty yards of bare stone in the last of the light, with six people looking at that stone; and he could not raise his eyes to them. He had them in his head only: two on the brow in front of him, two away on his left, one on his right, and one behind, where the west was still green. He walked with his eyes in the ground, and stepped over four bolts, and went round a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:235:He took the southern of its two ruts, since that was the side that the newer bar stood on. It was a trough a foot deep and as wide as a wheel, with turf grown down into it, and it ran straight up the fall of the ground to a stone that he could not yet see. He had a hundred and ninety yards of it to walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:239:He had been waiting for it. A thing made for hiding costs from the moment that a person wakes; he had paid that on every day since it came to him, by carrying it shut. To have it open was a different bill. It began in the ends of his fingers, as it had begun in a lane on the Fallow, and it was not the frost, since it came from inside. At fifty strides he could not feel his nails; at a hundred his hands hung at the ends of his arms like things that he was carrying for somebody, and the cold had gone into his wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:241:He looked up once, at a hundred and twenty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:243:He could not have said why, and he paid for it at once. The brow stood against the eastern sky, dark, and on it there were two shapes. One was sitting, with something long laid across its knees; the other stood a little to the south of the first, with its hands drawn up inside its sleeves, as a person stands who has need of his fingers and means to keep them warm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:249:At a hundred and fifty there was a noise that nearly stopped that heart altogether.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:251:It was the grey mule. She was hobbled above the stone with the brown one, and she had put up her head and brayed at the dusk, long and rusty, as she had no doubt brayed at every dusk of her life. The brown mule did not bray. She had turned her ears, both together, down the old road. She was looking straight at the place where he stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:263:He had known that there would not be. He could see the iron itself, a dark stroke above the frosted turf, and the grey stone beyond it; and the air between that bar and its fellow was as empty as any air. He took a stride, and another, and let Anchor look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:265:At twelve feet there was the greyness, like breath on glass. At ten, they were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:271:He stood in a ditch with the cold in his elbows and looked again. The three courses came to the newer bar and touched it, and there was a point where each of them met the iron; but they did not end. They went through those points as a rope goes through a ring, and on, southward along the edge, out of his sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:273:He had got it wrong. He had watched three lines fly across a road toward a bar, and had taken the bar for the end of them. A rope is not made fast where it lands; it is made fast where the hauling starts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:277:The old bar stood eight feet away across the grown-over road, under its five cold terms of rust. He was eleven feet from it, perhaps, where he stood, and at that distance he could make out only that there was something on it. He climbed out of the southern rut, and crossed the crown of the old road on his hands and knees, since the turf there stood a foot higher and he did not trust the thing that hid him to hide him against the sky; and he let himself down into the northern rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:281:It was on the far side of the iron, the side toward the brow, a hand above the turf, and he saw it through the bar, as a person sees a lamp through his own fingers. It was a single point, as big as a fist. Three courses came into it from the south, across the road; and three came into it from the north, round the whole of that great circle, the other end of the same rope. Nothing came out. Everything that held six people in a low place on the hundred and fourteenth day was hauled taut through that one point and belayed there, like the whole of a ship's rigging brought down to a single pin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:293:It went as a breath goes that a person has been holding, and the point was simply not under his palm. The three courses to the south dropped across the old road, and he saw the nearest ten feet of them go, like wet rope falling; and from the north, away round the dark edge of the low ground, he felt the rest come in and go slack and run out through the place where a knot had been, a great deal of it, for as long as it takes to draw a bucket. He had not known how much ground it held until it stopped holding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:295:Forty paces above him, on the brow, a standing shape flung both its arms forward out of its sleeves, as a man does when a rope is dragged through his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:5:It was the hundred and fifteenth morning. He had slept as a stone sleeps, and woken to find that the ground came to his feet when it should, and that his right arm would bend. His left palm was wound in what remained of a roll of tape. He had counted four upright shapes on the rim before he had his boots on, one at each of the places where a hood had stood the day before, and he had been on his feet with nothing in his hands before he saw whose they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:11:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:25:It was the same that she had made at Thornwater, two box-ropes spliced end to end and marked off with twists of red wool; and she had it laid along the turf from the grey stone northward, with its near end under her boot. She looked as though she had not slept, and she looked very happy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:41:At each cord's end Seln knelt and cut a turf, and Karis came up along the rope, counting, with her short cord in her other hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:45:The spear stood on the south, a tall man, grounded, with the butt of his weapon on the frozen turf and both hands on the shaft; and he watched a girl with one arm find an edge with her foot, sixty times over, and said nothing whatever. On the west it was the boy, sitting on his heels in the ruts with a heel of bread. He stopped chewing when Lira came abreast of him, and he had not started again when they were a hundred yards on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:53:"It is the same," she said. "Cord for cord, and the odd strides are the same number of strides." Her voice was not quite steady. "I said two hundred, from the ridge, by my eye, and I was twenty short of it, and a stride. It is the Fallow's round, and so it is the Fallow's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:65:"That's twelve. He'll want it for his count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:85:She had said at the stone that she would not sit outside a second day and listen. Four people who knew what a crossbow was were holding the rim, and she meant to be where a certain shoulder was; if anything opened, it could be carried up forty steps and two hundred yards with her walking beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:103:The healer had stopped at her stride and a half from the nearest wall. She had been round eleven stones on the Fallow thirty times and had never gone near enough to see that they were cut, and she had sat with her back to this for a whole day in the dark. She held up her two palms to it, slowly, as she held them to a patient whom she had not yet touched; and then she let them fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:127:It was the cord that Cael's eye went along first, since it was the only thing there that was going anywhere. It ran out over the floor toward the eastern wall, pale and thin, with a knot in it at every fifth span, and it lay as straight as a ruled line for half the width of the room. There it stopped. The rest of its length lay beside it in a coil that nobody had paid out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:131:"A mallet," said Brom. He was naming them as he came to them, like a man going along a bench of tools. "Iron on the head of it. A bar. And that's a book, on its face." He sounded almost injured. "Is that all? I said that a kit was a cart's worth. I said brass."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:199:"Then this is the book of whoever drove them." Seln kept his eyes on the leaf. "I put five cold terms on the old iron at the stone, and I see no cause to alter that for his book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:203:"It has. So that mark lay in this man's case five cold terms ago, and somebody set the same on a bar at our stone a good deal later. Those are two visits, and I can tell you no more of them. I said that I should not put a meaning on two strokes of a punch; I shall not put a date on them either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:239:"He does not need to say where. That is the whole of what a bearing is." Karis's eyes had gone to the middle box, which Seln had carried down forty steps on his back and set against the northern wall. A calfskin was in that box, with two rings on it that she had walked, and a strip of brass. "It does not have to be written on his leaf. It is written on mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:273:The cup and the bowl stood on the lid of the box, where the lamp could reach them; she had carried them from Thornwater in a twist of cloth. One by one, through the afternoon, he watched the grains leave the bowl: some went into the cup, and some she laid on the stone by themselves, in a row, and those were the ones which the second floor had shown her to be no kind at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:275:There were twenty-nine sheets when the first lamp began to gutter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:279:She counted the grains back into it, one at a time, with all of them standing round and Brom holding his breath. Then she uncovered a figure in the corner of a leaf, which had had her thumb over it for twelve days, and looked from that to the cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:287:"Kinds. Forty-one on the Fallow, when the doubtful ones are taken out or let in as this wall bids, and forty-one in the courses here. I have been over the courses of these four walls for six hours, looking for a forty-second, and there is none in them; and there is not one of the forty-one that is wanting." She looked up. "A man with apple trees told me that I wanted to see them both at the thaw. I have seen them both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:291:There was one thing more, and she had kept it for the last of the oil. She had been over the Fallow's eleven strips for four days at a trestle in a barn, and she had every stroke of them by heart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:295:"This one. It's not on the Ring. I looked for it for four days and it isn't on any upright. It's here, on every wall, and it's not in the courses with the rest — it's *between* them. On its own. Here." The pencil moved along the sheet to the next one, and the next. "And here. And here. I've paced the spacing because I pace things. It comes every — " she looked at the notebook, where she had written it, and read it out as a number she had checked twice — "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, to the stride. It recurs, on the wall, at the perimeter's radius."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:311:"I paced that round this morning with two box-ropes and a girl's left foot. He paced it with a knotted line, for people whom I should not trust to tell me the hour, and his line found what mine did." She laid her hand on the short cord. "A measurement is a fact about a ruler, not about the hand that held it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:329:"Give it to me on the stair," said Cael. "It's forty steps."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:331:"It was forty steps yesterday, with a bolt in, and I counted them." He looked at the three of them, and then at the healer, who had opened her mouth and shut it again. "It's the only thing that we ever fetched for a paper and found, and I'd like to be the one that brings it up. I didn't do a great deal else down here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:335:It was full day at the top, and bright, and so warm after that room that Cael's face ached. The sun stood in the south-west over the rim, and they went up the old road in file, with Seln carrying the middle box and twenty-nine sheets rolled under Karis's arm; and on the southern rim a tall man watched them come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:357:*Hundred and fifteenth day. The stone at the Stair. All six up, and nothing spent. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three. A hundred and two in hand. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against a broker's mark, and that mark is never going on this paper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:359:*Instruments recovered. The contract's paid, and the man who posted it is never going to see them, because Seln's keeping the ledger and I'm keeping the kit. Someone measured this place five years ago and wrote bearing and stopped. Karis has a straightedge. She's been waiting her whole life to use it. The Ring, the Stair, and whatever's on that bearing. Pike said a handful. We have two. The kit says three. We're going to go look.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:3:They turned for home on the hundred and twenty-fourth morning, and it took them six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:17:To Cael, who had learned that crew's sound on a hundred roads and had learned it again at a fork with a pair of boots gone out of it, five had already begun to seem the number that a road was walked by. He did not care for that, and he wrote it down, so that he should be held to noticing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:23:Lira walked in front of him, and he watched her do it for six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:31:Brom had the mule's rope in his good hand, and he did not let go of it. Not one of them went to lift her, since each of them knew what she would say to a hand under that arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:45:Nobody at that fire said what *it* was, and Cael noticed that none of them had, in six days. They spoke of the foot, and the boot, and the tape; and the name of the joint itself went all the way from a fence of iron to a river without being spoken by anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:47:He woke on the hundred and twenty-ninth morning lying on his left side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:53:On the hundred and twenty-fourth night, the first of the road home, Seln read a page and a half that he had by heart already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:61:He had wanted a face, and had gone up a ridge for one, and had been given six hoods. This was the nearest that he had come. It was a person's own book. In twelve years of that matter he had handled a great deal of paper. Every leaf of it had been written by a clerk, at dictation, for somebody up a stair. Nobody had dictated this. A man had gone alone into a hole in the ground with a cord and a mallet, and had put down what he found there, for himself and for no reader, in the order in which he found it, and had been astonished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:69:He had sat in that service for fifteen years. He knew what it did with its instruments when they were done with, and he had supposed that he knew the worst of it. He had not known that it would write *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper, about a kit that was still lying where its owner knelt, and offer three hundred marks for it as a hook to hang five people on. The man with the upright hand had been a clerk of a kind. He had written true lines at somebody's bidding and had not been told what they were for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:71:He had written *nothing to report* at the foot of a weekly return for twelve years, over the top of a case, and had thought it the hardest sentence in the service to write honestly. This man had written a better one. He had written what was there. He had put down eight figures that he did not understand, because they were so, and had underlined the one that frightened him; and Seln, who had read some thousands of returns, could not remember another that had been made by a person so little concerned with what his reader wished to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:79:He was walking a furlong ahead of the others, and he put his hand up to his coat as he did a dozen times in a day; and for once he noticed that the hand had two things to find. There was a case, locked, which he had carried shut through twelve years and three postings and across a Line. And there was a book, which he had carried shut for twelve days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:121:The first snow met them at the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:123:They had come down the south road since noon, past a well with a new rope on its windlass, under a sky the colour of a slate. Cael felt something touch his face as the meadow opened, and took it for ash from somebody's fire. Then there was another, on Lira's sleeve, which did not melt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:133:It was the fifth time that he had read that town, and the first from below. He had always had it from a saddle or a ridge, laid out like a board; now it stood over him. The river went by at his feet, dark, between two shelves of ice that had grown since he saw them last until each was as broad as a cart is long. The ferry's cable sagged across from bank to bank with a white rime on it. The scow lay on the near side, and a man with a mallet was knocking ice from its running blocks. In the doorway of the round shed a brown gelding with one white foot stood under a blanket and watched him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:135:Above the landing there was a long low building with a deep eave. The plank under the eave was bare of its book. There was the smith's chimney, and the taller house on the rise, with two. Along the water lay the meadow, where forty fires had burned in the dry months and twelve when he went south. He counted three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:143:"You've not got my rope," he said. It was the ferryman, and he did not say it as a complaint.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:155:He was right. The room was fuller than Cael had ever seen it, since the cold term had driven the country in off its roads. There were fifty people on those benches, and a fire on the hearth that a man could have stood up in. The hide-woman was there, and three grey heads over a pair of dice, and two carters with their boots to the blaze. At the last bench before the hearth sat a man with leather on his wrist, with four about him at their distances. One of the four was a lean old woman with a bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:173:*three hundred; taken; not paid*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:183:"It's first snow," said Pike. "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw. I've done forty today. This is the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:197:He took it by its corner and swung it on the bench, so that the writing faced the other way. Cael had read every line of that book upside down for a hundred and twenty days, across an oak plank, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter. It lay the right way up before him now. He read the season in a square black hand, and at the end of it a ruled space with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:211:By the fire one of the two carters had tipped his head back, to look at the sailcloth over Pike's bench. Five names stood there on the eleventh line, as they had for seventeen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:235:"Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the cold road," he said, to the fire, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "It's the long one, east, with the axe-head on its gate. He'd a day's posts still to set when he went off after you, and there's the whole far side to mend before the thaw. A hold will feed a crew that's mending its fence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:237:All four of them looked at Brom. He had asked a one-armed man once what a board paid somebody with no Path at all, and had carried the answer against his skin for seventy days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:3:They came to the fork two hours after sunrise on the hundred and twenty-second day, and the healer stopped the grey mule in the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:11:"It's sitting where she laid it. You'll not undo that knot, and you'll not have anybody undo it who hasn't forty cold terms of knots behind her. When you're down, you go to that woman before you go to your supper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:19:"All eight. They've taken no harm from a stair or a wall, which is more than you deserved of them." She drew the wool up and tied her own knot, high, with the hand at the collar-bone. "It holds nothing. I said first snow, near enough, and I'll say when; you'll not say it for me because a flake's come down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:23:Lira had the bow off her back. She held it out by the stock, with the one bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:29:She looked along the five of them then, once. Her sum ran to the oxen ford on the hundred and twenty-fifth, three weeks after it had looked for her; then she said the thing that she had stopped the mule to say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:31:"I'm three weeks late there, and three weeks is three weeks, and there's an old woman at Thornwater whose knee I said I'd see, and I said it after the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:49:She shifted the rope on her shoulder. She was looking up at the notch, and her face was as tired and as level as he had ever seen it; and whatever had given under her at a marsh's edge, three days before, had been put back where she kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:51:"Find me at Thornwater," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:There were four of them behind him, and one mule. They stood at the heap of stones as she had left them: a small man with a case on his chest, and a small woman with a hide under her arm, and a very big one bound up like a parcel, and a girl with a bow on her back and one sleeve empty. It was the crew that had come down off a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second day past the Line, and it looked a good deal the worse for wear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:81:It was the word that he gave at the end of every watch, and Cael had heard it some hundreds of times. He heard it seven times more before dark. Each time Karis nodded, and wrote the hour, and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:93:They slept on the ridge in a hollow, without wood, in every blanket that they had. Brom laid six bowls on the turf. He looked at the row, and took one back; and it passed without remark, since he had done exactly that on a hundred nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:149:She went down from the ruts by herself, on the whole of her foot, counting under her breath, and he counted with her. The line had lain forty paces inside the ruts at the Stair, and the ruts at the Fallow had kept forty yards. She passed forty with the iron still a long way below her. She passed a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:151:At a hundred and eighty-nine, or within a pace or two of it, she stopped one pace above the dark uprights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:155:"A hundred and ninety," said Karis, at the ruts. "Not forty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:169:These did not stand alone. Anchor had known it before his eyes did; it had asked at two bars with an old road between them, ten days ago, and here it asked at every pace, in its thin held note, as far as he could hear. There was a bar at his right knee, a hand's length clear of the turf, and another a pace beyond it, and another a pace beyond that. They went on so in both directions, one to a pace, round the head of that valley, for as far as he could see iron against the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:193:"Half the bend is twice the round. Four hundred. Near enough. Twice the radius. The same shape, at twice the size, round a thing I can't see."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:195:Her voice was quite steady. She opened her notebook against the lamp, and did the sum in it, with her lips moving. She had said two hundred of the Stair, by her eye, and had been twenty short and a stride; and he saw the figure that she wrote now, which was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from a line that she had not crossed to a middle that she had not seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:215:"There are forty bars to the left of me that I have counted, and a great many more that I have not," said Seln. "I should want to walk every one of them by daylight before I advised anybody to stand inside them. I do not think that we have the bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:*Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're oriented.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:3:He let Shadow go while he lay in the ditch, with his arm still round a bar of the registry's iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:15:He did it slowly, since he wished it seen. He set his boots down on the outside of a line that five people could not cross that morning, and turned his face up the old road to the brow, and stood there with his arms hanging. It was forty paces. In that light he was a dark upright thing against paler grass, and the man above him had hit a shoulder at five paces more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:19:He had priced this on the stair. There was one bolt in that groove, and when it was gone, a boot must go into a stirrup and a hook onto a string, and a back must straighten; he had watched that man do it once, from a frozen road, and it had taken him something over twenty counts. He had been told on a bluff never to spend the Wind near a thing whose single blow he could not price. He could price this one to a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:23:*Front. Back. Shoulders.* He loaded nothing on the third. He stood through it, with Wind lying quiet in his soles, and he watched a hooded shape forty paces up a hill. A girl had come down off a gate-walk eight days ago with a sore palm and three words, which a lean man had given her. Shooting was stopping. The stock on the brow settled into a shoulder. The shape behind it grew smaller, as a person does who is letting out his breath. And then it was entirely still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:35:He went up the slope on a slant, toward the old road, with the frozen grass breaking under him and forty paces of hill before him. He counted as he ran, and it was the bowman's count that he kept and not his own: at seven the hook was on the string, and at eleven the back was beginning to come up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:37:At twelve yards short of the brow he reached the lip of the old rut, and he gave up the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:39:It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half that had been under his soles since a stair went out from under them. He let Storm, the heaviest thing that he owned, have the air instead, and he laid the lane up the rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:41:It was a corridor as long as a cart and team, set down in that trough with its high end at his feet and its low end up the hill, and everything in it went one way at once. Frost went, and the seed that hung heavy on the grass at either lip, and chaff, and the dust of a hundred dry days; it went up that channel in a grey flood and burst out over the brow, twelve yards on, into two hoods.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:69:He did not let Anchor look. He had no need to; he had the hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:97:The west was the last. The sky there was green yet, low down, and the rim stood against it as black as a cut edge. A small upright shape had stood on that edge since first light and had never once loosed. It bent now, and laid something down. It walked away; and at the top of the rim, where another step would have taken it out of his sight, it stopped, and turned itself round, and looked back across five hundred yards of grass at a boy with a light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:101:Six. He counted them off that rim as Seln had counted them onto it. He let the fire go out, and walked down forty paces to the grey stone, and called one word down the old road into the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:157:He paid the rest of his own bill sitting against the old iron, with his knees drawn up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:197:*Anchor: looking, at the two bars, first light. I saw the fence go up. I stood two counts to see it, and Brom had those two counts with a bolt in him.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:199:*Shadow: once, from the fourth step to the old bar. Lira saw it go, from below. Seln turned his back. Three were round the door. The six were two hundred and sixty yards off, with a lip between; not one had a line to me. Let go in the rut. It holds. Dead to the elbows; I had to watch my own hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:203:*Anchor: looking, for the knot. I can't find what that costs. Entered as not found.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:205:*Anchor: the unbinding. Hand on it, from inside. Once.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:209:*Storm: one lane, up the old rut. Ten counts late.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:217:*Nine. With the reading on the road, ten since Thornwater. Deployment since the Line: twenty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:245:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:259:It had been the hundred and thirteenth morning, a day and a half before, when Teague took a slip that he did not want.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:273:"You've no road south. You've forty marks of fence east, with my capitals on it, and you've a day's posts still to set."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:293:Teague looked at him for some time. He had made it a rule, nine cold terms ago, that he would not walk three people up a road for a reason that he was ashamed to give them; and the rule held on a ridge at night as it held at a plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:307:They were at the weir by first light on the hundred and fourteenth, and the woman who kept the ford told him the news before he had asked for it. Five had come off the ridge the day before, at noon, with a mule; and the healer had gone up again with them, and had taken the mill's jenny, and had left word with a carter for a child at some gate to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:331:There was a light first. It was small, and low down, where the dark ground was; a point of fire that did not flicker as a brand flickers. Before he had fairly taken it in, there was a sound that he knew as well as his own name, since he had walked eleven cold terms beside a Wind, long ago, in another crew. That crack was a Wind's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:339:He stood on the ridge with the wind in his face and counted what he had. A Wind was one thing. An Ember, who could make fire stand on the air, was another, and he had worked beside two such; and whatever had gone up that ditch was a third. He had seen all three within the space of forty breaths, at one spot, and when he looked for the people who had done them he could find only one upright shape, very small, with its arm raised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:349:On the brow, where the ruts turned, two people had lain all day, and the frost showed him the two shapes, two yards apart, each a man's length. There was no ash of any fire, and nobody had dropped a crumb there, or a thread, or the paring of a nail. Beside the northern shape he found the round print of a stirrup pressed into the turf again and again in the one place. He counted, and there were eleven prints, and a twelfth half made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:3:It had been the afternoon of the hundred and nineteenth day when Karis sat down at the far fire with three clean leaves before her, and by the dusk of the hundred and twentieth she had risen from them only to sleep and to pace a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:7:She had never done such a thing in her life. A leaf was written once, and dated, and laid in its place, and the place was entered; that was what a box was for. But there were three boxes, and she had seen what a mountain could do to a cart. On the seventy-eighth day everything that the five of them owned had gone into a gorge except those three, and they had come off the hill only because a very large person went back for two of them, and a boy broke three ribs over the third. She had counted the loss that night, when she could count again. It had been blankets, and a kettle, and a rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:15:Then the walls, and here she was careful, since she had been greedy that morning and knew it. She had eleven sheets from the Fallow, where the bands ran round the feet of eleven stones, and twenty-nine from a room under the Stair. She had twelve from the hall. That made fifty-two, and the last twelve were all from one wall, rubbed upside down in an hour, by a person who was being held by the belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:17:She had gone through them twice by the fire, with the cup of barley at her knee. There were thirty-three kinds on those twelve sheets. Every one of the thirty-three was among her forty-one, and there was not a stroke on any sheet that was not. She entered it so: *one wall; thirty-three of the forty-one; none strange; a sample, and no more.* It would have been very easy to write a larger sentence, and she had struck larger sentences out of better people's work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:45:She understood that well enough. She had lived for years beside shelves that she was allowed to read at and forbidden to carry from. And he had been the keeper of one shut thing for twelve years; it was not to be supposed that he would take up a second and hold it open to everybody. He had come twenty yards across the turf with it, unasked, which was a great deal farther than she had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:61:Then the middles. She had stood on all three. One was the centre of a white floor with eleven stones about it, and one was a square hole with a stair in it, and one was a spring. She made a dot in each ring with the point of her pencil, as small as she could make it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:77:*The three middles lie on one straight edge, as near as this skin can show.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:It was entered. She sat and looked at what lay above it. There was a perimeter, three times, to the stride. There were forty-one kinds, and none strange. There was a line on a wall that was divided to a circle's radius, and stakes that were numbered in a row. And now there was a ruler lying through the three of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:91:She laid it out for them on the hundred and twenty-first morning, in daylight, on the lid of the middle box, with all of them looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:97:She showed them the three dots. Then she laid the brass down, and took her hands away from it, as she had at a grey stone six days before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:119:He went over it as he went over anything. There was meal for six days in the left-hand box, and a little bacon. There was a shoulder that was bound and would not be mended a third time, and an arm in a sling that was to hold nothing till the snow. The frost had come back in the night, harder than they had yet had it, and a healer had said at a stone that the snow would not be long. And there was a line. He had been walking toward something for a hundred and twenty days without knowing that it had a direction, and it had one now, and it went off the edge of a calfskin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:129:"I've a fence at forty with a day's posts to set," he said, "and a one-armed man to tell what I saw. I'll tell him what I saw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:145:"See that you do." She looked past him, at the hide. "There's a road a mile east of this water; I saw it from the rise. That quiet one says that it lies under your brass for a day, and that it forks. The left hand goes up through a gap, north and west, to Thornwater, in four of that carter's ticks." She took up the rope. "So I walk your road for a day, since it's mine as well. Where it forks, I'm the route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:149:Cael was the last to leave the grey stone. He looked down the ruts to the reeds, and along the pale straight path, to four walls standing in water with the morning in them. A thing lay under those walls with four bolts in it. It would go its round again, he supposed, once in a quarter of an hour, when it was able; and no carter would ever know that anybody had been in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:167:It came toward them a little after noon, behind a single mule, with a load of raw hides roped under a cloth and a smell going before it. The carter walked at the near wheel with a scarf over his nose. He looked at six people, and two beasts, and a sling, and an arm bound to a chest. He lifted one hand to them, as carters did on that side of the Line, and they lifted theirs, and nobody broke step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:169:There were two holds on that road, and both were shut. Each had its fence of split posts and its barred gate, and smoke going straight up from the long house; and at neither did anybody come to the gate-walk to see who went by. The cold term had come down on that country as a lid comes down on a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:The inn's keeper was a slow heavy man with a cloth over his shoulder, who had drawn their jugs for a hundred and twenty days and had never been asked for anything else. She told him what she wanted: a dry floor, and a door that shut, and a table that nobody ate at. It was for paper, she said. She would have it until the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:7:"There's the store behind the chimney," he said. "It's warm; it has the back of that fire for a wall. I keep meal in it." He looked at the three boxes, which Brom and Seln had brought in out of the snow. "Six, for the cold term. And you may lie at this end of the hearth for the standing-money, the five of you, as long as there's paper in my store."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:17:Brom set the kit on the table. Its case lay there in tarred canvas, with the strap coiled on the lid, as it had lain on a floor under the ground; inside were a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, and a mallet. The stake had never been driven. Beside it Karis put a dead man's ledger, shut. The three boxes stood against the warm wall, and each of them had one leaf in it that was the same as its fellows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:21:She laid them in three heaps along the deal, and did not try to make their edges agree; the Fallow's were long strips, and the Stair's were leaves, and the hall's were whatever a person could tear from a roll with her teeth. There were eleven in the first heap. There were twenty-nine in the second, and twelve in the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:25:"Fifty-two sheets. Forty-one kinds, at the two that I could do properly. Three perimeters of one size, and a fourth that is twice it." Karis leaned on the deal with both palms. "I had three boxes of other people's stone when I crossed the Line. I should not have said that there was anything east of it to put in them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:39:*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:47:The last ferry crossed at noon on the hundred and thirtieth day, with two fingers of snow on its rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:49:The ferryman had been at the shelf since first light with a pole and an axe, to cut her a lane. Half the meadow stood on the landing to see it. The scow went out along its cable slowly, shouldering the broken ice, with the gelding walking his round in the shed and the boy calling the turns. On the far bank a man in a hat with one side cut away stood waiting beside a mule, as he had stood at a ford on the fourth day past the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:55:"For the smith; from his brother, by the hand. For the smith." A folded sheet went along the oak. "For the house on the rise, a bill; they'll not want it said twice, and I'm saying it twice. For the landing: two, under his own seal, and I've not looked at them. For the landing, two." The broker's clerk took those. He did not meet anybody's eye. "For the woman with the hides, from a tanner. For the hold at the top of the north water. For Teague's old woman, from her daughter, and it's the first that ever came for her by this boat. For Thornwater, to go up tomorrow with the carter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:67:*Nineteen. I'm told it's late; the ferry's fault, not mine. Your grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. This one's true to a hair. Mind the shape. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:83:*Vell. There's a second ledger on the continent with my name in it, kept by a one-armed man in a town on no map, and this week he stopped numbering me. He said his book was wrong about us by being too small. You said once that somebody should have a record that doesn't answer to the Compact. There are two now. The session still stands. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:85:There was a third that he owed, to a friend on a bluff who lost to him every time he ran it. He had no line for that one yet. It would go by the first boat of the thaw, and he wrote in his own book that it was to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:87:He folded the two, and put no place on either, and gave them to the ferryman's boy with a copper apiece. The clerk's packet went into the same bag. Seln watched it go down the bank, with his hands behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:91:The scow went over once more, and came back empty. Then the ferryman and his boy took her lines in. They drew the hauling rope off the drum, fathom by fathom, stiff with ice, and coiled it in the shed; and the gelding was led away up the bank to the smith's stable with his blanket on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:99:It had been the hundred and twenty-second morning, eight days before, when Oryn left five people standing at a heap of chock-stones and took the left-hand road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:117:She had said the only true thing that she had. In eleven years she had put her hands into more bodies than she could number, the hurt and the whole and the dying, and every one of them had been all current, fast or slow, standing behind a break or running free. She had never met a still thing in a living person. The dying went still all at once, and everywhere. This was neither of those.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:123:*So are you.* It had come out of her crooked, and she had heard it come. She went over it now, strictly, with her knuckles ready. He was on the route. That was the whole of what the words said, and they were true of a miller's man and an old woman on a hurdle. She had gone into a dish of water after a shoulder, since she was nearest. He had gone into a hole after her. Neither of those had anything to say to a morning on a plank at Thornwater, when a boy lay still and let her spend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:133:She came down to Thornwater's eastern gate at dusk on the hundred and twenty-fifth day. The bar was drawn for her before she had called.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:147:There was no brown mule at the trough. She had known that there would not be, and she looked for it all the same, and was annoyed with herself. The back room had been swept. Her strip of canvas lay along the sill as she had left it, and somebody had put a cold fowl on the plank under a cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:151:She was a very small old woman. Oryn had mended that knee at a mill, thirteen days before, and had seen its owner go away up a valley on a hurdle between two grandsons. She came over the low place every year before the snow, to her daughter's hearth in this long house; and Oryn had told her, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee here, after the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:153:"I said after the thaw," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:161:Oryn got down on her heels in the frost, and put both hands round the joint. Her lips went to three. It was all there: the old banks, worn as smooth as a doorstep, and the current going through the place where a ladder had been as though no ladder had ever stood in it. She had promised that knee a look after the thaw, and it had walked over a hill to be looked at before the snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:181:It was the steading's woman who told her about the carter, on the evening of the hundred and twenty-eighth, with the sky gone the colour of a slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:191:She did not keep a book. She had said so to the pale one across a fire: a healer who needs a page to know who is owed has stopped looking at them. She had never in thirty rounds set down a word about any person whom she had had her hands in. But those five were two days south, with a saddle between that would be shut by morning, and she could not look at them. There was a girl down there with an arm in a knot of hers, who had been told *first snow*; and by the feel of that sky she would be looking at it out of a window tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:215:*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:219:The carter came over the saddle on the hundred and thirty-first morning, with a hand's depth of snow on the road and his eyebrows white, and he had a strip of bark for Pike and another that was not for Pike at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:251:"Someone made this pattern."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:263:Down the fire, the man who used to file reports about him watched the snow on the ferry landing with evident professional approval and said nothing, which — from him — was home.

