     1	# Chapter 59 — First Snow
     2	
     3	They turned for home on the hundred and twenty-fourth morning, and it took them six days.
     4	
     5	They did not go back as they had come. Seln had read the hide by Karis's lamp on the crest, and had found them a shorter way than a marsh and a stair: along the ridge to the heap of chock-stones, and then north by the hide carters' road, which ran through shut holds toward a river that all of them knew. He walked it ahead of them, as he walked everything. Four times in a day he came back along the file and said his word, and after the second day Karis left off writing down the hour.
     6	
     7	It grew colder with every morning. On the first the sky was clear, and the ruts rang. On the third there was a ring round the moon.
     8	
     9	"My father'd have had the horses in for that," said Brom. "He was wrong about a good deal. I never knew him wrong about a sky."
    10	
    11	"How long?" said Lira.
    12	
    13	"Three days. Four, if we're lucky, and we've not been."
    14	
    15	On the fourth the wind went into the north and stayed there, and they walked with their chins in their collars and the mule's breath freezing on her whiskers.
    16	
    17	To Cael, who had learned that crew's sound on a hundred roads and had learned it again at a fork with a pair of boots gone out of it, five had already begun to seem the number that a road was walked by. He did not care for that, and he wrote it down, so that he should be held to noticing it.
    18	
    19	His ribs kept the count for him.
    20	
    21	He could not lie on them, and he slept sitting, against a box or a wall, and woke at every turn of the watch. They were no worse on the road. They only spoke; and he counted a healer's ten days off against them each night, as a man counts off a sentence.
    22	
    23	Lira walked in front of him, and he watched her do it for six days.
    24	
    25	The foot was taped, from a roll that had been put into her hand at a fork, without a word, by a person who had then walked north; and she wound it herself each morning, sitting on a box, with her one hand and her teeth. Then she set the great boot down whole at every stride of every mile, on ruts that were iron and on turf that was glass.
    26	
    27	It went from under her on the fourth day, on a sheet of ice where a spring had crossed the road.
    28	
    29	He was three strides behind. He saw the boot go, and her right arm fly out; and he saw the left, under the grey wool, start to come and stop at a knot. She went down on her right shoulder and her hip, hard, with the bow clattering on her back. She lay for one breath with her cheek on the ice.
    30	
    31	Brom had the mule's rope in his good hand, and he did not let go of it. Not one of them went to lift her, since each of them knew what she would say to a hand under that arm.
    32	
    33	She got up by herself, on the right hand and both knees, as she climbed a ladder.
    34	
    35	"It held nothing," she said.
    36	
    37	"I saw," said Brom.
    38	
    39	"I'm saying it for her. Somebody's to tell her that it held nothing, and I'd as soon there were two of us who'd seen."
    40	
    41	She walked on, with the foot set down whole. Karis offered her the mule's back that evening, in a roundabout fashion, by remarking that three boxes might as well be carried by four people for a mile or two.
    42	
    43	"I rode a mule off a mountain once, for that," said Lira. "I'll walk home on it."
    44	
    45	Nobody at that fire said what *it* was, and Cael noticed that none of them had, in six days. They spoke of the foot, and the boot, and the tape; and the name of the joint itself went all the way from a fence of iron to a river without being spoken by anybody.
    46	
    47	He woke on the hundred and twenty-ninth morning lying on his left side.
    48	
    49	He had rolled onto it in his sleep, and it had let him. He lay and breathed, slowly, to the bottom of his chest, and nothing answered. It was ten days to the morning, as she had said.
    50	
    51	---
    52	
    53	On the hundred and twenty-fourth night, the first of the road home, Seln read a page and a half that he had by heart already.
    54	
    55	He read it on his watch, as he had on every night since a room under a floor. He sat with his back to four sleepers and the limp little book open on his knee, tilted to what the embers gave. He did not need the light. He used it regardless, since there is a difference between remembering a hand and looking at it.
    56	
    57	It was a small hand, and upright. It pressed. It ruled no margin of its own, having been given a ruled leaf, and it kept inside the blue lines as a careful man keeps to a path. He knew by the slope of the fours that their writer had stood to write them, with the book against his thigh. He knew that the man had been surprised at the eighth of eight figures, since he had gone back and underlined it; and that he had been more than surprised at the line below, which he had gone over twice until the pencil cut.
    58	
    59	Seln had no more of him than that. There was no name in the book, and he had not looked for one; a field ledger is signed for at a counter, and the name stays at the counter.
    60	
    61	He had wanted a face, and had gone up a ridge for one, and had been given six hoods. This was the nearest that he had come. It was a person's own book. In twelve years of that matter he had handled a great deal of paper. Every leaf of it had been written by a clerk, at dictation, for somebody up a stair. Nobody had dictated this. A man had gone alone into a hole in the ground with a cord and a mallet, and had put down what he found there, for himself and for no reader, in the order in which he found it, and had been astonished.
    62	
    63	And then somebody had left him there.
    64	
    65	Seln knew that counter. He had stood at one like it in his first posting, and had been given a satchel and four keys and a sheet to put his name to. The storeman had not looked up. *Returned or accounted for,* the sheet said, above the place for the name. He had thought nothing of it in his first year. It had not come into his head that there was a third thing which could happen to a man and his issue, and that the sheet had no line for it.
    66	
    67	He turned to the second page, which had one line on it and a stroke that went off toward the edge.
    68	
    69	He had sat in that service for fifteen years. He knew what it did with its instruments when they were done with, and he had supposed that he knew the worst of it. He had not known that it would write *abandoned* on a sheet of cream paper, about a kit that was still lying where its owner knelt, and offer three hundred marks for it as a hook to hang five people on. The man with the upright hand had been a clerk of a kind. He had written true lines at somebody's bidding and had not been told what they were for.
    70	
    71	He had written *nothing to report* at the foot of a weekly return for twelve years, over the top of a case, and had thought it the hardest sentence in the service to write honestly. This man had written a better one. He had written what was there. He had put down eight figures that he did not understand, because they were so, and had underlined the one that frightened him; and Seln, who had read some thousands of returns, could not remember another that had been made by a person so little concerned with what his reader wished to hear.
    72	
    73	He shut the book, and put it back inside his coat, against the strap.
    74	
    75	It lay where it had lain for nine days, on his breastbone, beside the other thing; and he sat for some time with his hand over the pair of them.
    76	
    77	It was three days later, on the fourth of that road, with the wind gone into the north, that he saw what he had been doing.
    78	
    79	He was walking a furlong ahead of the others, and he put his hand up to his coat as he did a dozen times in a day; and for once he noticed that the hand had two things to find. There was a case, locked, which he had carried shut through twelve years and three postings and across a Line. And there was a book, which he had carried shut for twelve days.
    80	
    81	He had not meant to keep it so. He had put it into his coat in a cold room because it was registry paper. Reading registry paper was the one craft that he had brought east with him. That had been true. It was also true that he had read it to nobody since. A researcher had wanted one figure out of it, at a fire by a marsh, and had not felt able to ask. She had stopped with her pencil over a leaf, and looked at her own cuff, where the figure had been washed out. He had gone to her. He had held the page to the light for her three times with his fingers shut on its spine.
    82	
    83	He had seen people stand so before him for fifteen years, on the other side of a counter.
    84	
    85	He went over it as he would have gone over a return. The case was shut for cause. There were names in it, and what was done to their owners; and there was a leaf in it that was no copy. It would be opened on a day, before the right people, or it would burn those whom it was kept for. He did not doubt that. He had audited it on a great many watches.
    86	
    87	The book was another matter. It was a page and a half of distances, set down honestly by a man with a knotted cord, and a line that stopped. A person at the back of this file needed it every evening of her life; and she kept a box in which, by her own rule, nothing was written of anybody that he had not heard to his face.
    88	
    89	He had spent fifteen years learning to keep things from being read. He could think of no worse shelf for a dead man's true figures than the coat of such a person.
    90	
    91	He stopped in the road, and waited for the four of them to come up.
    92	
    93	The big one went by with the mule, and the girl, on her whole foot, and the boy behind her with his arms wrapped about his ribs. The small one came last. She had the hide under one arm and her notebook in the other hand, and she was doing a sum as she walked, with her lips.
    94	
    95	He took the book out of his coat, and held it toward her, shut.
    96	
    97	She stopped. She looked at it, and then at him; and she put up her hand beside it, open, for him to lay the page against, as he had at the fire.
    98	
    99	"Which figure?" she said.
   100	
   101	"All of them. It is a field book. It ought to lie with the papers of the field."
   102	
   103	He put it into her hand, and took his own away.
   104	
   105	It was an odd thing to feel a weight leave his fingers that he had not been asked for. She did not move for a breath. Then she shut her hand on it, and held it against her coat as she had once held a flake from the footing of a city's gate.
   106	
   107	"I shall enter where it was found, and when," said Karis, "and by whose hand it came to me."
   108	
   109	"Enter the first two. The third is not a finding."
   110	
   111	"It is a provenance. I enter those."
   112	
   113	He could not dispute that, and he did not try. She had the book open before he had turned to go on; she walked the next mile with her nose a hand from a dead man's pencil, and went into a rut to the knee, and did not appear to notice.
   114	
   115	He took the hide from under her arm, so that she should have both hands, and carried it for her until dusk.
   116	
   117	His own hand went to his coat that evening, on watch, and found a strap, and the corner of a lock, and nothing beside them. He let it lie there. He had thought that he might want the thing back by nightfall, as a man wants a tooth that has been drawn. He left it where he had put it, that night and the next.
   118	
   119	---
   120	
   121	The first snow met them at the river.
   122	
   123	They had come down the south road since noon, past a well with a new rope on its windlass, under a sky the colour of a slate. Cael felt something touch his face as the meadow opened, and took it for ash from somebody's fire. Then there was another, on Lira's sleeve, which did not melt.
   124	
   125	"That's it," she said. She had stopped. She was looking at the grey wool over her left arm, and at a small white star lying on it. "That's snow."
   126	
   127	"It is a flake," said Karis. "She said that you were not to call it for her on a flake."
   128	
   129	"I'm not calling it. I'm looking at it."
   130	
   131	It came on as they went down, thinly, out of no wind at all. Cael walked through the meadow to the river's edge, to the planks of the landing, and stopped there and counted what he had.
   132	
   133	It was the fifth time that he had read that town, and the first from below. He had always had it from a saddle or a ridge, laid out like a board; now it stood over him. The river went by at his feet, dark, between two shelves of ice that had grown since he saw them last until each was as broad as a cart is long. The ferry's cable sagged across from bank to bank with a white rime on it. The scow lay on the near side, and a man with a mallet was knocking ice from its running blocks. In the doorway of the round shed a brown gelding with one white foot stood under a blanket and watched him do it.
   134	
   135	Above the landing there was a long low building with a deep eave. The plank under the eave was bare of its book. There was the smith's chimney, and the taller house on the rise, with two. Along the water lay the meadow, where forty fires had burned in the dry months and twelve when he went south. He counted three.
   136	
   137	The snow was coming down on all of it alike, and on five people and a mule.
   138	
   139	He noticed where he had begun. He had started at the eave, and gone on from there. It was how a person counts a place when he knows already which roof he is going to sleep under.
   140	
   141	The man with the mallet had straightened his back.
   142	
   143	"You've not got my rope," he said. It was the ferryman, and he did not say it as a complaint.
   144	
   145	"No. We've not forgotten it."
   146	
   147	"Nor have I. Sixty yards, four-strand." He knocked a last cake of ice into the river. "I'll not want it this side of the thaw. There's one more crossing in her, tomorrow, if that shelf lets me; the post comes down to the far bank for it. Then I take my lines in."
   148	
   149	"There's a light in it," said Brom, of the long low building. "And I can smell his fire. I'd like to be told that I'm allowed to stop walking."
   150	
   151	"You were to see a woman about a knot first," said Lira.
   152	
   153	"She'll be in there. They're all in there. There's nowhere else to be."
   154	
   155	He was right. The room was fuller than Cael had ever seen it, since the cold term had driven the country in off its roads. There were fifty people on those benches, and a fire on the hearth that a man could have stood up in. The hide-woman was there, and three grey heads over a pair of dice, and two carters with their boots to the blaze. At the last bench before the hearth sat a man with leather on his wrist, with four about him at their distances. One of the four was a lean old woman with a bowl.
   156	
   157	Pike was under his sailcloth. His eyes came up as the cold came in, and went over the five in the doorway, and went over them again.
   158	
   159	"Shut that," he said. "You're letting my heat out. Then come here and tell me whether I've got it wrong."
   160	
   161	He had the ledger open already at their heading. He turned his shoulder to the room and gave out a line that nobody had yet heard, in the voice that he used under his eave. It was a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting. It was taken. Six had gone in. There were instruments found there, and brought up, and kept by the crew that found them; and six had come out.
   162	
   163	"Is that wrong anywhere?"
   164	
   165	"No," said Cael.
   166	
   167	"His clerk's been up my bank four times to ask what he's to put a mark to."
   168	
   169	"Nothing. They're staying with us."
   170	
   171	"So I'd heard." Pike dipped his pen, and wrote, and blew on it. In the last column, where a price went, he set down three things with a stop between each, and read them out as he made them.
   172	
   173	*three hundred; taken; not paid*
   174	
   175	He said the whole of it over, to the room. Then he counted down the leaf with the end of his pen.
   176	
   177	"That's nine."
   178	
   179	---
   180	
   181	He did not shut the book.
   182	
   183	"It's first snow," said Pike. "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw. I've done forty today. This is the last."
   184	
   185	He turned his shoulder again. Cael saw the hide-woman put down her cup.
   186	
   187	"*Crew of six. Ford cleared, traverse mapped, Ring entered, Stair entered and instruments recovered, Hall entered. No losses. Returned every time.*"
   188	
   189	It lay on that hot room like the first of the snow. There was not a carter there who did not know those three places by the tales that were told of them. Cael heard a bench creak, and a die roll off a board and nobody stoop for it.
   190	
   191	Pike had his pen over the end of the line, where a figure went.
   192	
   193	It stayed there, and he did not bring it down. He looked at the place for as long as it takes to count ten, with his one hand quite still; and then he laid the pen in its groove and did a thing that Cael had never once seen him do.
   194	
   195	He turned the ledger round.
   196	
   197	He took it by its corner and swung it on the bench, so that the writing faced the other way. Cael had read every line of that book upside down for a hundred and twenty days, across an oak plank, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter. It lay the right way up before him now. He read the season in a square black hand, and at the end of it a ruled space with nothing in it.
   198	
   199	"Teague says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small."
   200	
   201	He did not say it sadly. He said it as he had once said what a Bronze was worth who lost a carter: as arithmetic.
   202	
   203	"You've left it empty," said Lira.
   204	
   205	"I have. I'd put a figure there for any crew on that cloth, and be within a line of right. I'll not put one where I know that it's wrong." He looked at the space. "It can stand till I've a column. I told the girl so, the night that you went south."
   206	
   207	Then he shut the book on the empty place, and put his stone on it.
   208	
   209	Cael stood before a hearth with snow melting on his shoulders. A magister had written of him once that no instrument would see his like. He had meant it kindly, and had entered it in a daybook that Cael was not let to carry away. This man had turned his own book about on a bench, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the hole in it.
   210	
   211	By the fire one of the two carters had tipped his head back, to look at the sailcloth over Pike's bench. Five names stood there on the eleventh line, as they had for seventeen days.
   212	
   213	"Eleventh," he said.
   214	
   215	"Aye," said the other, and spat into the ashes.
   216	
   217	Somebody laughed, a long way down the room, and it was not an unkind laugh.
   218	
   219	The man with the leather on his wrist had got to his feet. He came down between the benches without haste, and stopped a stride from Cael, with his thumbs in his belt over the hand-axe.
   220	
   221	"After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes."
   222	
   223	It was not an offer, and it was not a question. He had seen a strip of brass run off the edge of a calfskin, and had asked nothing about it, and had walked five days home to say this.
   224	
   225	"Noted," Cael said.
   226	
   227	It was the third time that he had given that man the word. Teague nodded, by the width of a finger, and went back to his bench. The boy who sat there had stopped with a heel of bread half way to his mouth. He did not start again until his captain was down.
   228	
   229	Brom had not waited for any of it. He had gone straight down the room to the lean old woman, as he was told at a fork. He stood in front of her like a boy with a torn coat. She did not get up. She looked at four yards of webbing, once, from the top turn to the bottom, and at how he held the arm inside it; then she went back to her bowl.
   230	
   231	"It'll do," she said.
   232	
   233	Pike had heard, and he scratched the stump of his arm through its pinned sleeve.
   234	
   235	"Fence. Forty. Teague says it's yours if you want it; he's got the cold road," he said, to the fire, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "It's the long one, east, with the axe-head on its gate. He'd a day's posts still to set when he went off after you, and there's the whole far side to mend before the thaw. A hold will feed a crew that's mending its fence."
   236	
   237	All four of them looked at Brom. He had asked a one-armed man once what a board paid somebody with no Path at all, and had carried the answer against his skin for seventy days.
   238	
   239	"Ours," said Brom.

