editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-127-"She looked at your hand before she said it," Karis murmured. Her voice was pitched for his ear alone. "And then at her own wrist. I have seen that look on a person in front of a shut press. She was curious about you. Enter that somewhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-129-"It's not mine to enter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-131-"It is an observation. You may put my initial against it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:133:He put it down, with her initial; and he had hardly lifted the pencil when a hoarse voice from the lean-to said that the arm had taken no harm on two days of frozen road, that all eight were as they had been, and that the knot had been retied by somebody who tied like a carter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-135-"That was me," said Brom, from the bench. "At the ford. She'd got it wet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-137-"I can see who it was. Hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-138-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-143----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-145-Cael told her, since it was his to tell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:147:He gave it in order and left nothing out: a line that was said twice at a hearth, and a nail, and a sheet of cream paper that had lain sealed in a broker's box beside the first. He gave her the sum on it. He told her what Seln had found in its margin, and what the five of them had made of it at a fire, and that one of the five had said walk away and been entered. He said that the place was half a day up the ridge from the fork over her head, and that he meant to be at its edge by dusk and across it in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-149-"You said not without you. So I've not come to ask you for anything. I've come so that you should have it from me today, with the bench done, and not from a carter in a fortnight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-151-She heard all of it with her eyes on her own hands. When he had finished she spread the left one on her knee, and began to fold the fingers down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-152-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-201-"I'm not coming with you. I've put you on the round. That's a different thing, and I'll not have it mistaken." Her eyes went along the five of them, and they were as level and as tired as he had ever seen them. "You're on the route now. Which means when I say lie still, you lie still, and when I say I can't help you, you believe me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-203-"That's the whole of it?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-204-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:205:"That's what every gate gets. They feed me, and they do as they're told on my plank, and when I tell them that a thing's past my hands they don't stand there looking at me as if I'd kept something back." She glanced at Cael. "You've stood there so. On a hillside, with her arm in my lap. I'll not have it a second time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-206-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-207-"You won't," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-208-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-209-"And I go when I go. The day that the round can't spare me, I turn north, and nobody says a word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-210-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-240-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-241-They forded below the mill, and the woman on the flat stone counted six of them and eight hooves across without lifting her net; and they climbed out of the green valley into the wind again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-242-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-243-At the fork there was a flat rock, and Cael sat down on it with Hesk's book while the two mules were let to blow. The grey had come up the notch backward for a good part of the way. The mill's wheel was still to be heard below them, very small. He wrote under what he had written on a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-244-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:245:*Hundred and thirteenth day, the fork above Millrace. Oryn came. On her terms. She'll turn north the day she has to and I won't ask her not to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-246-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md-247-He read it over. Then he looked at six people on a bare ridge, and at the left-hand ruts, which ran on to the south with the white sun over them.
