editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-5-Karis took her hands away from it. It was a strip no longer than her forearm, and it had come from a shelf at Ternhall, in the bottom of a box, across a Line and a mountain with a herd on it. It lay now on a carter's hide, on the lid of that same box. Its upper end rested on the ring that she had walked on the ninety-sixth day, and its edge passed through the middle of the ring that she had walked that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-7-"It doesn't reach," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:9:"It need not." Karis set the point of her pencil against the brass at its lower end, and made a dot on the skin; then she slid the strip down along itself with two fingers, a hand's length, until the dot and the lower ring both lay against it again. "A ruler is as long as its owner is patient."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-11-She did it three times. Cael watched the bright edge go down the hide, past the square beyond the ridge that was a mill, and out over the place where the carter's hill-hatching thinned. All six of them had their heads over the box by then, with the healer a stride and a half behind the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-13-At the fourth sliding the brass ran in among the strokes of his water.
