editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-314-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-315-The darkness rose at the plate's edge, and the plate did not tip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-316-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-317-He felt the thing get its head under, and heave; and a slab of grey that was only lying on the mud stayed where it lay as if it had grown there. The heave came again, harder. Then the two halves opened over the plate's edge, in a rush of water, and closed on the place where his palm had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-318-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:319:It was like having a tooth drawn. Every other fragment that he owned went thin and far away, Wind and Storm and the tenth together, and the cold of the water, and the kerb under his knees; there was only the one point, and something very strong that wished to take it up, and himself saying that it should stay. The jaws came down a second time, and a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-320-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-321-"Thirty," said a small voice, a long way off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-322-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-323-He had his teeth shut. Through them he heard Karis, who could not help observing a thing even then.
