--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-328-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-329-The thing did not go away. All round the held plate the others were lifting now, in a ring, and staying lifted; it had brought the whole of its length to that one place, for the first time in an afternoon, and laid itself about the only weight on its floor that would not come up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-330-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-331-"Sixty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-332-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:333:He let go. There was no point, and the jaws shut on water. The plate tipped at last, with nothing on it, and fell back; and for as long as it takes to count five the ring of lifted grey stayed as it was, like a dog standing over a hole. Then it sank, plate by plate, and the knocking went away round the dish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-334-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-335-He was on his hands and knees, shaking, and Brom's left fist was in his collar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-336-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md-337-"I can't count you here," said Oryn, from her stride and a half, "and I couldn't on the far side of that stone either. Can you stand?"
