editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-258-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-259-On that same night, the hundred and twelfth, Seln had the second watch, and he spent it reading two papers that were no longer in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-260-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-261-He needed no light for it, and he sat against the ash with his knees drawn up and the case on his chest, under his coat, where it had hung on every road past the Line; and the two sheets lay side by side in his head as plainly as they had lain on his knee. He could have counted the holes in either margin, and indeed he had done so, in the room, while a one-armed man was speaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-262-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:263:The frost was coming down, and he could hear it, very faintly, in the grass. Four people slept behind him, and a mule stood over three boxes; and at eleven other fires in that meadow there was nobody awake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-265-He began with what he knew, which was the clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-266-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-267-He had never met the man. He knew that he was no longer young, since his capitals were of a pattern that had not been taught for twenty years; that he ruled his own sheets and had been given a damaged wheel to do it with; and that he had been told what to write and nothing about why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-268-
