editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-290-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-291-Now there was a fifth grade, which nobody had taught him, and he had held it in his two hands tonight. It was a lure, and a lure was a different kind of thing from the other four. The record could be kept by nobody in particular, and the instrument read by any clerk; the knock came through a door and the hand out of a ditch. But a lure that is taken must be met. Somebody had to be there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-292-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-293-*People have faces.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-294-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:295:He had said it at a fire, to four people, in his own voice, and he was a little astonished at himself still. He went over it now as he would have gone over a return before it was sealed, to see whether it was true or only well put.
