editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md-299-It was not revenge, though he looked for that first, since it was the commonest thing. He turned out every pocket of it, and found that he did not want anybody on that rim hurt, and had no wish to see a hood pulled back by force. He had spent too long being the quiet man at the edge of a room to suppose that the people who would wait at the foot of that ridge were anything but quiet men at the edge of somebody else's room, sent by a stair that they had never climbed.
