editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md-209-Cael stood before a hearth with snow melting on his shoulders. A magister had written of him once that no instrument would see his like. He had meant it kindly, and had entered it in a daybook that Cael was not let to carry away. This man had turned his own book about on a bench, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the hole in it.
