editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-7-"Down. On the left side. Somebody get that coat off him, and somebody light me a fire, and I don't care which of you does which."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:9:It was Karis who lit it. She had come over the stone with a morning's pencil in her arms and her cheek bleeding, and she put the sheets into Lira's lap and held out her right hand; and a point stood on the kindling that Seln had laid ready before first light. It was the first thing that any of them had declared in a day, and Cael saw her look at her own palm afterward, as she had looked at it outside another stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-11-The coat came away from Brom's shoulder with a sound that he did not care for, and under it the binding hung in a loop, and the linen beneath was black.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-13-Oryn knelt, wet to the waist, with the wet stiffening on her as the frost found it, and she did not seem to know. She laid one hand on the front of the shoulder and one behind, and shut her eyes, and her lips reached three.
