editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-50-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-51-He sat with his back against a bar that the registry had driven five cold terms ago, and his legs straight out before him, as wet to the waist as the healer was. The fire was three yards off, and he knew that he ought to go to it; he did not seem to have the means, and so he went over his bill instead, since that could be done sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-52-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-53-The ribs came first, low on the left, the same three, and they hurt a good deal less than they had on a mountain; they were simply *there*, at the top of every breath, like a hand laid on him. Something a great deal heavier than he was had come up under him, and he had sent the whole of it through his own frame into a ledge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:55:There was the other thing, for which he had no good word. He had held a point against a pair of jaws while four bolts went in, and it had taken everything else that he owned to do it; not one of the ten fragments had yet come back to where it lived, and he felt like a house from which the furniture has been carried out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-57-And his eyes would not hold a near thing. That was the read, which he had leaned on harder than ever in his life, through water and stone, and when he looked at his own hand it swam.
