editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-114-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-115-She was as wet down the front as a person can be who has lain for an hour on her stomach on a stone with a spring slopping over it. Cael saw her shiver, from the near fire, and saw that she did not know she was doing it; she had her notebook open and three clean leaves before her, and her pencil had already begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-116-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-117-When he looked again, there was a blanket round her shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:119:It was a grey one, with a darn at the corner, and he knew whose it was. He turned his head. Seln was on the far side of both fires, by the mules, laying a stem of grass under a knot with his back to everything; and nobody had seen him cross twenty yards of open turf and come back. Karis drew the blanket closer with one hand, without lifting her eyes from the leaf, as a person pulls up a cover in her sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-120-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-121-Lira had not left the grey stone. She sat on it with her foot set down whole and the healer's bow along her knee, with its nose toward the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-123-"You could let the string down," said Cael. "It's been bent since first light."
