editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-129-"I know what he read. I watched it come up out of a hole this morning, the whole of it, when a carter had said that nobody ever saw so much." Her right hand moved on the stock, a finger's breadth, and lay still. "I'm not saying that it's coming. I'm saying that I've one, and it's in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-131-He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:133:It was the middle of the afternoon when he saw the other thing, and he saw it only because Brom was making a face at him. Brom lay on his back by the near fire, under two coats, with his right arm strapped to him in every strip of linen that the pannier held. He had his left forefinger laid across his lips, and with his eyes he was pointing at what sat beside him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-135-The healer was asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-137-She had been counting his wrist every quarter of an hour, by whatever clock she carried, and she had sat down to do it once more with her legs crossed and his hand in her lap. She had got as far as closing her fingers. Her chin was on her chest, and her back had gone round, and the blanket had slipped off one shoulder; she was breathing through her mouth like a child.
