editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-193-That was a fourth thing, and he could count it: nothing in him had ever met that place as a new thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-195-He thought of the three floors, since he could not help it. An edge lay round each of them, out on the grass, where anybody could set a stone; at that line five people's Arbiters stopped, and his ten fragments went by as though no line were there. This was of another kind. It lay inside him, and it put nothing out, and not one of the ten would cross it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:197:There was a leaf in the old volume of Hesk's book with a session's number at its head. He had written a label on that leaf, a long time ago, on a bluff, for a thing that he could not see; it had been a frown on a big man's face then, and a line in an inventory, and he had called it by those few words in pencil ever since, for want of anything to point at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:199:He had something to point at now. The old words went onto it, and fitted, as a name fits a person whom one has known for years only by letters.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-201-He took his hands from himself, and the going-along came home and lay down. Then he sat up, with his ribs speaking, and wrote by the embers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-203-*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md-204-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-1-# Chapter 45 — Eleventh
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:3:The bar came out of Thornwater's western gate on the hundred and eleventh morning for five people, three boxes and a mule that belonged to none of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:Brom had loaded her by the well, in the grey, with the steading's woman holding the lantern. Two boxes hung from the pack-saddle on their own ropes, one to a side, and the third rode between them on a folded blanket with the empty pannier strapped over it. He had gone round the animal three times, pulling at each knot. Then he had stood back and looked at the load as he would have looked at a wheel that he had built himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-7-"She'll want to go on the low side of you," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-9-"On the low side of nothing. I go on the low side of her." He clicked his tongue as he had been shown, and the small brown ears came round. "I was told it once. I'd be ashamed to be told it twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-11-It was the hardest cold that they had yet walked in. The ruts of the valley road had frozen as the last grain cart left them, with every hoof-mark standing up like a thing cut in iron, and the pale grass on either hand was white to the knee. Their breath went ahead of them. On the gate-walk a lean man stood with his long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every morning that they had spent behind that fence; and when Lira looked up, he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had nothing in her right hand, and she lifted two of her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-13-They went down the stretch at the one time of day when nothing had ever come up it. Cael walked last. He had the white stone on his left hand at a hundred and ten yards, and he gave it the look that he gave it every time. Fifty-seven evenings had gone by since anything stood there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:15:Lira was in front of him, and he watched how she went. The ankle was taped, and under a healer's order still, so that she set the great boot down whole at every stride, heel and toe together, and rolled nowhere. She had done it for fifteen days on turf. On frozen ruts it was a different trade. Twice in the first mile the boot skated, and twice her right arm went out and her left stayed where a knot held it; she had been told that she was to fall on her face sooner than put that hand out, and he saw that she had taught the hand to believe it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-17-"I can hear you counting," she said, without turning her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-19-"I'm not counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-20-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-49-It was the same thing that she had stopped at the well. He wished to have three readings done in three nights, so that he could set them down at her first bench and stand square. She had told him to read it frightened, and to read it when he was not. She had said nothing about reading it in a hurry; that was a third temper, and it was the one that he had been in when he lay down on her table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-50-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-51-He took his fingers off his wrist. The listening went on at the edge of his thought, quick and slow, and he let it go on, as a miller lets a mill; he would read himself on a night that was only a night, and until then the debt could stand where she had put it. It was the first that he had ever left standing without picking at it. He wrote one line, and gave the watch to Brom at the turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-52-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:53:*Hundred and eleventh day. The halting-ground above the wash. One reading done, two owed, and nothing tried tonight; I was in a hurry, and she never asked for that. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-55----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-57-They came over the saddle above Lowmarch in the afternoon of the hundred and twelfth day, and he stopped on the crown of it and counted what was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-58-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-93-They went down into the meadow with the light going. The old place under the ash was empty, since a good many places were, and Brom unloaded there and rubbed the mule's back with a twist of hay while Seln walked once round the twelve fires and came back and said that there was nothing. A boy came for the standing-money. Karis gave him two coppers, which were too small a matter for her book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-95-The plank under the lower inn's eave was bare of its book. Pike had taken it in for the night, as he did when the dusk came early; but a line of yellow showed under the inn's door, and the noise of the room came through the wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:97:"He's not said ours," said Brom. "The Fallow. He wrote it eleven days ago, and sent it up to us on a piece of bark, and it's not been said where we could hear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-99-"Then he'll say it," said Karis. "He has never yet kept one back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-100-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-101-Cael put his hand to the latch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-102-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-103----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-104-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-105-Pike said it before the door was shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-106-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:107:He was on the hearth bench, under the sailcloth, with the ledger open beside him and the horn pot at his elbow; and his eyes came up as the cold came in. His look went over the five in the doorway once, and then a second time, as it went over everything. Then he turned his shoulder to the room and gave it out, in the voice that he used under his eave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-109-It was the eighth line under their heading. Cael had read it on a scrap of bark in a grain hold, across the bones of a goat, and he knew every word; but he had never heard it said. Pike gave the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the broker's posting. He said that it was taken and witnessed. He said what had not been found there, and that the crew had come back. He gave the count of those who went in and of those who came out, which was the same count, and at the end of it he said that no price was paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-110-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-111-Then he said the whole of it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-112-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-125-Not one of them spoke. Cael had been looked at in a great many rooms, and he knew the difference between a room that has made up its mind about a person and a room that has had to change it. This one was changing it, a face at a time, with no word said. It did not seem to be making it up again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-127-At the far end, on the last bench before the hearth, a man had got to his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:129:He had done it without haste. He was of the middle height and heavy in the forearm, with a band of black leather laced on his right wrist. Three people sat behind him at their distances. He stood with his thumbs in his belt and his eyes on Cael, and he stayed on his feet while the line was said the second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-131-Cael had seen that man count three on a sill before he looked at a room. He had been told by him on a river bank what a mountain was worth. He had never once seen him stand up for anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:Pike had dipped his pen. He did not write with it. The line had been in his book for eleven days. He raised his eyes to the sailcloth over his head, and found the place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-135-"Eleventh. Teague's ten up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-137-The man with the leather on his wrist sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-138-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-141----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-143-"Eleventh," she said, in front of the cloth. "We were tenth when we went up that road."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:145:She had gone straight to it, on her whole sole, and stood with her nose a hand from the square black writing. Cael came up beside her. The five names were where she had said, on the eleventh line; and over them, where there had been two crews under theirs, there was one that had been written back above.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-147-"It's the wooden foot," said Lira. "The four who never lost a mule. They've gone over us again." She turned on the hearth bench. "We went into a place that you've never stepped over the edge of, and six of us came out. You've just said so twice. And it's cost us a line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-149-"It has."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-150-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-159-"Then make one," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md-160-
