editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-214-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-215-At the fourth from the top he stopped. The square was over his head, and it was the colour of slate; he could see one star in it. Below him the stair went down into black, and he knew that a girl with one arm was looking up it at his back. He knew that Seln had turned round in the doorway and was facing the room, which was his way of saying that he had seen the thing before and did not need to see it again. Three more people were beyond the corner of that door, and could have seen nothing if they had tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-216-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-217-Above him there was the lip of the floor, and a great deal of open sky. He was a yard below the level of the stone, and no person on that rim could have seen the top of his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-218-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:219:It was dusk. Shadow had been lifting in him for an hour, as it lifted at every dusk, like a tide coming up a beach. He had told it no on the fifth evening past the Line, and at a ford with the light going, and on a gate-walk; he had refused it on something over a hundred evenings, and let it have him once, for ten strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-220-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-221-He did not tell it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-222-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-223-The sound of his own breath stopped. That was how it came: the world went on exactly as before, and he went out of it. He lifted his boot to the third step and set it down, and no stone answered, and he could not hear his coat. Far below him he heard a small sharp breath that was Lira's, since she had been looking at a boy on a stair, and now she was looking at a stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-224-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-256-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-257-Cael walked on, and he could not feel his arms below the elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-258-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-259----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-260-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:261:At fourteen feet from the newer bar there was nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-262-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-263-He had known that there would not be. He could see the iron itself, a dark stroke above the frosted turf, and the grey stone beyond it; and the air between that bar and its fellow was as empty as any air. He took a stride, and another, and let Anchor look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:265:At twelve feet there was the greyness, like breath on glass. At ten, they were there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-266-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-267-There were three courses across the old road, at the knee and the chest and over the head, exactly as he had seen them thrown in the morning; they were as thick as his wrist, and they had not slackened in a day. He went along them with that other sight to the newer bar, where he had watched them arrive, and looked for the place where they were made fast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-268-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-269-It was not there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-270-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:271:He stood in a ditch with the cold in his elbows and looked again. The three courses came to the newer bar and touched it, and there was a point where each of them met the iron; but they did not end. They went through those points as a rope goes through a ring, and on, southward along the edge, out of his sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-272-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-273-He had got it wrong. He had watched three lines fly across a road toward a bar, and had taken the bar for the end of them. A rope is not made fast where it lands; it is made fast where the hauling starts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-274-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-275-He turned his head, slowly, to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-276-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:277:The old bar stood eight feet away across the grown-over road, under its five cold terms of rust. He was eleven feet from it, perhaps, where he stood, and at that distance he could make out only that there was something on it. He climbed out of the southern rut, and crossed the crown of the old road on his hands and knees, since the turf there stood a foot higher and he did not trust the thing that hid him to hide him against the sky; and he let himself down into the northern rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-278-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-279-It was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-280-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:281:It was on the far side of the iron, the side toward the brow, a hand above the turf, and he saw it through the bar, as a person sees a lamp through his own fingers. It was a single point, as big as a fist. Three courses came into it from the south, across the road; and three came into it from the north, round the whole of that great circle, the other end of the same rope. Nothing came out. Everything that held six people in a low place on the hundred and fourteenth day was hauled taut through that one point and belayed there, like the whole of a ship's rigging brought down to a single pin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-282-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-283-He could see it. He had been able to see a line across a doorway once, with a girl's shoulder pressed against it, and it had done him no good whatever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-284-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-285-He lay down in the rut on his left side, with the bar at the length of his arm. He put his right hand out toward it, and round it, and could not tell when he had touched it; he had to watch his own fingers close on the scale to learn that they had. Past the iron, his hand was on the far side of that edge, and he laid the dead palm against the outer face, low down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-286-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-288-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-289-*A thing set against a surface and told to stay.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-290-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-291-He told it not to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-292-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:293:It went as a breath goes that a person has been holding, and the point was simply not under his palm. The three courses to the south dropped across the old road, and he saw the nearest ten feet of them go, like wet rope falling; and from the north, away round the dark edge of the low ground, he felt the rest come in and go slack and run out through the place where a knot had been, a great deal of it, for as long as it takes to draw a bucket. He had not known how much ground it held until it stopped holding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-294-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md-295-Forty paces above him, on the brow, a standing shape flung both its arms forward out of its sleeves, as a man does when a rope is dragged through his hands.
--
