editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:275:*Hundred and thirteenth day, night. At the edge of the second one. A stone, and two bars, both numbered thirty-two; one five cold terms old, one a cold term or two, with a mark under its number that Seln won't read. A floor with no stones on it, and a hole, and a stair. Nobody on the ruts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-276-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-277-*Tide: once, on the healer, on the ridge road. I asked twice today and she granted the second. The short count and then a minute of the long, at one sitting; I am counting that as one, since it was one asking and one yes. At three there were banks. I knew the Path by ten. In the minute, one channel and one thing in it, going somewhere. It cost her nothing that she would notice. It cost me the use of that hand for an hour, and most of a loaf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-278-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-279-*She says not tonight for the other, and she is right.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-280-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-281-*Deployment since the Line: fourteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-282-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-283-He slept a little toward morning. When Brom woke him the stars were going out, and the frost lay so thick on the old road that it showed like a chalk line all the way down to the floor. Seln was coming back along the ruts from the north, and he shook his head at them from thirty yards away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-284-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:285:They ate cold. Brom put his boot into the stirrup of a healer's bow, and set the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped behind its catch, and he laid one of the five bolts in the groove. Karis had her lamp and her notebook and a flask, and Lira drew the knot of her sling tight with her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-286-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md-287-Then Seln stepped over the stone.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-1-# Chapter 51 — Declaring
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:3:He let Shadow go while he lay in the ditch, with his arm still round a bar of the registry's iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-5-It went out of him as it had come. The sound of his own breath returned, loud and ragged, and after it the creak of frozen grass under his hip and the grey mule shifting her feet above the stone. He was a boy lying in an old rut at dusk, under the turf's lip, where nobody on that brow could have seen more of him than a shoulder; and whatever they had looked at for the last quarter of an hour, it had not been him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-7-He drew his arm back. Both his hands were dead to the elbow, with a cold that had nothing to do with the frost, and when he pushed himself up on them it was like leaning on two posts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-8-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-31-*One.* Everything that he owned went dumb. For the length of that count there was a boy standing on a hillside who could not have shifted a foot to save his life; and he spent it looking up at the brow, since his eyes were the only part of him that would answer. The man with the bow had not stood to watch his bolt. He had the nose of the thing on the turf already, and his boot going into the stirrup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-33-Then the count was done, and Cael ran.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:35:He went up the slope on a slant, toward the old road, with the frozen grass breaking under him and forty paces of hill before him. He counted as he ran, and it was the bowman's count that he kept and not his own: at seven the hook was on the string, and at eleven the back was beginning to come up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-37-At twelve yards short of the brow he reached the lip of the old rut, and he gave up the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:39:It was like shutting an eye. The pace and a half that had been under his soles since a stair went out from under them. He let Storm, the heaviest thing that he owned, have the air instead, and he laid the lane up the rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-41-It was a corridor as long as a cart and team, set down in that trough with its high end at his feet and its low end up the hill, and everything in it went one way at once. Frost went, and the seed that hung heavy on the grass at either lip, and chaff, and the dust of a hundred dry days; it went up that channel in a grey flood and burst out over the brow, twelve yards on, into two hoods.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-43-He let it go, and began to pay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-44-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-69-He did not let Anchor look. He had no need to; he had the hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-71-They were a man's hands, broad, and short in the finger. That was all that he was ever to know of their owner. There was a pale band round the third finger of the left, where a ring had been taken off before the work began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:73:His own left hand had found his knife. He could not feel the hilt. He drew it, and held it out at the full length of his arm, with the point a hand's breadth under those two open palms; and he asked Ember for the one thing that it had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-74-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:75:A point of fire stood on the knife's tip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-77-It was no bigger than a candle's. It lit the palms from beneath, and the grey cuffs, and nothing above them. He held it there. He said no word, and he did not move the blade nearer by the width of a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-79-A person cannot thread a needle with a flame under his hands.
