--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-87-The two of them went north along the carters' road, side by side, at a trot. They did not run until they were sixty yards off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-89-Cael turned to the low ground, and lifted the knife over his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:91:The lag had gone out of him. He stood on the brow in the last of the dusk, with a point of fire at the end of his arm where anybody on that rim could see it, and he looked for the other four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-93-To the south there were two shapes still. One of them, the one that had stood all day with its arms folded, raised a single arm above its hood and let it fall. Then both stooped, and straightened, and went away over the rim to the south, with nothing in their hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-95-When he looked to the north, there was nobody there at all.
