--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-109-He heard them long before he saw them. There was no need for quiet now. Brom's breathing came first, heavy and careful, and then a shuffle of boots on frozen turf; and at last five shapes grew out of the dusk on the old road, with a small one in front that made no sound at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-110-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-111-Seln stepped over the stone, and stood a breath on the outside of it. Cael saw his shoulders come down by the width of a finger, as a man's do when a Path that he has done without all day is handed back to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-112-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:113:Karis was next, and she had said that she would never grow used to it. She crossed, and stopped, and put out her right hand in front of her in the dark. A point of light stood on the air above her palm, as small and steady as a star.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-114-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-115-"I have a hand," she said, to nobody. Her voice shook. "I have a hand again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-116-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:117:She set the point on the wick of her lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-119-The healer came over between two of them, with Brom's good arm across her shoulders and his weight on her; and Cael watched her do what he had seen her do at a rock on the Fallow, eight times in one grey morning, when she thought that nobody saw. She took one stride past the stone. She stopped dead, with her eyes shut and her lips moving, and her free hand closed on Brom's wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-120-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-121-The count went to four, and on, and did not stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-123-"There. Back," she said. It came out of her on one breath, hoarse, as a person speaks who has been holding it since the morning. She opened her eyes and looked at her own left hand in the lamplight, back and palm. "The clock started again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-125-Then she was a healer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:127:"Down. Here, on the blanket, on your left side. You, bring that light and hold it where I point. You, the pannier, the one that's on the grey." She had Brom on the turf before any of them had moved. "And somebody give me a knife that'll cut ash."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-129-It was Seln's. She had him kneel behind Brom's shoulder and cut through the shaft below the iron head, a notch at a time, while she held the wood still in both her fists so that it should not turn. It took as long as it takes to say a line over twice; then the head came away, and she threw it on the grass, and put one hand flat on the front of the shoulder and took hold of the feathered end with the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-131-"Breathe out," she said. "All of it. I'll not lie to you; you'll know that it's leaving."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-132-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-141-"I've another side," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-143-"I've seen it. Be quiet." She turned on her knees. "You. Give me that arm, and give me the lamp to somebody else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:145:Lira gave the lamp to Karis. She held out the sling without a word, and the healer undid her own knot, and unwound a strip of Cael's shirt that had gone stiff and dark, and put her two hands round the forearm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-147-It took a quarter of an hour. When she had finished she did not let go of the wrist at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-149-"It held a stair. It's not going to hold anything else this season."
