--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-155----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-157-He paid the rest of his own bill sitting against the old iron, with his knees drawn up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:159:It came all at once, as it had come at the stone on the Fallow. Across his left palm there was a white weal as long as a finger. He had held a knife with a flame on its point in a hand too cold to know that the hilt was hot, and he had found it out only when he tried to open that hand again. He could not stop shaking. He had had a week of barley sacks and tables to rest in, and he had spent the whole of what it gave him between a stair and a brow, in less time than a kettle takes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-161-The healer looked at the palm from her stride and a half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-163-"I can't touch that. You know why." She threw him what was left of Lira's tape. "Wind it round, and keep it out of the frost. It'll close faster than it ought to. Yours always do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-164-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-165-Seln had gone to the mules.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-167-He went round the three boxes under their blanket with the lamp held low, and stooped over each cord in turn, and touched nothing. When he came back his face was as it had been all day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-168-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:169:"I tied those to be read. There is a stem of grass laid under every knot, and each is where I laid it." He set the lamp down. "Six people with a quarter of a mile of rim apiece do not leave it to look into a mule's load."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-171-"What did they see?" said Cael. "Tell me as you'd write it for them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:173:"A fence that failed at dusk, at its knot, which was set on an iron bar that stands upon the very edge of the place; and directly afterward a boy, who stood up out of the old road beside that bar and declared." Seln folded his hands. "Nobody on that rim saw anything between the stair and the bar. I asked Lira what she saw from the thirty-fourth step, and she says that she saw a stair. They will write that he crept up a rut in bad light while they were watching a hole, and that a knot tied a hand's breadth from such an edge is a knot which nobody should have trusted. A man who lays fences for his living would far sooner put that on paper than the other thing, and he has no cause to think of the other thing at all. It holds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-175-He turned, and went up the old road to the brow by himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-177-When he came down he had a crossbow in his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-178-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-195-He ruled a line, and put down what he had spent, one thing to a line, as he had on the Fallow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-197-*Anchor: looking, at the two bars, first light. I saw the fence go up. I stood two counts to see it, and Brom had those two counts with a bolt in him.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:199:*Shadow: once, from the fourth step to the old bar. Lira saw it go, from below. Seln turned his back. Three were round the door. The six were two hundred and sixty yards off, with a lip between; not one had a line to me. Let go in the rut. It holds. Dead to the elbows; I had to watch my own hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-201-*The read: the ground, from the hole to the bar. All my eyes. I looked up once, and it left me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:203:*Anchor: looking, for the knot. I can't find what that costs. Entered as not found.*
