editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-246-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-247-Teague's eyes did not leave him, and Cael waited for the rest of it: how, and with what, and what a boy was who had a Wind's crack in him and a fire as well. He watched the man weigh each of those and set it down again, as he had set down a question at an inn's door two nights before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-248-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-249-"Then you're not on the board anymore. You're something the board doesn't have a column for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-250-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:251:It was the second time in three days that Cael had been told so. A one-armed man had said it of a line in a book. This man said it of him, to his face, across a lantern, with no heat in it and no fear; he might have been reading out the weight of a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-252-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-253-"I've three with me, and a light," said Teague. "They'll want that rim walked before any of you sleeps. I'll have it walked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-254-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-255-He took up his lantern, and went back up the old road to where his three were standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-256-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-257----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-258-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:259:It had been the hundred and thirteenth morning, a day and a half before, when Teague took a slip that he did not want.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-260-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-261-He had been at the plank before the mist was off the river. The five had gone by it in the dark, two hours earlier, and he had heard the mule. Pike was setting out his book; and there was a shingle on the oak, six along from the left-hand post, which had hung there for nine days because no crew of any standing would trouble with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-262-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-263-*Millrace. Three stakes of the weir, gone in the last high water. To be set before the ground shuts. Four marks.*
