editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-276-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-277-Pike said nothing more. He wrote the name. But he kept his one hand on the shingle for a breath after the ink was dry, and Teague knew that he had been read; and he found, somewhat to his surprise, that he did not mind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-278-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-279-He had told a boy at a door that his crew would be at Millrace in two days, and it had been true when he said it only because he had decided, between one sentence and the next, to make it true. He had been turning that over ever since. A man does not walk up to another crew and hand it the hair on his own neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-280-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:281:But he could be half a day from where a certain paper pointed, with a spear and a bow and a boy, on honest business. Nobody could say that it rested on nothing, since it rested on three stakes and four marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-282-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-283-They walked at noon, and slept at the fork above the mill, without a fire, a crew's length apart; and before he lay down he counted his three by their breathing, as he did every night of his life. The bow's was the short one, since that hip had not liked the ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-284-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-285-It was the boy who asked, at that fork, before the blankets were down. He had a heel of bread half way to his mouth, and he stopped it there, as he stopped eating whenever his captain was about to speak.
