editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-345-"We'll not run in on it," he said. "Give them the time to be gone. Then we light the lantern, and we carry it where it shows."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-346-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-347-He read the ground by that lantern as they came down, since it was the thing that he could do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-348-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:349:On the brow, where the ruts turned, two people had lain all day, and the frost showed him the two shapes, two yards apart, each a man's length. There was no ash of any fire, and nobody had dropped a crumb there, or a thread, or the paring of a nail. Beside the northern shape he found the round print of a stirrup pressed into the turf again and again in the one place. He counted, and there were eleven prints, and a twelfth half made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-350-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md-351-That was a crossbow. He had expected a bow of some kind. He had not expected that every sign of its owner would stop at the height of a boot.
