editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-41-Something was wrong with that shot, and though he had only fifteen yards to cover, it took him the whole of them to say what. A bow on that brow could have let all five of them walk down to the floor, and had them two hundred yards inside, with nothing over them and nowhere to go. It had chosen to shoot the last man instead, when he was five strides from the way out, with a healer standing in plain sight at the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-43-A shot of that kind did not want them to go on. It wanted them to turn round, or it wanted somebody to come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-44-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:45:He was twelve feet from the stone when he made up his mind. He had refused Anchor, the thinnest of his ten, at these two irons the evening before, and again in the frost an hour ago. He let it look now, on purpose, with his eyes open; and since he had stopped dead to do it, he stood on a frozen road for the length of two counts while a friend with a bolt in him was being turned about by the arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-46-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:47:At twelve feet there was a greyness in the air between the bars, like breath. He took one stride more, and it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-49-Three lines were going across the old road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-50-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-51-They came from the old iron, on the north side, and they went to the newer one, eight feet away, at the height of a knee and a chest and a hand above a tall man's head. He saw them arrive, and they did not creep; they swung across as a gate swings to, all three together, and caught on the newer bar, and ran on past it to the south until they were beyond his seeing. On the north side they ran away from the old iron along the turf in the same three courses, and went out of his sight in that direction too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-52-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:53:He had seen such things once before, in a chalk cut on the Ostrand road, and he knew the craft as he knew his own handwriting. A point was set against a surface and told to stay, and a line was drawn from it to the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-55-But these were not hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:57:The woman in the cut had laid nineteen points round him while he fought her, each as fine as a fishing line, from fifteen feet, with the stillness of a person threading a needle. Whoever had laid these was forty paces off. And they were thick: every one was as thick as his wrist, and there were three where she would have used thirty. It was the same trade in a different hand, as a ship's cable and a stitch are the same trade: this hand threw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-59-"Stop," he said. "It's shut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-61-The healer did not so much as turn her head. She had Brom's left arm across her shoulders and she was bringing him up the road toward the stone at a stumbling run, with her eyes on the grey wool of his coat where a dark stain was beginning. She had three strides to go, and Cael saw her thinking of nothing in the world but those three strides, at the end of which she would be a Tide again.
