editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-185-"Stand where you are," said Seln's voice, close to his ear. "All of you. I am striking a light."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-186-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:187:There was a scrape of steel, and a spark, and another. Karis's breath went in and out somewhere to the left. Cael knew what she was doing as well as if he had seen her. She had put out her hand in the dark to set a point on her own wick, as she had done every night of her life since she was fourteen; and there was no hand to reach with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-188-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-189-The tinder caught. Seln held it to the lamp, and the small brass thing took the flame and steadied. Nobody had spoken since the fortieth step, and the only sound in that place was six people getting their breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-191-It showed them a room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-193-It was square, and Cael put it at ten strides each way. The floor was the same grey, level and seamless. So was the roof. He looked up, and it took him a breath to understand what he was looking at: the roof of that room was the underside of the floor that they had run across. There was no beam in it and no arch; it lay over them like a lid, and he judged it a foot above Brom's head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-195-The walls were cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:197:Karis made a sound in her throat. From the floor to the roof, on all four sides, the grey was covered with the fine sunk lines that had been round the feet of eleven stones on the Fallow: straight, and hooked, and crossing. They ran in bands, one above another, each as tall as a man's forearm. There was more of it on one wall of that room than on the whole of the other place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-199-And in the middle of the floor, where the lamp only just reached, something lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md-201-It was a dark oblong, the length of a man's arm, and it had a look of leather or tarred cloth about it, as a case has. From one end of it a pale line ran out along the floor toward the wall that they had come in by, and stopped.
