editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-13-"I know what it is. I did not need to count."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-15-It was a narrow room with one small window, and it smelled of barley. There was a table in it of scrubbed deal. The keeper moved his meal-sacks to one end, and by the time the hearth had been banked for the night she had made the other end her own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:17:Brom set the kit on the table. Its case lay there in tarred canvas, with the strap coiled on the lid, as it had lain on a floor under the ground; inside were a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, and a mallet. The stake had never been driven. Beside it Karis put a dead man's ledger, shut. The three boxes stood against the warm wall, and each of them had one leaf in it that was the same as its fellows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-19-Then she took out the sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:21:She laid them in three heaps along the deal, and did not try to make their edges agree; the Fallow's were long strips, and the Stair's were leaves, and the hall's were whatever a person could tear from a roll with her teeth. There were eleven in the first heap. There were twenty-nine in the second, and twelve in the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-23-"Fifty-two," said Lira, from the door. She had counted them as they went down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-24-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-25-"Fifty-two sheets. Forty-one kinds, at the two that I could do properly. Three perimeters of one size, and a fourth that is twice it." Karis leaned on the deal with both palms. "I had three boxes of other people's stone when I crossed the Line. I should not have said that there was anything east of it to put in them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:27:She set her lamp in the middle, and lit it with a point, and unrolled the hide.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-29-Cael did not go to the hearth with the others. He sat down at the meal-sack end of that table, with Hesk's book, while she worked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-31-*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-33-He ruled a line beneath it. What came next was the inventory, which he made at the start of anything: every fragment that he carried, in the order of its coming, with the Path that it stood beside and the person who had given it. He had last made one in a loft, on the night that he was nineteen. This was the first that he had ever written under a roof which he meant to come back to, and it was to stand as the count for the whole of a season past the Line; so he wrote it slowly, and left nothing out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-35-*Ten confirmed fragments.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-39-*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Not an entry any more; a location. The still place. Read three times. Doesn't flow. Doesn't change. Everything goes around it. Not the Quiet. Not a fragment. Older than the log. Observation continues.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-41-*Reydan will get his answer. Daeva will get her rematch. Both are going to need a map.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md-42-
