editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:15:He turned that over in the dark, since he had the leisure. He had said once, at a fire in a meadow, that he kept waiting for someone to ask for his report. For fifteen years his reports had been asked for by a slot in a door; for a hundred days after that they had been asked for by nobody, and he had gone on walking the first hour of every road, alone, and saying what he found or not as it bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:37:Seln had done the sum himself, in the half-breath before. It was the first thing that a person of his trade did on entering any room, and he had done it without choosing to: who is here, and what can each of them see, and where will each of them be tomorrow. There were four who had known for years and had been tried. There was a healer, who was owed everything and knew nothing, and who walked seven roads. She had her back turned and a hundredweight of copied stone between.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:291:"It is a question. It is put to this hillside, and the hillside cannot write. So it is put to whoever will walk here for a hundred marks; and there is a clerk at a landing, with ink on his cuffs, who has been told to see what is brought down and send word of it west by the next boat." He lowered the paper. "The word that he sends does not need to have a brass level in it. It needs only to say what became of the crew."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:Cael folded the sheet along its creases. He had two things now, and he laid them side by side in his head, as Karis had once laid a noun beside six numbers. Somebody above a registry had asked which way certain people had gone; and a paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, written by a hand that had learned its trade at a ruled desk, sending whoever took it two days east of a fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:21:"No; he has measured. He knows what the first was priced at and who took it. He knows that five people took it who have a hundred or so in hand and the cold term coming, and he has asked himself what sum such a crew cannot leave hanging on a nail." Seln's voice did not change. "That's not a wage. That's a lure, cut to fit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:47:He said it to the fire, in the mild voice in which he had once read out a floor schedule, and then he sat back out of the light. Cael understood him well enough: a broker's clerk had stood at a plank for a hundred days and watched which crew took which paper, and it had wanted only one fact about a boy to write the second sheet; the boy had supplied that fact himself, aloud, at an inn's boards, when he showed that he had never yet walked past a paper that paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:77:"It isn't. You said what the paper is. I'm saying what the number is." He frowned at the coals. "My father bought a horse at a fair, the year I was nine. A grey, sixteen hands, with a neck on him like a bridge. Every man on that field could see that he was worth three hundred of anybody's money, and the dealer wanted a hundred for him, and wouldn't say why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:155:Lira's eyebrows went up, and Cael felt his own go. In two years on a bluff, and a hundred and twelve days of roads, he had never heard Brom repeat what another person had said to him. Brom carried other people's words inside his shirt, on dead slips, and did not say them over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:217:*HOLD.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:53:The five waded after. It was a hundred yards of gravel under a foot of quick water, cold enough to make the bones of his feet ache. The tape told him where the edge was whenever a stone turned under him. He kept his breath shallow and his eyes on the cart's tail. Seln waded a little upstream of him. He had taken up the strap of the locked case by two holes before he stepped in, so that it rode high between his shoulder-blades, well clear of the river; it had been on him on every road since their own cart was sold, and he had declined, mildly, to let it ride on another man's bed with the boxes. Three boxes sat on it under a sheet of patched canvas, left and middle and right, with the corner that was worn white turned outward. On top of them, roped down by her own instructions, sat Karis, who had been lifted up there like a sack and had made no objection.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:97:"I heard." The purse came down over the cart's side in a small pale hand. "I shall want it understood that this is the board's money and it has bought a boot and a half's worth of leather that nobody can wear. In hand, a hundred and fourteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:117:A breath went in to a count of three before the tape spoke. On a level road he could take that breath at every fourth stride and never touch the edge. On a rise it wanted every third, and on the steepest of the valley's little hills every second, and there the breaths came so shallow and so quick that his head swam. He had always walked in fits. He had gone quick and then slow since he was a child on the lanes of Denvash, running to look at a thing and standing still to count it. He had watched a healer come down a mountain for a day and a half at one unbroken beat, and he had not been able to copy her for a hundred yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:199:It took her the better part of an hour to do two hundred yards, and there were a hundred and forty crosses.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:209:His left hand had been in a sling and a cuff, by turns, since a river on the fifty-third day. That made thirty-five days. Cael watched the two wrists, as he had watched them under a cart on the eighth narrowing, and no grey started at either. There was a hundredweight of copied paper on them and they were the colour of wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:213:He carried it the whole way over. He came back for the left-hand one, and then for the right; and when Seln stepped forward for the third, since three crossings of that moss was a long way under a box, Brom shook his head and took that too. The carter watched him go by each time with a hundredweight in his arms and a look of deep content on his plain face, and plainly could not make out what he was seeing. Carrying boxes was only what a big man was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:259:*Eighty-ninth day. The west bank, a morning short of the ferry. The boxes rode from Oxhollow on the keeper's grain cart, for nothing, since the map said no. In hand, a hundred and fourteen; a boot. Six owed to the smith and a rope to the ferryman.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:93:He was at a hundred and ten, and the pack had begun to move with them. It did not come on as seven had come on at a ford, in a line, behind one that went first. It drifted. A grey shape would get up and walk a few yards toward the southern rim and stand, and another would pass it and stand; and so, by pieces, without any one of them appearing to decide anything, the nine were always a little nearer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:131:"You didn't make the kind of noise they listen for." Cael had the count at a hundred and sixty, and his eyes on a long-legged shape that had begun to pace them along the inside of the rim. "You're a man with a stick, and you can't declare. So far as that floor's concerned you haven't said a word since you came over the stone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:137:He grounded the haft and walked on, a foot from the lip, with a hundredweight of copied stone on his shoulders. Cael looked at the broad back in front of the grey, and at the wrists, which were the colour of wrists. Brom had stood on a rock in a river once and been the loudest thing for half a mile, on purpose, for three breaths; he was doing a harder thing now, which was to be nobody, with a pole, in the right place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:93:They slept on the ridge in a hollow, without wood, in every blanket that they had. Brom laid six bowls on the turf. He looked at the row, and took one back; and it passed without remark, since he had done exactly that on a hundred nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:149:She went down from the ruts by herself, on the whole of her foot, counting under her breath, and he counted with her. The line had lain forty paces inside the ruts at the Stair, and the ruts at the Fallow had kept forty yards. She passed forty with the iron still a long way below her. She passed a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:151:At a hundred and eighty-nine, or within a pace or two of it, she stopped one pace above the dark uprights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:69:It was work, besides, that four half-mended people could do. A man with a bitten calf could pace a level shelf and write. A girl with a stiff ankle could tell him whether a hundred yards of it would bear her, and a researcher who could hold a pencil for a quarter of an hour had a cord, and a clock, and a head that kept numbers as a jar keeps water. Nobody would have to be fast, or hard, or to set anything alight; the job asked for no Path from any of them, and for no fragment of his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:219:It was dusk. Shadow had been lifting in him for an hour, as it lifted at every dusk, like a tide coming up a beach. He had told it no on the fifth evening past the Line, and at a ford with the light going, and on a gate-walk; he had refused it on something over a hundred evenings, and let it have him once, for ten strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:235:He took the southern of its two ruts, since that was the side that the newer bar stood on. It was a trough a foot deep and as wide as a wheel, with turf grown down into it, and it ran straight up the fall of the ground to a stone that he could not yet see. He had a hundred and ninety yards of it to walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:239:He had been waiting for it. A thing made for hiding costs from the moment that a person wakes; he had paid that on every day since it came to him, by carrying it shut. To have it open was a different bill. It began in the ends of his fingers, as it had begun in a lane on the Fallow, and it was not the frost, since it came from inside. At fifty strides he could not feel his nails; at a hundred his hands hung at the ends of his arms like things that he was carrying for somebody, and the cold had gone into his wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:241:He looked up once, at a hundred and twenty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:249:At a hundred and fifty there was a noise that nearly stopped that heart altogether.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:131:She had gone up the ladder behind the lean man with her right hand and both knees, and had stood up on the planks with the whole valley in front of her. There was the stretch, a mile of pale frozen grass between two shoulders of hill. There was the white stone, a hundred and ten yards down it. And forty paces out from the gate somebody had driven a stake, and hung a sack on it that was stuffed with straw, with a ring burnt on the sacking as big as a plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:47:He had read that man's feet in the silt of a ford, and his halts at five halting-grounds after it. He had watched him count three people on a sill before he looked at a room, and had been told by him on a river bank, in a level voice, what a mountain was worth. Now he had the man's answer to a hundred marks as well, at second hand, from a book-keeper who gave out nothing that he had not seen with his own eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:61:*HOLD.* It was in the square capitals, and it was not his. There were five names under it, and five again under the same word on the shingle beside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:81:The price came first, and it was a hundred, which was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:85:The ground he knew. He had been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and had looked across the grass each time at a row of grey stumps and kept walking. Every carter's child knew what was said of it. He had never put it to the proof, and he did not need a paper to tell him why not: he had three behind him, and in that place the spear would be a man with a spear, and the bow a man with a bad hip, and the boy a boy. He would not take them in for a hundred, or for twice a hundred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:99:It was all either of them said about it. There was a fence on the oak, four slips along: the axe-head hold's longer fence on the east road, forty marks, HOLD. Teague drew its tack and held it out, and Pike wrote his name to it before he shut the book and tucked it under what was left of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:149:All of those were his, and he saw that each of them was a way of dressing the plain thing so that it should look better on him. The plain thing was that five people had a hundred and eight marks and the cold coming, and that this paid as much again for four days of road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:215:*Ninetieth day. Lowmarch. The seventh line is said: seventy in the purse, sixty-four on the wall. Tenth. The smith is paid, and we have a hundred and eight. A rope is owed to the ferryman, with no figure. Two holds have asked for us by name, and will wait a fortnight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:243:"Then you come down and tell me so, and I say that." Pike was blowing on the strokes to dry them. "I write what was done. There's a hundred in my box against his mark on this paper, and if his mark never goes on it, the box stays shut. I've nothing in the book that pays a crew for walking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:13:They went down the stretch at the one time of day when nothing had ever come up it. Cael walked last. He had the white stone on his left hand at a hundred and ten yards, and he gave it the look that he gave it every time. Fifty-seven evenings had gone by since anything stood there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:119:Three old men sat beyond her, at boards of their own. Cael had seen them under the ash for a hundred days without ever learning what they did, three grey heads over a pair of dice. The dice lay on the wood between them now. The eldest had his hand half closed over them, and he neither threw nor gathered them up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:191:For the space of a breath there was only the fire. Three hundred marks was more than twice what the five of them had earned in a hundred and twelve days. He did the sum without wishing to, and saw Brom doing it, with his lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:135:He had laid two things side by side at a wash four days ago, and had refused to rule a line between them for want of a third. A query had come down to a registry from somewhere above it, asking which way certain people had gone. A paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, in the hand of somebody who had learned to write at a ruled desk. And here, on the edge of a place where a carter would not put a wheel, was a bar of iron with a registry's stamp on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:77:At a hundred Lira's bad foot dragged, and she made no sound; he knew it only from how the healer's shoulder dropped to take her. At ninety Brom said, without turning, that there was something in the lane behind them, and Cael looked. One of the eight had come out onto the trodden grass thirty yards back, and was sitting there with its tongue out. It got up when they had gone on ten strides, and came after, and sat again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:115:That left one. The healer knew nothing of him, and she spoke to every carter on seven roads. She had her back to him. A big man and a hundredweight of box stood between her and the place where he was, and her eyes were on Karis. She would see where he ended. She could not see where he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:227:He had counted his strides from the place where he dropped into the rut, and he was at a hundred and thirty when he heard it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:25:The mule drew the thing away to the south, outside the line, a hundred yards and more downwind, and left a flattened track through the grass like the lane on the far side. Cael watched it go. He had held one of those by the head for the length of thirty counts, and thrown another, and been struck by a third in the dark; and in daylight it was a long grey animal with frost on its hide, being pulled over a field by its feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:139:The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:209:She walked on, flat. The arm lay where it had been put. It had started again before she had gone a hundred yards, at a gust, and she felt it reach the knot and stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:29:He went to her. She held out her wrist, inside upward, as he had seen a hundred people hold theirs out to her; it was broad and brown, with a white scar across the heel of the hand that he had never been near enough to notice. He put his right hand on her wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:133:The carters' ruts did what he had seen them do once before. Halfway down the slope the ground eased into a kind of brow, and at the brow the live ruts swung away to the north and went round the whole of that low place, keeping their distance from it, and came back to their line on the far side. He could see them all the way, two pale threads on the grass, and inside them an older road went straight on down, grown over, with a hundred years of turf in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:147:"It's what he calls the road," said Karis. Her voice was very small. "It is on no map that I own, and every carter in that meadow has known for a hundred years what is at the end of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:81:"Weight. I wanted to know whether a hundredweight was a hundredweight in here, with nothing behind me." He set it down inside the line, quite gently. "It is. So that's what I've got. Write that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:87:Cael wrote it. He wrote *Skin called aloud; none*, and under it, since the row was Brom's, *lifted a hundredweight; no difference*; and it seemed to him that of the two findings the second was the one that Brom would take home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:159:Cael had not seen that it was a matter. He had known for eleven days that three boxes stood in that loft at a hundredweight each, that the carter who had brought them east was gone home with grain, and that the ferry was two days off. He had put all of it where he put the things that would be dealt with when there was leisure, behind a table and a birthday and a week of slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:"So that's two boxes on two backs, and one left over, and a case that the quiet one won't put down." She counted it on her fingers. "And a girl who's to fall on her face sooner than put a hand out, on a road that's ice in the ruts. Who steadies her, when the pair of you have a hundredweight apiece?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:241:He could not see her. He could see a strip of grey blanket through a gap between two planks, lying across her breast, and it did not stir. He thought of a girl who had run a quarter-mile of that fence flat out and stopped dead on both feet. He thought of the same girl a hundred yards down a ledge with her arms out and a herd coming, who had never in her life done a thing slowly if there was a way to do it fast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:317:"What was it like?" she said presently. It was her old voice, the one that she had kept for the rail of a practice floor. "When you let them go, in the lane. You'd held them a hundred days. Was it heavy? Did they come slow?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:119:He went over it as he went over anything. There was meal for six days in the left-hand box, and a little bacon. There was a shoulder that was bound and would not be mended a third time, and an arm in a sling that was to hold nothing till the snow. The frost had come back in the night, harder than they had yet had it, and a healer had said at a stone that the snow would not be long. And there was a line. He had been walking toward something for a hundred and twenty days without knowing that it had a direction, and it had one now, and it went off the edge of a calfskin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:63:He was at a hundred and eight. That was nine minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:153:That was the first thing that he was sure of. He had refused nine things a stride at a time for a hundred days, and he was better at that one act than at anything else he did. He had only to say no, at the first stride, as he had said it on a gravel bar. Her current would go by the empty place and find it shut, as it had found the ninth; she would come to the end of her twenty minutes and lift her hands and tell him that there was nothing, as before. He would get down off her table with what he had lain down on it with. Nothing would have been taken from anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:232:He had a hundred days of road behind him in which he had been asked what he was by nobody. He had thought, on a shelf above a valley, that this was freedom, and had written it down. He lay now under the hands of the one person past the Line who had tried to find out without a seal, and who had just paid for the trying with something that he did not yet know the size of; and he had a leaf in a loft that said what he owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:143:It was a hundred and ninety yards of grass. He had paced none of it, and he measured it now with his lungs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:67:"Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and eight, less six." The hinge creaked. "A hundred and two. It is the first that I have paid out. And been glad to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:73:Cael had seen the man before without knowing whose he was. He was small and spare, a head shorter than the steading's woman, with a bald brown crown and flour to both elbows; and in all his days at that hold Cael had never heard him say a word, since he had never been near enough to the hearth. Thornwater, it appeared, kept its talk in one place. The woman at the gate counted people with her lips and spent a word on every six of them, and the man on the platform had said perhaps forty in a hundred days. Here was the hold's whole share of speech, walking beside Brom with a wooden spoon in his belt and getting through it at a great rate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:83:It was the second time in three days that he had spoken of that house, after a hundred in which he had not named it once, and Cael noted it and let it go by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:45:The spear stood on the south, a tall man, grounded, with the butt of his weapon on the frozen turf and both hands on the shaft; and he watched a girl with one arm find an edge with her foot, sixty times over, and said nothing whatever. On the west it was the boy, sitting on his heels in the ruts with a heel of bread. He stopped chewing when Lira came abreast of him, and he had not started again when they were a hundred yards on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:227:It was not a finding. Nothing on the three leaves said it; they only lay in a row, and let her say it for them. If she wrote it, it would stand in her hand, in ink, in a box that she meant to outlive her, and whoever opened that box in a hundred years would take it for a thing that she had known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:"That's how you find a handful. One at a time, for money." She nodded toward Hesk's satchel on its nail, where a broker's cream paper lay folded in a book. "Somebody paid a hundred marks to learn whether a crew would come out of the first, and I do not think that he has stopped wanting to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:279:She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. She had gone in with her palms up, and with two fingers on her own wrist, and backward, and with her eyes shut; and she had stood in it for a count of a hundred with her arms hanging. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:173:He had not asked that thing for anything since a rut on a hill, and not once in a hundred days for practice. But this was a yard, at noon, inside a fence, where a healer mended with her door open and a researcher might light a lamp; and if he was to be half a beat behind himself on some road next week, he would sooner learn what that was like here, among geese.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:241:He sat down beside her on the coping. His calves ached pleasantly, and the thing under his soles was still half a beat behind, and it no longer troubled him. He had spent a hundred days holding nine things shut; and until this noon he had never been able to hear what any one of them cost while he was spending it. A bill had always come afterward, in a forearm or a rib. This was the first time that he had been shown the price beforehand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:The inn's keeper was a slow heavy man with a cloth over his shoulder, who had drawn their jugs for a hundred and twenty days and had never been asked for anything else. She told him what she wanted: a dry floor, and a door that shut, and a table that nobody ate at. It was for paper, she said. She would have it until the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:17:To Cael, who had learned that crew's sound on a hundred roads and had learned it again at a fork with a pair of boots gone out of it, five had already begun to seem the number that a road was walked by. He did not care for that, and he wrote it down, so that he should be held to noticing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:173:*three hundred; taken; not paid*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:197:He took it by its corner and swung it on the bench, so that the writing faced the other way. Cael had read every line of that book upside down for a hundred and twenty days, across an oak plank, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter. It lay the right way up before him now. He read the season in a square black hand, and at the end of it a ruled space with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:41:It was a corridor as long as a cart and team, set down in that trough with its high end at his feet and its low end up the hill, and everything in it went one way at once. Frost went, and the seed that hung heavy on the grass at either lip, and chaff, and the dust of a hundred dry days; it went up that channel in a grey flood and burst out over the brow, twelve yards on, into two hoods.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:187:It was a hundred and eighty-five yards from the stone to that kerb, as Karis paced it afterward, and nobody else among them could have told iron from a reed at such a distance. Cael did not doubt her. Somebody had come here five cold terms ago with a cord and a mallet, and had driven one stake on the line, where he was sent to drive it. Then he had walked in along that grey path to set another on the lip of the thing itself; and whatever lay under the lip had not cared for the weight of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:219:They walked. Though not one of them could have said so yet, that was the rule of the place, and it kept them company the whole of the way in: a hundred and eighty-five yards, which Karis counted under her breath, with something in the reeds holding its station on them for exactly as long as they did not stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:275:She gave it to him before she had thought, and then she stared, and so did the rest of them. He had read out a great many things to that notebook in a hundred days: the width of a road, and the age of a bar, and what a paper meant. He had asked each time that it should be entered as said. He had never once put a mark in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:27:She sat down where she had knelt, on the frozen turf, with her knees drawn up and her forehead on them, and it was as long as it takes to count a hundred before she lifted her head. Nobody went to her. Cael had heard her say on a mountain that her reserve was a purse; he had watched her empty it at a mill, and at two stones, and he understood by now that what she spent on such a morning was the very thing that a child at some northern gate was waiting for, and that she knew it to the grain.
