169:| The faceless faction | Unnamed, unlocated | Six hooded on the Stair's rim, plus a second Anchor hand. No faces |
196:- **#23 executed on the page:** the gelding went to the ferryman and the cart to the smith in Lowmarch's first week; the sale and the rest of the Book 6 purse are sewn and waxed into a reserve under five thumbprints at the bottom of the third box. "In hand" never counts the reserve.
216:- **The sale (#23; flag):** the gelding to the ferryman, for his capstan, with dry room for the three boxes in the loft over the drum as part of the price; the old capstan mule put out to grass. The cart to the smith, who means to cut its axle to mule gauge and hire it out. Brom's bill-hook came under the cart's seat board. Lira's long knife is the last thing the Book 6 purse buys. No figure is given for the sale, the purse or the reserve.
217:- **The reserve:** sewn into canvas by Karis, waxed by Brom, five thumbs in the wax, at the bottom of the third box under the masonry chips; opened by all five or not at all.
248:**Objects and money.** Three numbered boxes, in the meadow with them on day 49 (they live in the loft over the ferryman's drum when the crew is near; they rode the Thornwater carter's cart both ways). **The cart and the gelding are sold** (day 12). **The reserve** is sealed at the bottom of the third box and has not been opened. Board purse: **eighteen marks earned** (ferry 2, fence 4, wash 3, Thornwater road 9); **about ten in hand** after one jug. Twenty pounds of flour from the steading, mostly eaten. The two letters to Hesk and Vell went west on day 4, a copper each to the turf fire. Hesk's satchel holds the log, the old volume, the observation notebook, and Pike's line on a scrap of bark. The ash wedge is still with them. The registry's printed sheet is in the middle box and was not unrolled.
263:- **Asset state after #23:** no cart or horse; the reserve is sealed under masonry chips in the third box and opens only by all five; the boxes lodge over the ferry drum when the crew is near Lowmarch; Seln's closed case is back on its strap (`manuscript/chapter-03.md:107,123-185`; `manuscript/chapter-04.md:277`; `manuscript/chapter-05.md:55-65`).
327:**Objects and money.** Three boxes over the ferry drum. The reserve unopened. Board purse: **sixty-two marks earned** (18 + ford 40 + well 4); **fifty-two in hand** (one mark on flour and bacon on day 51; one to the hold for linen and thread; coppers unitemised). Hesk's satchel holds the log (with the finding and the seal entry), the old volume, the observation notebook, Pike's two lines on bark, and Hesk's letter. Cael's coat has lost its left sleeve. The ash stick is propping an old carter's shaft under the ash.
344:- Equipment details: three boxes are back over the ferry drum; the reserve remains sealed; Cael's coat lacks its left sleeve; Brom still owns the hedging bill and the older ash wedge; the borrowed ash walking stick has been left and repurposed under a carter's shaft (`manuscript/chapter-11.md:231`; `manuscript/chapter-14.md:345-359`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:144`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:248`).
383:- **Tide, as taught (flag):** contact only. What mends is the patient's own current, which stands at a break "like water behind a board"; she finds where it stands, opens the way with her own, and holds it open while the body does six weeks' knitting in under an hour, and she pays for the six weeks. A reserve "like a purse", spent at the rate of what it mends: three mendings in a day if she has slept under a roof and been fed, two on a road. She will not touch a table until she has eaten and slept two hours. She cannot do it to herself. She mends what she can read. **The surface reading:** hands on the hurt, or two fingers on a wrist and two on the neck, for a count of ten; it costs her nothing she would notice. **The deep reading ("the long one"):** both hands, all of her, minutes, the patient still and whole, at a mending's cost; not run on a road. A person's current runs in a bed, and "the shape of the bed is their Path"; she names Karis as Ember by it. **The stall:** a hand on a wrist for a breath as the other means to declare, and the declaration does not come; it costs a day's mendings and the next day's and mends nobody; she has done it and hates it. No count of times is given.
403:**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No declaration; the Skin did not start under the cart at the eighth narrowing. **Left forearm opened at the wrist on the 78th (two boxes), mended on the 80th; in a linen cuff; a week from the middle of the 80th, to take what sits on it and nothing that hangs from it.** He carried the third box down the mountain on his back. He has the hedging bill and the older ash wedge.
413:**Objects and money.** **The cart is in the gorge**, with the ferryman's rope, the blankets, the kettle, the canvas and the flour. Three boxes, whole, and Brom's ash wedge beside them; the reserve sealed in the third, unopened. Seln's case. Hesk's satchel, with the log, the old volume, the notebook, Hesk's letter and Pike's lines on bark. Karis's lantern, cord, brass straightedge and Hesk's clock. Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two marks earned** (62 + Oxhollow 70); **a hundred and sixteen in hand**; **six owed to the smith** for the cart. Pike's slip is in hand, with five strokes and Oxhollow's mark at its foot, to be carried to the plank. The long leaf was unrolled on the keeper's board and paid for there; the text does not say who carries it away, and the keeper is its buyer.
419:**Open threads.** A fourth reading, promised at Thornwater in nine days and not possible, by her own sum, before the hundredth; and what four of the five felt when a healer said "both hands, and all of me". Twenty-one days of tape. Karis's reserve. A leg that may not run for a month and an ankle that will set. Six marks owed for a cart, and a rope. What Pike will write for a job that delivered a no and lost a cart. "The same contract inside a season." Animals lost from a hill, heard and not sworn, and twenty-five counted. Two hounds still open below Thornwater's ford. Lira's question, now with a job in it and a ledge under it. Hesk's letter, and no roof.
431:- The three boxes are whole at Oxhollow; the reserve is sealed in the third. The marked Oxhollow slip must return to Pike. The long map leaf was paid for on the keeper's board; the keeper is its buyer. Seln's closed case remains on its strap, and Hesk's satchel still carries the log, old volume, notebook, Hesk letter, and Pike's bark lines (`manuscript/chapter-15.md:153`; `manuscript/chapter-20.md:27-37,89-93`; `manuscript/chapter-21.md:321-361`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:323-327`).
446:- **Pike spoke of Teague unasked, to the ledger:** Teague read the Fallow slip twice, put it back under the stone and took the fence. Teague has no stated reason. The two HOLD slips are unresolved ("a fortnight, then Teague").
461:- **The two HOLD slips (flag):** one bark and one shingle; a bull to be brought down off top grazing before the snow, and a ford to be staked. Each hold "asked who". They will wait a fortnight for the five and then have Teague. Cael tells Pike on the 91st not to keep them past the fortnight. **They are still on the oak at the end of the movement.**
471:- **Karis's reserve:** her first point since the 78th is set on a lamp-wick in Thornwater's yard on the 93rd, at Oryn's bidding; "three parts full".
477:- **What each of the five reports inside** (beyond the protected lines): Karis can see where sixteen points would go and "can't find the hand to reach with"; this is not being empty ("reserve present, sigil gone"). Brom: "There's no next room"; a hundredweight weighs the same. Seln: "It is not there to be asked." Lira: a yard and a half, and no crack. Oryn: the surface count stops at "Four—"; returns at the stride; and stops when she alone is inside with Brom's wrist held across the line.
499:**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Nothing to lift inside. Unmarked. He has read the posting as an office's paper, written by somebody who learned to write inside the Line; the stake as five years in the weather and driven at the far end of a measure; the eastern runs; the den. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut**; it went through Oxhollow's ford on the 85th, left Thornwater's loft with him on the 94th and crossed the line on the 96th, unopened.
505:**Objects and money.** The middle box: chips, lamp, paper, ten rubbings (the eleventh is in Karis's coat), the two maps, the brass, the short cord. Seln's case on his strap. Two boxes in Thornwater's loft, the reserve sealed in the third, unopened. The Oxhollow cart has gone home with grain. Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and eight in hand.** Owed: one rope, sixty yards, four-strand, to the ferryman, with no price. A hundred marks lie in Pike's box against the broker's mark on the Fallow paper. Cael's coat has no sleeves.
523:- **Exact party/object placement:** six at the den; Brom wears the middle box; Seln is on one knee; Oryn's mule is hobbled west of the line stone with two panniers, six borrowed blankets, the unused left boot and covered crossbow; two boxes remain in Thornwater, with the reserve sealed in the third (`manuscript/chapter-27.md:13-29`; `manuscript/chapter-30.md:3-7,219-239`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:167,183`).
526:- **Accounts/open obligations:** 108 marks in hand; one sixty-yard four-strand rope owed in kind; the two HOLD slips remain unresolved; the Fallow hundred remains lodged against the broker's mark (`manuscript/chapter-24.md:3-21,227-245`; `manuscript/chapter-25.md:229-253`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:181-185`).
538:- **Shadow has been used ONCE and remains sealed.** Only Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln had the sightline; Oryn was screened and saw him arrive, not come; the pack is no witness. Oryn cannot identify Seln as its source. Seln struck Cael's second reason for silence ("I shall not be a reason… I should sooner have her told what may be done to her than be used to keep her ignorant") WITHOUT offering to be known as the source: Movement 6 tells her sources by Path, not by person, and the seal serves again at the Stair.
583:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. **Nine points and eight joins for a count of six at the line on the 96th**; about a tenth left that night; nothing since. Throat mended on the 97th; hoarse single words by the 99th. Holds: eleven Ring sheets, dry, not yet looked at; the box unopened; the carter's map with three rings (two still *guess*) and Seln's charcoal sheet; her leaf *Where / Entered as said / Blank. Stamped. Filed.*; the stub and the entry against herself of the 99th. The two torn leaves lay on the loft's boards. She has asked him nothing.
589:**Objects and money.** Board purse unchanged: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and eight in hand.** The Fallow hundred lies in Pike's box; no instruments were found; the posting (five strokes) is in Hesk's book. The crossbow, with **five bolts**, is with the crew; its spanning hook is in the mule's near-side pannier. One bolt lies on the Ring's floor. Six blankets returned, one a hand narrow. The rope is still owed.
593:**Pike's book and the wall.** Unchanged: seven lines; tenth; Teague nine up. The Fallow line has not been said. The two HOLD slips are unresolved on the page.
606:- **Karis/papers:** eleven dry Ring sheets remain unopened at movement close; the charcoal overlay and two map circles marked *guess* remain; her two torn model leaves, stub and exact covenant leaf are in the loft (`manuscript/chapter-34.md:203-223`; `manuscript/chapter-36.md:41-55,180-219`). Seln knows only the remembered maps and has never seen the file (`manuscript/chapter-34.md:1-107,227-265`; fixes 8 and 15).
607:- **Accounts/open threads:** start day 100 with 108 in hand; the Fallow hundred remains unpaid in Pike's box; one sixty-yard four-strand rope, two HOLD slips and three replies remain owed; the completion report travels west saying six; seven hounds remain untracked north/south along the outside (`manuscript/chapter-24.md:19,247-251`; `manuscript/chapter-33.md:193-195`; `manuscript/chapter-34.md:273-307`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:499-505`).
678:**Objects and money.** Three boxes; the reserve sealed in the third, unopened. **Oryn's mule, with the crew** (one empty pannier on the saddle). Hesk's satchel: the log (the rule, the note, the second pole, the birthday line), the old volume, the notebook, the letters, the broker's posting, Pike's lines on bark including the Fallow copy. The crossbow, hook and five bolts. Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and two in hand** (six to the hold on the 102nd). Owed: the ferryman's rope; the Fallow hundred lies in Pike's box unpaid. Hesk's parcel has not come; no boat has come in from the west since the 91st.
680:**Pike's book and the wall.** **Eight lines**: the eighth is the protected Fallow line, entered on the 101st and sent up on bark (read aloud at the hold's board by Brom, and answered as not wrong); Pike has not said it in the crew's hearing. The wall's order after it is not known to the crew. The two HOLD slips are unresolved on the page. The broker's clerk's copy went west on the boat of the 102nd.
698:- **Open accounts:** Pike has eight completed lines but the crew has not heard the eighth read at the board; the Fallow hundred remains in Pike’s box, the ferryman’s rope and two HOLD slips remain unresolved, Hesk’s parcel has not arrived, three letters are unanswered, and seven hounds remain untracked (`manuscript/chapter-40.md:221–263,383`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:173–179`).
710:- **The rim:** six, faceless — no seals, names or office; four crossbows and two without, the second Anchor hand one of the six (not the Ostrand-road woman: the same craft, a different weight in the laying). They waited forty paces outside the edge. Their bows are left disabled; their bolts are too stout for Oryn's bow. The crew's five bolts stayed five. The Stair slip's three hundred was taken and never paid. The second paper was lodged sealed with the first.
712:- **The Stair's chamber:** walls cut floor to roof in five courses, each as tall as a forearm; a line that is not on the Ring stands BETWEEN the courses, on its own, recurring "every ten cords and a piece. Two hundred and twenty yards and a stride" — the Fallow's radius. (There is no continuous groove.) Karis paced the perimeter on the morning of the 115th and got two hundred. The open kit with its contents in taking-out order was found and is carried by Brom; **the case is untouched; the index and the stroke-and-bar are not decoded; nobody theorizes.** "Compact" is not said in this movement.
715:- **Length:** the reads split; the coordinator ruled that the Stair's action is not cut for the map's 40,000 line, and repetition is removed instead. **Names:** none minted.
721:- **The ground at the Stair (flag; one picture for chapters 48–52).** A ridge runs north and south; the Stair road comes south along its top and drops off its western face. Halfway down, the slope eases into a brow, and there the carters' live ruts turn north and go the whole way round a shallow bowl some five hundred yards across. The edge of the Quieting lies forty paces inside the ruts. The old straight road, two grown-over ruts, runs on down from the brow, crosses the edge between two iron bars eight feet apart (the old one on the north side, the newer on the south, with Karis's stone between), and goes to the floor: a hundred and ninety yards of grass, then thirty of floor to the hole. The floor is a bare round plate sixty of Cael's strides across, with no upright thing on it. The hole is two strides square, at the middle; the first step is on its eastern side and the stair goes down westward, forty steps, cut and not laid, every edge unworn; the air changes at the twentieth. The chamber opens westward from the stair's foot: ten strides each way; its roof is the underside of the floor, a foot above Brom's head; dust "as thin as the bloom on a plum". The rim (the ruts) is two hundred and sixty yards from the hole and sixteen hundred paces and a little more round.
724:- **Hound sign at the Stair (flag):** old runs in the grass on the north side, unused since the frosts began; no den that Seln can find from outside. He says that he does not know why and will not guess. No stillhound appears in this movement.
742:- **Lira's bowls (flag):** "We lay six bowls… Brom puts six out every night and takes one back." / "I'll eat out of it. I'll not sit in a place." At the Stair she eats out of the last of the six, standing.
756:**Calendar.** Late afternoon of the **hundred and fifteenth day past the Line**, at the stone on the eastern edge of the Long Stair, in full sun. No season, month or English weekday name is on the page ("the cold term", "frost", "the thaw", "the snow", "a dry month"). Days on the page: 111, 112, 113, 114, 115; in Seln's window, the night of the 112th; in Teague's, the 113th, 114th and the dawn of the 115th. Weather: hard frost every night, ringing roads, ice along Lowmarch's bank; no snow. The next movement opens where this one stops.
760:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten confirmed fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-three**, by his own log (fourteen on the 113th; nine on the 114th; none on the 115th). **Shadow used twice in all** (the Ring; the Stair), unseen by anybody outside the circle who could carry it; it is shut again. Anchor: looked twice and unbound once. The tenth has now read one other person, with her leave, at one sitting; **he has still read himself once only**, and owes Oryn two more readings on different days and then all of it; she told him "not tonight" on the 113th. What he found in himself is unsaid and unnamed aloud. Body: the right forearm struck (bends on the 115th); a weal across the left palm, taped, unmended; hands warm again. He has said *Noted* twice to Teague. He holds the kit (Brom carries it). Hesk's satchel: the log, the old volume, the notebook, the letters, both of the broker's papers, Pike's lines on bark. Three letters still unanswered.
766:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. One point set, on her lamp, outside the stone on the night of the 114th. Holds: **forty sheets** (eleven of the Ring, twenty-nine of the Stair); **forty-one kinds, the same at both**, entered and underlined; **the line between the courses** (not on the Ring; on every wall of the chamber; recurring at ten cords and a piece, two hundred and twenty yards and a stride, the perimeter's radius), shown on the eastern wall's sheets; the Stair's perimeter table (the Fallow's round, to the stride); the surveyor's *Reach, edge to centre* figure, written down from Seln's reading; the leaf of the trials, inside her coat; the calfskin, with *guess* struck out beside the Stair's ring and still standing beside the far south-eastern one; the Ternhall brass, out of its cloth and held over the hide. Her hypothesis is unentered and unsaid. She has said that the floor was carved by somebody who knew the circle's size, and nobody took it up.
776:**Objects and money.** Board purse: **a hundred and thirty-two earned; a hundred and two in hand**, unchanged. The three hundred lies in Pike's box against the broker's mark, and will not be marked. The Fallow hundred lies there too. **The kit:** case, pacing cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, mallet; the ledger apart, with Seln. Four crossbows by the stone, strings cut and locks empty, one broken; the rim's recovered shafts in a row beside them (thirteen loosed, by the log of the 114th), useless to the healer's bow. Karis's lamp filled; Teague's lantern returned. Owed still: the ferryman's rope; three replies; Hesk's parcel has not come.
778:**Pike's book and the wall.** **Eight lines**; the eighth said twice in the crew's hearing on the 112th. **Eleventh; Teague ten up.** The Stair paper carries five strokes and **HOLD, on Pike's own mark**, said to the room and to be said to the meadow. Pike has said that his book has no column for what the eighth line means.
780:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six. That the fragments answered at the Stair: the six. Which fragments, by Path: the six; where the ninth came from: the five. Teague and his three: a declaration outside the edge, of more than one kind. Pike and the room: that six went in at the Fallow and six came out. The registry's side: as above. Nobody has said why anything answers him inside. Nobody has put a verb between a made floor, an age with no ruler, the same round twice, a circle whose size the carvers knew, and a boy.
790:- **Research custody:** Seln has the field ledger inside his coat; Karis has eleven Ring sheets plus twenty-nine Stair sheets, forty-one kinds, the trials leaf, the perimeter table, the calfskin with the Stair's *guess* struck and the south-eastern *guess* still standing, and the Ternhall straightedge (52:165–209,247–305,355–369).
792:- **Open accounts:** the three hundred is taken and never to be paid; Pike's Fallow hundred, the ferryman's rope, Hesk's parcel and three replies remain outstanding; Teague forfeited the four-mark weir job and still has a day's fence posts (51:263–325; 52:355–359).
796:**Movement 7 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1: the ten audit fixes kept and their joins smoothed; ch52's three tracked passages restaged (scene 2 re-entered on the bolt at the thirty-first step; the kit read from the cord inward); the design confirmed once; twice-told states removed from ch49–51 with the night log whole; the closing log's "five years ago" made to stand (the ledger's eight stations are the old irons, five cold terms by Seln; the stroke-and-bar is "two visits", the later undated; nothing decoded); a register pass. Sol recheck r1: **CLOSE WITH LINE FIXES** — seven applied by the coordinator: **what Cael asked at Millrace's bench is corrected to the source's actual distinction** (leave to touch a boy's wrist while Oryn held it — refused; on the road he asks to read HER — granted, "a different question"), with the bench's count and the day's log to match; and ch52 says Karis reads the radius figure from scaled divisions within the lone line, not as a spacing along the wall. All three tracked passages RESOLVED, no new tracking. Gates 0; overlap as below. 42413 words (the length ruled by the coordinator: the Stair's action not cut for the map's 40,000 line). Author-sourced sections above refreshed from the post-repair report; where they still describe the bench request as a request to read Oryn, the Tide ruling above governs.
804:- **Deployment since the Line: TWENTY-SEVEN at book end.** Twenty-three after the Stair; then at the Hall on the 119th the read under water (24), Compression taken standing (25), an Anchor point laid by hand in the breach and held (26); and on the 120th Tide on Oryn, asked and granted, one sitting (27). The Anchor point set as a trial on the 118th is NOT counted ("I'll not call a trial a use"), which agrees with the protected inventory: Anchor used twice this year, once each way (the Stair's unbinding; the Hall's holding). Self-readings are not counted. **Shadow was not used**; it has been used exactly twice in the book and remains sealed; Oryn does not know Seln is its source.
808:- **The sites and the line:** the Ring (the Fallow), the Stair, the Hall, and the fourth, taken as lying on one line "as near as this skin can show"; no compass bearing is claimed for the whole. Only two maker sentences exist outside protected text: "Someone made this pattern." (said once; nobody answers) and "a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one." Karis's hypothesis is still unstated; her window ends on the pencil stopping.
809:- **Sheets: fifty-two** (eleven Ring, twenty-nine Stair, twelve Hall). The survey kit is on the table at Lowmarch: the case in tarred canvas, a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, never driven, and a mallet; the dead man's ledger, shut, is Karis's (Seln gave it to her). The case of Seln's is shut and untouched; nothing is decoded.
819:- **The brass on the hide (flag).** The Ternhall strip is slid down along itself with a pencil dot (a ruler "as long as its owner is patient"). Laid through the Fallow's ring and the Stair's, it runs into the carter's water-strokes and passes his third blank **a finger outside** Karis's pencilled ring. She moves nothing on the 115th. On the 120th she rubs the ring out and draws it where the cord put it, with *moved; paced.* On the 123rd she adds a fourth ring "at the very edge of the skin", with no word beside it; by the 131st a fine pencil line is ruled through the three centres and the fourth ring and off the hide. **What a ruler on a map of days is worth:** "a direction that is good to the breadth of this pencil at the length of my arm… a strip of country a mile wide." On the ridge she carries the line by ranging: Seln a furlong ahead, the last resting-place behind, moved until the three lie in a row. No compass direction is claimed for the line as a whole (ruled KEEP).
821:- **The road to the Hall (flag):** the Stair road beyond the low ground, south and east under the ridge, for a day; then a grown-over track with a tree in one rut, for a day; then no track for some hours; then the track's last furlong straight down to the reeds. A mild spell on the 116th–118th; hard frost again from the night of the 120th.
835:- **Teague, days 115–121 (flag):** **he did not set the weir's third stake; the ford-keeper did not mark the shingle; the four marks were lost, as Movement 7 closed it.** He sent word to Pike by the mill's carter ("five strokes to look at, and all of them walking; and the three hundred was to stay where it lay"); followed the trail from the Stair for three days "for what's behind them, if there's anything", walking back a mile along it at each dusk; found nobody on it. He hears Karis's sentence and Cael's ruling on the 121st, watches the brass run off the hide, and asks nothing. His five reach Lowmarch before the five do.
839:- **Pike's first snow (flag; ruled KEEP):** "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw." He has done forty that day. The ninth line is given in narration (a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting; taken; six gone in; instruments found, brought up and kept by the crew that found them; six come out). The clerk "has been up my bank four times to ask what he's to put a mark to", and is given nothing. Pike turns the ledger toward Cael for the first time in this edition.
851:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten confirmed fragments.** **Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven** (the ford 1; the Ring 12; Oryn on the ridge 1; the Stair 9; the Hall 3; Oryn at the marsh 1). The trial point at the Hall's kerb on the 118th is logged and not counted. Shadow used twice in all and shut. Anchor: looked three times; unbound once (the Stair); a point set twice, of which one is the uncounted trial and one held the breach. Tide: Oryn twice, each time by her leave; himself three times (102, 119, 120); nobody else; never inside a line. **The still place:** his old log label, now a location under the breastbone, read three times; nothing moves in it; all ten go round it and none through; they do not pile against it; not the Quiet; not a fragment; told to Oryn at once and in order on the 120th, and to the other four in three sentences on the 121st; he has been told to write it down and stop touching it, and has not touched it since. Body: ribs quiet since the morning of the 129th; right forearm and left palm no longer remarked; calf a closed seam. Hesk's satchel: the log (the entries of the 118th, 119th, 120th, the fork, the crest, and the inventory of the 129th), the old volume, the notebook, Hesk's letter and note, Ephram's and Vell's letters, both of the broker's papers, Pike's lines on bark, and Oryn's letter on bark. **He has Hesk's brass straightedge, and it reads true in his hand.** He has said "Noted" to Teague three times. He has not answered Ephram.
863:**Teague.** Unread and unasked. **Four marks poorer for the weir slip, which he forfeited.** He knows: six went into the Fallow and came out; one walked out of the Stair declaring more than one thing; six came out of the drowned hall wet; a strip of brass runs through three places on a hide and off its edge. He has asked "What are you." once and been answered "I don't have one." He knows nothing of the mechanism or of anything working inside a line. His term: "After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes." He has given the five the forty-mark fence and has the cold road. His crew is unnamed.
865:**Pike's book and the wall.** **Nine lines** under the five names; the ninth is the Stair, with *three hundred; taken; not paid*. The season line said and written. **No number against it.** **Eleventh on the cloth, unchanged.** He has turned the ledger toward Cael once. The Fallow hundred and the Stair's three hundred lie in his box unmarked.
867:**Objects and money.** **Ninety-six marks in hand** (a hundred and two, less six to the inn). The reserve sealed in the third box, unopened. Three boxes against the warm wall of the back room. **The kit on the table**: the case in tarred canvas, the cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, the mallet; the ledger beside it. Two brass straightedges. The brown mule (the text does not say where she is stabled). Oryn's crossbow, hook and one bolt; four bolts are in the wold-wyrm. Owed: sixty yards of four-strand rope to the ferryman, "not this side of the thaw"; a reply to Ephram. The fence at forty is taken and not yet begun.
869:**The Quieting, as known.** Four sites on one edge of brass. The Fallow Ring (stake 31), the Long Stair (32, on two bars, one with the stroke and the bar; the same mark on the kit's unused stake) and the Drowned Hall (33, old, no second) entered. The fourth seen and paced from outside on the 123rd: twice the radius by the bend of its cordon; stakes numbered 34 with the stroke and the bar, a pace apart; old ruts kept a hundred and ninety paces off; a standing grey shape that nobody could make out. **Not entered, not named, not dated, and not tried: no Arbiter has been taken to that line and what it does is unknown.** At the other three the live Arbiters stop at the line and Cael's fragments answer inside; whether a line twice the size does anything more is Lira's question and is unanswered.
871:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six. The still place: the six. That fragments answer inside a line: the six. The Compact's side: a completion report on the Fallow; what the rim saw at the Stair; and now a clerk's packet saying that a crew kept a kit. Nobody outside the six knows that anything works inside. Nobody has said why. Nobody has put a verb between a made circle, an age with no ruler, four on a line, and a boy.
889:- **Karis/research:** Ember, Iron Rank Three; voice whole; 52 sheets split 11/29/12; forty-one kinds at the Ring/Stair and thirty-three seen on the sampled Hall wall; four copies of the three-site leaf; carter's map with four unlabeled/aligned circles; Ternhall straightedge; kit field ledger; hypothesis unspoken and unentered (`manuscript/chapter-57.md:3-87`; `manuscript/chapter-60.md:17-27,239-255`).
892:- **Board/work:** five names remain eleventh; nine completed lines; no number on the first-snow season line; Stair price says “three hundred; taken; not paid”; Teague's exact term is “After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes.”; the five take the forty-mark fence, whose day's posts and far side remain to do (`manuscript/chapter-59.md:167-247`). Teague's four-mark weir slip remains forfeited after fix 8.
893:- **Money/accounts:** ninety-six living-purse marks (102 less six); sealed cart-and-gelding reserve still unopened in the third box; Fallow hundred and Stair three hundred remain in Pike's box and are not crew-payable debts; ferryman is owed sixty yards of four-strand rope, not wanted before thaw; fence not begun (`manuscript/chapter-52.md:357`; `manuscript/chapter-59.md:145-157,175-179,241-247`; `manuscript/chapter-60.md:7-11,31`).
896:- **Knowledge limits/open pressure:** Teague/Pike/holds/Compact do not know the mechanism or that fragments work inside; Oryn knows sources by Path but not Seln as Shadow source; no theory connects the sites, still place, or Cael; the cordon has no watch-post or date; the central shape has no name; the line runs off the map; the unanswered questions are twice-size effect, who/why, the unopened case, the archive index/mark, Ephram's reply, the rope, and Oryn's withheld answer (`manuscript/chapter-56.md:155-197`; `manuscript/chapter-58.md:117-229`; `manuscript/chapter-60.md:85-89,239-267`).

