editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:169:"I don't know. I tried it a second time, on a doorway, with Lira on the far side, and I couldn't get hold. I could see it and I couldn't reach it; seeing isn't reach. I'd have to find where this one's made fast, and get my hand on that place, from the inside. I can't see any of it from farther than ten feet." He made himself finish. "And it's better than two hundred yards from here, with six of them looking down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:293:It went as a breath goes that a person has been holding, and the point was simply not under his palm. The three courses to the south dropped across the old road, and he saw the nearest ten feet of them go, like wet rope falling; and from the north, away round the dark edge of the low ground, he felt the rest come in and go slack and run out through the place where a knot had been, a great deal of it, for as long as it takes to draw a bucket. He had not known how much ground it held until it stopped holding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:"The first was a question, and I said so at a stake. It sent a crew to fetch what was not there, in order to learn what became of the crew. A hundred marks is a fair price for a question. It is high, since nobody had ever taken such a paper; but a carter could believe in it." His finger moved to the bracket. "Nobody who has kept a book believes in this. It is two days out and two home, for a case that one person can carry on a strap. There is not a hold on that sheet that would pay thirty for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:29:"The first described a rise and a bend, which any carter could have told a clerk across a table. This one is written by somebody who has stood on that ridge, or who had beside him the notes of a person that did. *The ground opens.* He knows what is at the foot of the hill, and he has chosen it." He laid the second sheet upon the first. "I have sat at the desks where such things are drawn up, though never for a matter of this weight. Before an office moves to take a thing out of somebody's keeping, it makes a list. It writes down what is to be taken, and who holds it, and what each of the holders is able to do if he should object. Then it looks for a place where he cannot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:59:"He's just told you what it is. It's got a ruled edge and a word for itself. I don't need it read to me a third time." Her right hand had closed on the knot. "There's a walk on that oak at fifty that nobody's scratched, a string of salt to the middle holds before the pass shuts. There's a fence at forty, the day the hold comes off it. That's ninety marks of work that was posted by people who want a fence. We'd be down from both before the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:217:*HOLD.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:307:But a boy had come up a hill with a word on his file where a tier should have been, and had looked at every instrument that was turned on him as though somebody had built it. That had been the end of the weather. If a thing was built, then there was a builder, with a walk, and a way of holding a pen, and a tiredness at the end of the day. Seln could no longer do without seeing one: he wished to stand on the same ground as a single one of them, in daylight, and learn what a person looked like who did this for his living.
