editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:109:It was the eighth line under their heading. Cael had read it on a scrap of bark in a grain hold, across the bones of a goat, and he knew every word; but he had never heard it said. Pike gave the recovery, and the Fallow road, and the broker's posting. He said that it was taken and witnessed. He said what had not been found there, and that the crew had come back. He gave the count of those who went in and of those who came out, which was the same count, and at the end of it he said that no price was paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:153:"You've read my lines. Every one says what came back, and what was paid on it. That one says the things weren't there and the box stayed shut. A hold comes down to my plank with a job to give, and asks me who fetches what he's sent for. I've a crew that was sent for a thing and came home without it." He held up his one hand. "It's nothing against you. The cloth can't say whose fault a thing was. It can only say what was on the cart."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:165:He had been a number in other people's books for as long as he could remember. A magister had given him one every week, to the decimal. Vell had kept one for him in a yard. Every one of those figures had been somebody's best account of how much of a thing he was; and here was a book-keeper with one arm, who had never owned an instrument, saying aloud in a room that his own book had a true line in it and was too small to hold what the line meant. It was not a thing that any registry seat would have said. Cael did not know what to do with it, and he set it by, to be looked at later.
